3500:
** νέκρ-ωσις**, εως, ἡ,
**mortification,** {{NT+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων ὀξέων παθῶν” 2.10, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(1).156; μήτρας NT.Rom.4.19: _metaphorically_, νεκροὺς ὁρῶν νέκρωσιν ἕξεις πραγμάτων 2nd c.AD: Astrampsychus Onirocriticus “Onirocritica” p.6R.}}
**II** **death,** τὴν ν. τοῦ Ἰησοῦ ἐν τῷ σώματι περιφέροντες {{NT|NT.2Cor.4.10.}}

3501:
** νέος**, νέα,
_Ionic dialect_ νέη, νέον; _Ionic dialect_ **νεῖος** (which see): [_feminine_ νέας as _monosyllable_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 327 (Lyric poetry)}}; _contraction_ _feminine_ νῆ {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 42 (= 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 9), 5th c.BC: Eugaeon Historicus (?) 1]:}}
**1** **young, youthful** (of children, youths, and of men at least as old as {{Refs 8th c.BC+|30, see 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.35), ν. πάϊς 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.665; κοῦροι ν. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.95; ν. ἀνήρ 23.589: alone, νέοι}}**youths,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|1.463, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 281, etc.}}: later mostly with _Article_, οἱ νέοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1059, etc.: proverbial, ὁ ν. ἔσται ν.}} 'boys will be boys', {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Epistulae” 910.3}}; οἱ ν., corporately organized, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“SIG” 831.8 (Pergam., 2nd c.AD), etc.; opposed to ἔφηβοι, παῖδες, [prev. work]589.38 (Magn.Mae., 2nd c.BC): opposed to γέρων, ἠμὲν νέοι ἠδὲ γέροντες 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.789, etc.; ἢ ν. ἠὲ παλαιός 14.108, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.395, etc.; opposed to γεραίτερος, 3.24; opposed to προγενέστερος, 2.29}}; opposed to γεραιός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 1.7}}; εὐθὺς ἐκ νέου ἐθίζειν from **youth upwards,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 510d, etc.; ἐκ νέων παίδων [prev. author] “Lg.” 887d; ἐκ νέων ἐθίζεσθαι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1103b24}}; ἐκ νέας (i.e. ψυχῆς) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 409a}}; τὸ ν., ={νεότης}, **youth** (in the abstract), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1229 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 545: also in concrete sense, τὸ ν. ἅπαν}} all **young creatures,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 653d; οὐ δύναται τὸ ν. ἡσυχάζειν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1340b29; σκιρτητικὸν τὸ ν. 1st c.AD: Cornutus Philosophus “de Natura Deorum” 20}}; also, of **minors,** νέου ὄντος ἔτι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.107}}; compare νεώτερος.
**1.b** rarely of animals and plants, ὄρπηκες, ἔρνος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.38, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.163; οἱ ν. τῶν νεβρῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 9.8.}}
**2** **suited to a youth, youthful,** ἄεθλοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.43; ν. θράσος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 744 (troch.)}}; ν. φροντίς **youthful** spirits, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 48; νέαις ταῖς διανοίαις χρωμένους 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 24.16}}; of persons, ἄφρων νέος τε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 489, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 378a; ν. τε καὶ ὀξύς [prev. author] “Grg.” 463e (but διαφέρει οὐδὲν ν. τὴν ἡλικίαν ἢ τὸ ἦθος νεαρός 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1095a6).}}
**II** **new, fresh,** ν. θάλαμος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.36; ν. ἄλγος 6.462; νέῳ.. κόλλοπι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.407 (this sense elsewhere in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus only in _adverb_ νέον, see below)}}; λίνον {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 15 (uncertain); πόνοι.. νέοι παλαιοῖσι συμμιγεῖς κακοῖς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 740 (Lyric poetry), etc.; οἶνος ν. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 916; ἐν τοῖς μουσικοῖς τὰ ν. [μέλη] εὐδοκιμεῖ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.38}}; ἡ ν. (i.e. σελήνη) the **new** moon, especially in phrase ἕνη καὶ νέα, see at {ἕνος} {{Refs|2}}; but μηνὸς τῇ ν. (i.e. ἡμέρᾳ) **on the first day** of the month, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 849b; ν. ἦμαρ 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.1479}}: in this sense rarely of persons, ὁ ν. ταγὸς μακάρων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 96 (_anapaest meter_), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 960; οἱ ν. θεοί 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 721}}; compare νεώτερος.
**II.2** of events, etc., **new,** with collateral notion of **unexpected, strange, untoward, evil,** τί ν.; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ag.” 85 (_anapaest meter_); προσδοκῶ τι γὰρ ν. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 99}}; μῶν τι βουλεύῃ ν.; [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1229, compare 554, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 794, “Ba.” 362. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.50. etc.: ἀπροσδοκήτους καὶ ν. λόγους 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 712; καινὰ ν. τ᾽ ἄχη [prev. author] “Pers.” 665 (Lyric poetry): this sense is more common in _comparative_, see at {νεώτερος}.}}]
**III** _neuter_ νέον as _adverb_ of Time, **lately, just now,** opposed to both to distant past and present, παῖδα ν. γεγαῶτα [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.400, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.394; ν. κρατεῖν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 35, 955, etc.: also used Adverbially with the _Article_, καὶ τὸ πάλαι (variant{παλαιόν}) καὶ τὸ ν. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.26: in Prose νεωστί (which see): rarely _comparative_}}] _adverb_ νεωτέρως,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 907c}}: _superlative_ νεώτατα **most recently,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.7}}; also ἐκ νέας, _Ionic dialect_ αὖτις ἐκ νέης, **anew, afresh,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.60, 5.116.}}
**IV**. the degrees of _comparative_ are νεώτερος, νεώτατος, see at {νεώτερος}: νεαίτερος is corrupt for νεαίρετος in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 330. (νέϝος (in νεϝόστατος, which see)}}, cf. Sanskrit _návas,_ Latin _novus,_ etc.)

3502:
** νεοσσός**,
_Attic dialect_ **νεοττ-**, ὁ, (νέος) **young bird, nestling, chick,** {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.311, 9.323, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 425, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 835, NT.Luke.2.24, etc.; ἀπτῆνες ν. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.48a.}}
**2** **any young animal,** as a young crocodile, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.68}}; of young children, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 256, 501, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 403 (Lyric poetry), +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 776a: _feminine_, ἦν νεοττὸς καὶ νέα (i.e. Lais) 4th c.BC: Epicrates Comicus 3.15}}: in _plural_, **young bees,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 7.34, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 624a22}}; Ἄρεως ν., of the cock, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 835}} (also ironically, of a person, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 104): collective, ἵππου ν.}} the horse's **brood,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 825.}}
**3** **yolk** of an egg, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 565a3, Oracle texts cited in 3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.344}}; compare νεοττίον.—The _disyllable_ form **νοσσός** is cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Anecdota Graeca - Bekker” 109 from 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 113 and occurs in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oxy.” 2081}} (**b) fragment **3: this and _cognate_ forms (commonly found in later Gr.) are condemned as ἀδόκιμα by {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 182.}}

3503:
** νεότης**,
_Doric dialect_ **-τας**, ητος, ἡ, (νέος) **youth,** ἐκ νεότητος.. ἐς γῆρας {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.86, cf. NT.Mark.10.20, etc.; ἀτέμβονται νεότητος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.445}}; ἐρατὴν γὰρ ἀπωλέσαμεν νεότητα, i.e. we died young, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 89, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 637 (Lyric poetry), “fragment” 149}}; ἐν νεότατι, ἐπὶ νεότητος, in one's **youth,** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 12.3, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1199, compare “Ach.” 214: in _plural_, αἱ ν. ἄφρονες “Anthologia Graeca” 9.359 (3rd c.BC: Posidippus Comicus or 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus or Crates)}}; αἱ ν. ῥωμαλέαι{{Refs 4th c.AD+|[prev. work] 360 (4th c.AD: Metrodorus Epigrammaticus).}}
**2** **youthful spirit, impetuosity,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.13}}: in bad sense, **youthful folly, insolence,** ἀκολασίᾳ καὶ ν. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 26e; ν. καὶ ἄνοια 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.7.}}
**II** collective, ={νεολαία}, **body of youth,** especially of military or athletic age, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 8(7).75, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.3, 9.12, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.8, 20, etc.}}
**III** in Crete, **νεότας**, ἁ, _accusative_ νεότα, _genitive_ νεότας, **board of officials representing the** νέοι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|compare (νέος 1.1), “GDI” 5012.6, “SIG” 525.9 (Gortyn, 3rd c.BC).}}

3504:
** νεό-φῠτος**, ον,
**newly planted,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 828; ἀμπελών “PCair.Zen.” 236.4 (3rd c.BC)}}; κτῆμα {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 909.16 (3rd c.AD): _neuter_ _plural_ νεόφυτα, τά}} **young plants,** {{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.Psa.143(144).12, compare “PRyl.” 138.9 (1st c.AD}}; νεώ- Papyrus), etc.
**II** _metaphorically_, **new convert, neophyte,** {{NT|NT.1Tim.3.6.}}

3506:
** νεύω**,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.133, etc.}}: _future_ νεύσω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.283, etc.:}}_aorist_ ἔνευσα, _Epic dialect_ νεῦσα (see. below): _perfect_ νένευκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1581, etc. (_future_ _middle_ νεύσομαι only in compounds)}}:—**incline in any direction**:
**1** **nod, beckon,** as a sign, νεύσω μέν τοι ἐγὼ κεφαλῇ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.283; νεῦσ᾽ Αἴας Φοίνικι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.223, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.330; νεῦσαν ἐς ἀλλήλους “Hymni Homerici” 7.9; ὅρκος βέβαιός ἐστιν ἂν νεύσω μόνον 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 91, compare 178.3}}; **beckon** with the hand, δεξιᾷ δέ μοι ἔνευσε {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Ezekiel Poeta Judaeus “Exagoge - Mnemosyne” 73}}: with _infinitive_, **beckon** to one to do a thing, in token of command, νεανίαις ἔνευσε παρθένον λαβεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 545.}}
**2** **nod** or **bow in token of assent,** ἐπὶ γλεφάροις ν. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 8(7).50}}; νεῦσον, Κρονίων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “P.” 1.71; νεῦσον, τέκνον, πείσθητι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 484, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 883}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, **grant, assure, promise that**.., νεῦσε δέ οἱ λαὸν σόον ἔμμεναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.246}}: with _infinitive_ _future_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.67}}: with _infinitive_ _aorist_, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 6.244 (1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Crinagoras Epigrammaticus)}}: with _accusative_ _of things_, **grant, promise,** νεῦσε δέ οἱ κούρην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Cererem” 445, compare 463; νεύσατε τὰν ἀδόκητον χάριν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 248 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 978 (Lyric poetry).}}
**3** generally, **nod, bend forward,** of warriors, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.133; νεῦον τὸ αἰδοῖον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.48; λόφος καθύπερθεν ἔνευεν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.337, compare 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 15.3, etc.; στάχυες νεύοιεν ἔραζε 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 473, etc.; ν. κάτω}}**stoop,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 839; ν. ἐς τὴν γῆν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1110, compare 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22.90: with _accusative_, οὕτω νῦν μνηστῆρες.. νεύοιεν κεφαλὰς δεδμημένοι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.237; ἐς πέδον κάρα νεῦσαι φόβῳ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 270, compare 441.}}
**4** **incline, slope,** ν. ἀπό τινος εἴς τι **incline** to wards, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.100}}; εἰς τὸ αὐτὸ ν. **tend** to the same point, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 945d}}; πρὸς τὸ λυπῆσαν, πρὸς τοῖς ῥήμασιν, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “Problemata - Physici et Medici Graeci Minores” 1.48, 78}}; of countries, etc., **slope,** ν. εἰς δύσεις, πρὸς τὸ πέλαγος, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.42.6, 1.73.5, etc.}}; of buildings, etc., **look, face,** εἰς νότον, etc., {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PLond.” 3.978 (4th c.AD), etc.}}; μηδαμοῦ ν. **to be in equilibrium,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.10.7; ταῖς πρῴραις ἔξω νεύοντα τὰ σκάφη [prev. author] 1.26.12}}: Geometry texts, of straight lines, **verge, tend** to a point (i. e. to pass through it when produced), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Anthologia Graeca” 0.76b9, 3rd-2nd c.BC: Apollonius Pergaeus Geometra 1.2, etc.}}: _metaphorically_, **to be inclined,** ἄλλως ν. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.109}}; ν. εἰς ὀργάν, εἰς ἔλεον, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“APl.” 4.136 (1st c.AD: Antiphilus Epigrammaticus); ἐπὶ χάριν 3rd-2nd c.BC: Phalaris Epistolographus “Epistulae - Epistolographi” 78; πρὸς γαστέρα 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 14.659a; πρὸς θῆλυ “Tragica Adespota” 355.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **decline, fall away,** ἐκ.. τῶν ποτε λαμπρῶν νεύει βίοτος, νεύει δὲ τύχα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 153}}: in Neo-Platonic philosophy, **decline, sink** in the scale of Being, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 2.9.4, etc.}}
**III** νεύει· ἐπανέρχεται ἢ μᾶλλον φεύγει, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**IV** _passive_, only _perfect_ _participle_ νενευμένος **inclined,** Teucerin {{Refs|“Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 7.202}}. (Cf. Sanskrit _návate_ 'turn round', Latin _nuo._)

3507:
** νεφέλη**, ἡ,
(νέφος) **cloud, mass of clouds** (distinguished from ὁμίχλη, **mist** or **fog,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 346b33), 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.20, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 745, etc.}}; ν. κυανέη, πορφυρέη, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.75, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.551; ἐριβρόμου νεφέλας στρατός 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 6.11; νεφέλαι πόκοις ἐρίων ὁμοῖαι 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Signis Tempestatum” 13.}}—Mostly _poetry_, but found in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.8.8, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus}} [same places], and in late Prose, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.777e, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Epistulae” 24.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, νεφέλη δέ μιν ἀμφεκάλυψε κυανέη, of death, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.417}}; τὸν δ᾽ ἄχεος νεφέλη ἐκάλυψε μέλαινα **a cloud** of sorrow,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|17.591, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 528 (_anapaest meter_); πολέμου ν. 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 89; φόνου ν. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 9.38, compare “I.” 7(6).27}}; Κενταύρου φονίᾳ νεφέλᾳ, i.e. with his blood, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 831 (Lyric poetry)}}; κελαινῶπις ν., of sleep, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.7.}}
**II** of **clouds** in urine, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προγνωστικόν” 12, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.252.}}
**II.2** ={νεφέλιον} 11.2, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 2.20.}}
**II.3** **cloud** on a mirror, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Insomniis” 459b30.}}
**III** **fine bird-net,** in _plural_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 194, 528 (_anapaest meter_), 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Aetia” 3.1.37, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 1.2, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 1.25d: in _sing._, “Anthologia Graeca” 6.11 (1st c.BC(?): Satyrus Epigrammaticus), 109 (Antip.), 185 (1st c.BC: Zosimus Epigrammaticus).}}
**IV** Alchem., **sublimate,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Pseudo-Democritus Alchemista Alch. p.50 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}

3509:
** νέφος**, εος, τό,
**cloud, mass of clouds,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.275, +others; σμικροῦ νέφους ἐκπνεύσας μέγας χειμών 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1148; ν. ὄμβριον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 288 (Lyric poetry); ν. καὶ ὁμίχλη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 49c; τὸν κίνδυνον παρελθεῖν ὥσπερ ν. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.188.}}
**2** _metaphorically_ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|compare (νεφέλη 1.2), θανάτου δὲ μέλαν ν. ἀμφεκάλυψεν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.350, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.180, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 12.64; λάθας ν. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.45}}; σκότου ν., of blindness, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1314 (Lyric poetry)}}; ν. οἰμωγῆς, στεναγμῶν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 107 (_anapaest meter_), “HF” 1140}}; ὀφρύων ν. **a cloud upon** the brow, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Hipp.” 172 (_anapaest meter_); ὑπὸ τοῦ μετώπου οἷον ν. ἐπανεστηκός 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physiognomonica” 809b22; διασκεδᾶτε τὸ προσὸν νῦν ν. ἐπὶ τοῦ προσώπου 4th c.BC: Anaxandrides Comicus 58.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, also, **a cloud of men,** etc., ν. πεζῶν, Τρώων, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.274, 16.66}}; ψαρῶν, κολοιῶν,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|17.755; ν. τοσοῦτον ἀνθρώπων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.109}}; πενεστάων ν.{{NT|Timo 39; μαρτύρων NT.Heb.12.1}}; πολέμοιο ν. **the cloud** of battle, **thick** of the fight, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.243, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1090: applied by 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.9 to a single hero: used by Prose writers for poetry νεφέλη (which see)}}. (Cf. Sanskrit _nábhas_ 'fog', 'cloud', Slavonic _nebo_ 'heaven', Latin _nebula._)

3511:
** νεώκορ-ος**, ὁ,
_Doric dialect_ **νᾱοκόρος** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“GDI” 2116.14, +others (Delph., 2nd c.BC), 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}: _contraction_ **νᾱκόρος** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PMagd.” 35.7 (3rd c.BC, probably _Doric dialect_), “GDI” 1912.9, +others (Delph., 2nd c.BC), 5087 (from Crete)}}: as _feminine_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“IG” 42(1).393, +others (Epid., 2nd c.AD)}}; **ναυκόρος**, ἡ, Buresch **Aus Lydien** p.58: _poetry_ **νηοκόρος** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.22 (1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus)}}:—**warden of a temple,** as a sacred officer, τοῖς ἱεροῖς ν. γίγνεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 759a}}; ἱερέας τε καὶ ν.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 953a; παρὰ Μεγαβύξῳ τῷ τῆς Ἀρτέμιδος ν. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.3.6, compare “Inscription Prien.” 231 (4th c.BC)}}; βωμοῖο ν. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 11.324 (1st c.AD: Automedon Epigrammaticus); ν. τοῦ μεγάλου Σαράπιδος “POxy.” 100.2 (2nd c.AD).}}
**2** **sacristan,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 4.41, 45, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 10.12.5}}; ἐνβόλιον ἔχων ν. in a list of silver articles, {{Refs|“IG” 7.3498.25 (from Oropus).}}
**II** title assumed by Asiatic cities in Imperial times, when they had built a temple in honour of their patron-god or the Emperor, as Ephesus, ν. Ἀρτέμιδος {{NT|NT.Act.19.35}}; also as _adjective_, τῷ ν. Ἐφεσίων δήμῳ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“OGI” 481.3 (2nd c.AD),compare “BMus.Inscription” 481*.4 (Ephesus, 2nd c.AD)}}; δὶς ν. τῶν Σεβαστῶν, of Ephesus, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“OGI” 496.7 (2nd c.AD)}}; of Smyrna, {{Refs|“IGRom.” 4.1419. (Prob. derived from κορέω}} **sweep,** the originally sense being probably **temple-sweeper,**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 115, 121, 795 (where the word does not occur), νεωκορέω 1.2, 11, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.236, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus; but 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus explanation it ὁ τὸν νεὼν κοσμῶν.., ἀλλ᾽ οὐχ ὁ σαίρων.)}}

3512:
** νεωτερ-ικός**, ή, όν,
**natural to a youth, youthful,** ἀγωγή {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.21.7; αὐθάδεια 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 16.11.8; ἐπιθυμίαι NT.2Tim.2.22; ἁμαρτήματα 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 118.3}}. _adverb_ **-κῶς**{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Dio” 4.}}
**II** **modern in style,** κάτοπτρον {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1449.56 (3rd c.AD).}}

3513:
** νή**,
Particle of strong affirmation, with _accusative_ of the divinity invoked, once in Trag., ν. τὼ Λαπέρσα, ν. τὸν Εὐρώταν τρίτον, ν. τοὺς ἐν Ἄργει καὶ κατὰ Σπάρτην θεούς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 957}}; frequently in Comedy texts and Prose, especially in the phrase νὴ Δία, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 319, “Th.” 240, etc.}}; with the _Article_, ν. τὸν Δία {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pl.” 202, 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 179.3, etc.}}; with the names of other gods, usually with _Article_, ν. τὴν Δήμητρα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 24; ν. τὴν Ἀθηνᾶν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 218; ν. τὸν Ἀπόλλω [prev. author] “Ec.” 160}}; ν. τὴν Ἄρτεμιν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 90; ν. τὸν Ποσειδῶ [prev. author] “Nu.” 83, 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 265}}; ν. τὼ θεώ (Demeter and Cora) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 51, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Γεωργός” 24 (a woman's oath, _accusative_ to 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 171}}; ν. τὼ σιώ (of the Dioscuri) used by a Spartan, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.234f); ν. τὴν Ἥραν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 230b; ν. τὴν Ἑστίαν 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 185; ν. τὴν Ἀφροδίτην 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 189, 4th c.BC: Nicostratus Comicus 35; ν. τὸν κύνα, see at {κύων} 1 near the end}}]; ν. τοὺς θεούς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 74, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 531a, +others; ν. θεούς 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιστολαί” 17}}; ν. τόν alone, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.271.}}
**II** ν. (τὸν) Δία is also used,
**II.1** in answering questions, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.6, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 312a, etc.: in a supposed answer from antagonists, followed by γάρ, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.16.}}
**II.2** in introducing objections or contentions supposed to come from antagonists, followed by γάρ, {{Refs|[prev. author] 19.285}}; followed by ἀλλά, {{Refs|[prev. author] 18.117, 19.272}}; preceded by ἀλλά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.3.10, “Mem.” 1.2.9, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.125, +others}}
**II.3** to add force by way of climax, ἄλλως τε μέντοι ν. Δία πάντως καὶ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 35d, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.7.21.}}
**II.4** in adjurations, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 661, “Ra.” 164.}}

3516:
** νήπῐος**, α
{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|(2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.7, +others)}}, _Ionic dialect_ η, ον, also ος, ον {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 638}}:— **infant, child,** frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, νήπιον, οὔ πω εἰδόθ᾽ ὁμοιίου πολέμοιο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.440; νήπια τέκνα 2.136, etc.; βρέφος ἔτ᾽ ὄντα ν. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1399, compare “Andr.” 755, etc.; νηπίους ἔτι [prev. author] “Heracl.” 956; τὸ ν. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 366d; ἁρμόττουσα τοῖς ν. [πλαταγή] 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1340b30}}; ἐκ νηπίου from **a child,** from **infancy,** [τὸ ἡδὺ] ἐκ ν. ἡμῖν συντέθραπται {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. author] “EN” 1105a2; ἐκ νηπίων 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.20.8; ἐκ ν. ἡλικίας “PFlor.” 36.5 (4th c.AD)}}; **infant** in law, **minor,** ἐφ᾽ ὅσον ὁ κληρονόμος ν. ἐστιν {{NT|NT.Gal.4.1}}; of children up to puberty, αἱ τῶν ν. ἐκλάμψιες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 6.1.4 (compare 3rd c.BC: Herophilus Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 17(1).826)}}; but of the foetus in its early stage, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 4.1 (compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 17(1).653).}}
**2** less frequently of animals, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.311, 11.113}}; νήπια alone, **the young** of an animal, {{Refs|17.134.}}
**3** of plants, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 8.1.7.}}
**II** _metaphorically_,
**II.1** of the understanding, **childish, silly,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.237; μέγα ν. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.46, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.44}}; simply, **without foresight, blind,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.445; ἀνὴρ ν. 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 79, compare 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 11.1, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.82, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 443, 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 76, etc.; ν. ὃς.. γονέων ἐπιλάθεται 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 145 (Lyric poetry)}}; οὔτε πρὶν νήπιον, νῦν τ᾽.. μέγαν no **child** before and now full-grown (i.e. in mind), {{Refs|[prev. author] “OT” 652 (Lyric poetry)}}; of words, νήπια βάζεις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 157; ἀντιτείνειν νήπι᾽ ἀντὶ νηπίων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 891; μηδὲν εἴπῃς ν. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 105.}}
**II.2** of bodily strength, **like that of a child,** βίη δέ τε ν. αὐτῶν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.561.}}

3517:
** Νηρ-εύς**, έως,
_Ionic dialect_ ῆος, ὁ, **Nereus,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Hymnus ad Apollinem 319, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 240, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 8.7, etc.}}
**2** **sea,** Λίβυς, Ἄραψ N., {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 25.51, 32.194.}}

3520:
** νῆσος**,
_Doric dialect_ **νᾶσος**, ἡ, **island,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.721, etc.}}; ἐν τᾷ μεγάλᾳ Δωρίδι νάσῳ Πέλοπος, i.e. the Peloponnese, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 696}}; μακάρων νῆσοι, see at {μάκα; αἱ ν}. **the islands** of the Archipelago, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1319, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.8.1}}; καὶ πῶς γυνὴ.. νῆσον ἀμφιέννυτα; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Anaxilas Comicus 35 (compare περίνησος)}}: irregular _genitive_ _plural_ νησάων {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Delum” 66.}}
**2** **land flooded** by the Nile, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PHib.” 1.90.7 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}; opposed to ἤπειρος, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PGiss.” 60 (2nd c.AD)}}; νῆσοι ποταμοφόρητοι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1445.13 (2nd c.AD)}}; so of **alluvial land** in {{Refs|“Tab.Heracl.” 1.38.}}

3522:
** νηστ-εύω**,
**fast,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1519, “Th.” 949, NT.Matt.6.16, etc.}}; νηστεύσαντες, opposed to ἐδηδοκότες, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 676a1.}}
**2** with _genitive_, **abstain from,** κακότητος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 144.}}

3523:
** νῆστις**,
_genitive_ ιος or ιδος, ὁ and ἡ (see. below); also _dative_ νήστει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 60: _plural_ νήστεις 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 138, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Ars Rhetorica” 9.16}}: (νη-, ἔδω):—**not eating, fasting,** of persons, ἀνώγοιμι πτολεμίζειν υἷας Ἀχαιῶν νήστιας, ἀκμήνους {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.207; νήστιες ἄχρι.. κνέφαος 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.370, cf. Diocl.fragment 43, NT.Matt.15.32, etc.; νήστισιν ἐπιθέντες οἱ πολέμιοι 1st c.AD: Onosander (Onasander) Tacticus 12.1: with _genitive_, νῆστις βορᾶς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 973: _metaphorically_, νῆστιν ἀνὰ.. ψάμμαν}} over the **hungry** sand, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 573 (Lyric poetry).}}
**2** with an abstract _substantive_, frequently in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus, νῆστιν νόσον}}**famine, **{{Refs|“Ag.” 1016 (Lyric poetry); ν. λιμός “Ch.” 250}}; νήστισιν αἰκίαις the pains **of hunger, Pr.**{{Refs|599 (Lyric poetry)}}; νήστιδες δύαι {{Refs|“Ag.” 1621}}; also νῆστις ὀσμή the bad breath **of one fasting,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista “Praeparatio Sophistica” p.91 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}
**3** _active_, **causing hunger, starving,** πνοαὶ νήστιδες {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 193 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II** as _substantive_, νῆστις, ἡ, _accusative_ νῆστιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 318.3, 506.4, 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 110.}}
**II.1** the **intestinum jejunum,** from its always being found empty, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ σαρκῶν” 19, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 506.4, 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 63.5 (_anapaest meter_), compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 675b33.}}
**II.2** ν. κεστρεύς, fish so called because its stomach was always found empty, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 156, etc.}}: hence in Comedy texts, of 'empty bellies', ἐγὼ δὲ κεστρεὺς νῆστις οἴκαδ᾽ ἀποτρέχω {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 256, etc., compare 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 7.307d.}}
**II.3** Νῆστις, ἡ, ={ὕδωρ}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 6.3, compare 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 322.}}

3525:
** νήφω**,
_Doric dialect_ **νάφω** (see. infr. II), used by early writers only in _present_, mostly in _participle_: later _imperfect_ ἔνηφον {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Choricius Rhetor in “Rev.Phil.” 1877.67:}}_aorist_ ἔνηψα {{NT+5th c.BC+|NT.1Pet.4.7, Oracle texts cited in 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Fragmenta” 103, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 11.3.3, 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in Platonis Parmenidem commentarii” p.741 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, (ἐξ-) 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων χρονίων παθῶν” 1.5, (ἀν-) 1st c.BC: Nicolaus Damascenus Historicus 4 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}:—**to be sober, drink no wine,** οὔτε τι γὰρ ν. οὔτε λίην μεθύω {{Refs 7th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 478; νήφειν 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 4, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 213e, +others:}}_participle_ νήφων as _adjective_, ={νηφάλιος}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.133, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1228; ὑμῖν ἀντέκυρσα.. νήφων ἀοίνοις 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 100; ὑπ᾽ ἐχθροῦ νήφοντος ὑβριζόμην 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.74}}; τὸ τοὺς μεθύοντας... πλείω ζημίαν ἀποτίνειν τῶν ν. Legal Pittaciap. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1274b20; μεθύοντα.. παρὰ νηφόντων λόγους παραβάλλειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 214c}}; ν. θεός, i.e. water, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Lg.” 773d: proverbial, τὸ ἐν τῇ καρδίᾳ τοῦ νήφοντος ἐπὶ τῆς γλώττης τοῦ μεθύοντος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.503f; [Ἀναξαγόρας] οἷον ν. ἑφάνη παρ᾽ εἰκῇ λέγοντας 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 984b17; νήφων μεθύοντα ὑπὸ τῆς Ἀφροδίτης θεᾶται 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 8.21; τὸ νῆφον ὑπὸ τοῦ πάθους βυθίζεται 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 1.13.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **to be self-controlled,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 918d}}; **to be sober and wary,** νᾶφε καὶ μέμνασ᾽ ἀπιστεῖν {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus [250]; γρηγορῶμεν καὶ νήφωμεν NT.1Thes.5.6}}; νήψατε εἰς προσευχάς {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.1Pet.[same place]; νήφων καὶ πεφροντικώς 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.800b; ν. καὶ φροντιστής 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 17(1).991; προμηθής τε καὶ ν. 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 2.15.1}}; καρδίῃ νήφοντος poetical cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 34.4; ν. λογισμός 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 3p.64U.}}
**II.2** ν. ἐκ κακοῦ **recover oneself** from.., {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Achilles Tatius Astronomus 1.13}}; ἐγερθέντων καὶ νηψάντων ἀπὸ τῆς πτώσεως Procl.[same place]

3528:
** νῑκάω**,
_Ionic dialect_ **νικέω** {{NT+4th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 249, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 1.51, also “GDI” 1413.16 (Aetol.), “SIG” 265.4 (Delph., 4th c.BC), variant in NT.Rev.2.7}}; _Aeolic dialect_ **νίκημι** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.40, “Anthologia Graeca” 7.743 (Antip.)}}; also in _imperfect_ νίκη conjecture in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 5.5, compare 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 6.46}}: _Epic dialect_ _imperfect_ _1st pers._ _plural_ νικάσκομεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.512:}}_future_ -ήσω, later **-ήσομαι**{{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Hierocles Facetiarum Scriptor “Facet.” 205}}; _Doric dialect_ _2nd pers._ _singular_ νικαξῇ variant in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 21.32:}}_perfect_ νενίκηκα, etc.: (νίκη):
**I** _absolutely_, **conquer, prevail** in battle, in the games, or in any contest, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.439, etc.}}; ὁ νικήσας the **conqueror,**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 138, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 5.9, etc.}}; ὁ νικηθείς the **conquered,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.656, 663}}; ἐνίκησα καὶ δεύτερος καὶ τέταρτος ἐγενόμην I **won the first prize** [at Olympia], etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.16, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 16.33}}: _present_ frequently in sense, **to be** (or **be proclaimed) conqueror,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.112, 13.30, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.2.27, “An.” 2.1.1}}; νικᾶν πᾶσι τοῖς κριταῖς or ἑνὶ κριτῇ in their opinion, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 445, 447}}; πολὺ ν. **win** a decisive **victory,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.34, etc.; τὰ πάντα ν. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “An.” [same place]: frequently with _dative_ modi, πυγμῇ}} **in** boxing, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.669; ναυμαχίῃ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.10.β᾽}}; ἵππῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 6.122; μάχῃ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1143, etc.; ἵππῳ ἢ συνωρίδι ἢ ζεύγει 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 36d; λαμπάδι 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.42, etc.: with _accusative_ _cognate_ in same sense, πάντα ἐνίκα}} he **won** all the bouts, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.389, 5.807}}; τὰ κοῦφα, τὰ μείζοναν., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 1029, 1031; τῶν παλαισμάτων ἓν ν. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 256b; ἅρμα ν. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 4(3).25; παγκράτιον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.49}}; ναυμαχίαν, μάχας, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 7.66, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.257, etc.: frequently ν. Ὀλύμπια}} **to be conqueror** in the Olympian games, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.126; τὠλύμπια 4th c.BC: Timocles Comicus 8.17; τὰ Παναθήναια 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Ion” 530b; ν. Ὀλυμπιάδα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.33 (also ν. Ὀλυμπίασιν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 36d; ἐν Πυθίοισι 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 2.9): with _dative_ et _accusative_, τὰ Πύθια τῷ τεθρίππῳ ν. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.33}}; πολλοὺς ἀγῶνας οὐ παγκρατίῳ μόνον, κτλ., {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.811d}}; Ὀλυμπίασι παῖδας στάδιον ν. **conquer** in the boys' race in the stadium at Olympia, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 58.66: with double _accusative_, Πύθια ν. ἄνδρας}} Diog.Cyn. cited in {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 6.33}}: also in _Attic dialect_ Inscrr. with _genitive_, Λεωντὶς ἀνδρῶν ἐνίκα {{Refs|“IG” 2.1291, +others: generally with _accusative_ _cognate_, νίκην ν.}} **win** a victory, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1060, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 465d, etc. (compare below 11)}}; also ν. τρίποδα **win** it, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 147.}}
**I.2** **prevail, be superior,** μύθοισιν, ἔγχεϊ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.252; δόλοισι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.121}}; κάλλει ἐνίκα (i.e. κρητήρ) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.742; πᾶσαν ἀρετὴν νενικηκώς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 964c}}: with _participle_, εὐεργετῶν ν. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 9.7.}}
**I.3** of opinions, etc., βουλὴ κακὴ νίκησεν the evil counsel **prevailed,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.46; τὰ χερείονα νικᾷ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.576, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.404; ἐνίκα ἡ γνώμη 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.36, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.12, etc.; ἡ νικῶσα βουλή 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 912}}; ἐκ τῆς νικώσης [γνώμης] according to **the prevailing opinion, vote of the majority,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.1.18, 6.2.12; ταῦτ᾽ ἐνίκα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 274; νικᾷ πάσαισι ταῖς ψήφοις ὁ νόμος}}**is carried,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 801a; σὺν ψάφῳ τᾷ νικεούσᾳ “SIG” 265.4 (Delph., 4th c.BC)}}: frequently of orators, νικᾷ.. ὁ κακὸς ἐν πλήθει λέγων [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 944; ν. γνώμῃσι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.82 (so γνώμῃ, variant{γνώμην}, [prev. author] 1.61, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 594): frequently _impersonal_, ἐνίκα (i.e. ἡ γνώμη)}}] **it was resolved,** with _infinitive_, ἐνίκα μὴ ἐκλιπεῖν τὴν πόλιν **it was carried** not.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.101; τέλος γε μέντοι δεῦρ᾽ ἐνίκησεν μολεῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 233, etc.}}; ἐνίκησε.. λοιμὸν εἰρῆσθαι **it was the prevailing opinion that**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.54}}; ἐν δημοκρατίᾳ νικᾷ ζῆν **it is preferable**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 303b.}}
**I.4** with _infinitive_, **succeed** in.., ἐνίκησε σκορπίσαι {{Refs|“Psalms of Solomon [1st c.BC]” 4.13.}}
**I.5** as law-term, ν. τὴν δίκην **win** one's cause, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 955, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 581; simply νικᾶν “GDI” 5011.11 (from Gortyn), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 42.1, “Rh.Al.” 1433a6, “PHal.” 1.58 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}; νικήσεις ἐν τῷ κρίνεσθαί σε {{NT|NT.Rom.3.4}}:—_passive_, with _genitive_, αἴ κα νικαθῇ τῶν ἐνεχύρων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Schwyzer” 177.9 (Crete, 5th c.BC)}}; see below 11.
**II** with _accusative_, **conquer, vanquish,** Ἕκτορα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.192, etc.: frequently with _dative_ modi, μάχῃ ν. Ἀχαιούς 16.79; ἀγορῇ ν. υἷας Ἀχαιῶν 2.370; πόδεσσι δὲ πάντας ἐνίκα 20.410; κάλλει ἐνίκων φῦλα γυναικῶν 9.130}}; πάντα ν. ἄνδρα.. κακοῖσιν **surpass** him in miseries, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 659; ν. τινὰ ἔν τινι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 213e, etc.}}; μὴ φῦναι τὸν ἅπαντα νικᾷ λόγον **excels** the whole account, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1224 (Lyric poetry); νίκα ἐν τῷ ἀγαθῷ τὸ κακόν NT.Rom.12.21: with _accusative_ _cognate_, μάχην ν. τινά 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.58, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.181, etc.}}:—_passive_, ἔστιν ἃ τῶν ἄθλων δὶς ἕκαστος ἐνικήθη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.5.2}}: with _participle_, ν. ἀλεξόμενός τινα {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 1.9.11, etc.}}
**II.b** as law-term {{Refs 8th c.BC+|compare(1.5), νίκης τήν μιν ἐγὼ νίκησα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.545}}:—_passive_, ἧ δέ κα νικαθῇ {{Refs|“Leg.Gort.” 1.23, +others}}; also of objects in dispute, damages, etc., **recover,** {{Refs|[prev. work]1.28, +others}}:—_passive_, **to be assigned, adjudicated,** {{Refs|[prev. work]1.55.}}
**II.2** generally, **overpower,** especially of passions, etc., νόον νίκησε νεοίη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.604; μὴ φόβος σὲ νικάτω φρένας 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 88, compare 133; [φύσις] νικᾷ τῷ ἥσσονι τὸ μεῖζον τῆς ἐλπίδος 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 176}}; βαρεῖαν ἡδονὴν νικᾶτέ με grievous is the pleasure ye **win prevailing over** me, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1204: with}}_infinitive_, μηδ᾽ ἡ βία σε.. νικησάτω τοσόνδε μισεῖν let not violence **prevail on** thee to.., {{Refs|[prev. author] “Aj.” 1334: with _genitive_ of comparison, νικᾷ γὰρ ἁρετή με τῆς ἔχθρας πολύ}} **weighs with** me **more than** enmity, from the comparable force in νικᾷ, {{Refs|[prev. work] 1357 codices}}
**II.3** _passive_, **to be vanquished,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus only in}}_participle_ νικηθείς (see. above {{Refs|1.1)}}; νικᾶσθαι ὕπνῳ, κέρδεσιν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 291, 342; ἡδονῇ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1272; συμφορᾷ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1195; also ὑπὸ τοῦ κακοῦ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.51; πρὸς ἱμέρου 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 932.4, etc.: sometimes with _genitive_, ἱμέρου νικώμενος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 1005; αὐτῆς [τε] τῆς δίκης.. αὐτοῦ τε τοῦ ἀληθοῦς νικᾶσθαι 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.87: frequently of persons, νικᾶσθαί τινος}} with _genitive_ of comparison, **to be inferior, yield to,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1353, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 315, “Cyc.” 454}}; ξείνων νενίκανται θύραι the doors **give way to** the guests, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 9.2; ἢν τοῦτο νικηθῇς ἐμοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1087.}}

3529:
** νίκ-η** [ῑ], ἡ,
**victory,** ν. φαίνεται Μενελάου **victory** clearly belongs to M., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.457, compare 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 80, etc.; μάχης ν. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.26, 8.171; ν. πολέμου 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 641a, compare c; ἡ ἐν τῷ πολέμῳ ν. [prev. work] 647b}}: frequently of **victory in the games,** Ἰσθμία ν. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 2.13; ν. παγκρατίου or ἀπὸ π., [prev. work] 7(6).22, 6(5).60: with _genitive_ objecti, ν. ἀντιπάλων}} **victory over**.., [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 521; ἡ τῶν ἡδονῶν ν. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 840c: with _genitive_ _of things_, τῶν πολεμικῶν ν. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.4.5; ν. δοῦναί τινι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.845, etc.; ν. καὶ κράτος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 85; νίκην νικᾶν τινα, see at {νικάω} I.1, 11.1b.}}]
**2** later, generally, **mastery, ascendancy,** etc., in all relations, νίκην διασῴζεσθαι to keep the **fruits of victory,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.2.26, compare 4.1.15.}}
**3** **success** in a lawsuit, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.544}}; in the concrete, **damages,** etc., **recovered,** {{Refs|Leges Gortynensium 9.31.}}
**II** _proper name_, **Nike,** the goddess of victory, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 384, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 2.26, etc.; Νίκη Ἀθάνα Πολιάς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 134, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 457 (Lyric poetry), 1529.}}
**II.2** Astrology texts, name of sixth κλῆρος, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Paulus Alexandrinus Astrologus “K.” 3, “Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 1.160.}}

3538:
Included with: ** νίζω**,
{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus [273]}}; _imperative_ νίζε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.830}}; _infinitive_ νίζειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.374}}; _participle_ νίζων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.425, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1338}}: _Epic dialect_ _imperfect_ νίζον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.112, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.846}}:— the _present_ **νίπτω**, analogically formed from νίψω ἔνιψα, first in {{NT+5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 543, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “epigram” 19, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.19.5, NT.John.13.5, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Theseus” 10, though 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus}} uses _middle_ νίπτομαι {{Refs|“Mul.” 1.57 (but δια-νιζέσθω [prev. work] 84, περι-νιζέσθω [prev. work] 2.158)}}: _future_ νίψω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.376, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 255}}: _aorist_ ἔνιψα {{Refs|[prev. author] “Sthen.Prol.” 25}}; _Epic dialect_ νίψα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.505}}:—_middle_, νίζομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus (see. above)}}: _imperfect_ νίζετο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.224:}}_future_ νίψομαι (see. ἀπο-, ἐκ-νίζω){{LXX|late νιφήσομαι LXX.Lev.15.12:}}_aorist_ ἐνιψάμη; _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ νίψατο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.230:}}_perfect_ νένιμμαι (see. below): _aorist_ _passive_ ἐνίφθην (κατ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 2.23}}:—**wash the hands** or **feet** (see. entry near the end), νίζε δ᾽ ἄρ᾽ ἆσσον ἰοῦσα ἄναχθ᾽ ἑόν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.392; αὐτὰρ ἐπεὶ νίψεν [prev. work] 505, compare 358; τῷ σε πόδας νίψω [prev. work] 376}}, cf. Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.19; ἁ δὲ χεὶρ τὰν χεῖρα νίζει 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus}} [same place]:—_middle_, νίψατο δ᾽ αὐτὸς χεῖρας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.230, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 739}}; νίψασθαι, _absolutely_, **to wash one's hands,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.138, etc.}}; χεῖρας νίψασθαι ἁλός [with water] **from** the sea, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|2.261 (see. below 11); νίψασθαι λίμνης πόδα 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Fragmenta” 122; οὔρῳ νιψάμενος τοὺς ὀφθαλμούς 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.111.}}
**2** generally, **purge, cleanse,** νίψαι καθαρμῷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1228, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1191.}}
**II** **wash off,** ἐπεί σφιν κῦμα θαλάσσης ἱδρῶ.. νίψεν ἀπὸ χρωτός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.575; ἀπ᾽ αὐτοῦ δ᾽ αἷμα κελαινὸν νίζ᾽ ὕδατι λιαρῷ 11.830, 846; φόνον ἐμῆς ἔνιψε χειρός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Sthen.”}}[same place]:—_passive_, αἷμα νένιπται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.419:}} —_middle_, ἐκ ποταμοῦ χρόα νίζετο ἅλμην **he washed** the brine **off his** skin [with water] from the river, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.224.}}—Commonly used of **washing part** of the person, while λούομαι is used of **bathing,** πλύνω of **washing clothes,** etc.; but νίζω is sometimes used of things, σπόγγοισι.. τραπέζας νίζον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.112; [δέπας] ἔνιψ᾽ ὕδατος καλῇσι ῥοῇσι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.229; ὕδατι νίζειν.. πλίνθον 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 16.62.}} — _Attic dialect_ Prose writers use the word only in compounds, see.{ἀπο-}, ἐναπο-, ἐκ-νίζω. (Cf. OIr. **nigid,** Sanskrit _nénekti_ 'wash', **niktá**- 'washed', Gr. ἀ-νιπτό-ποδες, χέρνιβα, I.-{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **nig[uglide]**-.)

3539:
** νο-έω**,
_Aeolic dialect_ **νόημι** (which see): _Epic dialect_ _aorist 1_ νόησα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.91}}; _Ionic dialect_ ἔνωσα (ἐν-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.86:}}_perfect_ νενόηκα, _Ionic dialect_ νένωκα (ἐν-) {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.6}}; _imperative_ νενόηθι Hilgard **Excerpta e libris Herodiani** {{Refs|30}}:—_middle_, _Epic dialect_ _aorist_ νοήσατο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.501}}; _participle_ νοησάμενος {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 7.6, νωσάμενος 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1298, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.263, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 345, etc.:}} —_passive_ (mostly in _middle_ sense), _future_ νοηθήσομαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 2.175, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 17.1}}: _aorist_ ἐνοήθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 692c}}; also _Ionic dialect_ (ἐπ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.122, 6.115:}}_perfect_ νενόημαι, _Ionic dialect_ νένωμαι {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 10, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.53, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 182, Aëthlius 4: _3rd.pers._}}_singular_ _pluperfect_ ἐνένωτο (in _middle_ sense) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.77. Hdn.Gr. 2.253 cites νοῦνται from 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus (see. below)}} and _perfect_ _passive_ νένοται. —The compounds with ἀπό, διά, ἐν, ἐπί, μετά, πρό are used chiefly in _middle_:—**perceive by the eyes, observe** (οἱ ἀρχαῖοι τὸ ν. σωματικὸν.. ὑπολαμβάνουσιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 427a26), 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.396}}; ὀξὺ ν.{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 374, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 838, etc.}}; ὀφθαλμοῖσιν, ἐν ὀφθαλμοῖσι ν., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.422, 24.294.}}
**2** **perceive by the mind, apprehend,** τὸν δὲ ἰδὼν ἐνόησε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|11.599; οὐ.. ἴδον οὐδ᾽ ἐνόησα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.318, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.550, 24.337, etc.; ἡ δ᾽ οὔτ᾽ ἀθρῆσαι δύνατ᾽ ἀντίη οὔτε νοῆσαι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.478}}; ἢ λάθετ᾽ ἢ οὐκ ἐνόησεν or did not **take notice,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.537, compare 5.665}}; νοέεις δὲ καὶ αὐτός thou thyself **art aware of** it, {{LXX+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.257; θυμῷ νοέω καὶ οἶδα ἕκαστα 18.228; ν. τῇ καρδίᾳ LXX.Isa.44.18}}; πρὸ ὃ τοῦ ἐνόησεν one **perceives** before the other, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.224: _absolutely_, [θεὸς] οὖλος ὁρᾷ, οὖλος δὲ νοεῖ, οὖλος δὲ τ᾽ ἀκούει 6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 24; ταὐτὸν δ᾽ ἐστὶ νοεῖν τε καὶ οὕνεκέν ἐστι νόημα 4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 8.34}}: frequently in Philos., of **thought,** μάλιστα ἔοικεν ἴδιον [ψυχῆς] τὸ ν. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 403a8; ἔοικε δὴ τὸ ζῆν εἶναι κυρίως τὸ αἰσθάνεσθαι ἢ ν. [prev. author] “EN” 1170a19, compare 1166a22; καλῶς ν. καὶ λέγειν καὶ πράττειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 1.18}}: also with _participle_ added, ὡς ἐνόησεν ἔμ᾽ ἥμενον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.375}}; of a future event, νοέω κακὸν ὔμμιν ἐρχόμενον {{Refs|20.367}}: with _infinitive_, οὐκ ἐνόησα ἄψορρον καταβῆναι {{NT+8th c.BC+|11.62; πίστει νοοῦμεν κατηρτίσθαι τοὺς αἰῶνας NT.Heb.11.3: followed by ὡς.., 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.32, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epinomis” 977c; νόει θ᾽ ᾗ δῆλον ἕκαστον 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 4.13}}:—_middle_, νωσάμενος {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1298; νοούμενος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1487}}:—_passive_, **to be apprehended by thought,** τὰς ἰδέας νοεῖσθαι μὲν ὁρᾶσθαι δ᾽ οὔ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 507b}}; τὰ νοούμενα, opposed to τὰ αἰσθητά,{{NT|[prev. work] 508c; τὰ ἀόρατα τοῖς ποιήμασι νοούμενα NT.Rom.1.20.}}
**3** **think, consider, reflect,** φρεσὶ ν. ἔνθ᾽ εἴην ἢ ἔνθα” {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.81; μετὰ φρεσὶ σῇσι νόησον Αἰνείαν, ἤ κέν μιν ἐρύσσεαι ἦ κεν ἐάσῃς 20.311; οὐδ᾽ ἐνόησε κατὰ φρένα καὶ κατὰ θυμὸν ὡς.. [prev. work] 264}}; ἐπ᾽ ἀμφότερα ν. **look** to both sides, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.22: with _accusative_ _cognate_, ἄλλα νοέειν}} **to be** other wise **minded,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 7.168; also εἰπὲ δ᾽ ᾗ νοεῖς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1135, compare “El.” 1435}}: _participle_ νοέων, έουσα, **wary, discreet,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.577; τὴν μέν κεν ἐπαινέσσειε νοήσας 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 12, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.170}}; τὰ νοέων λέγει what he says **advisedly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.102}}; νοῶν καὶ φρονῶν **sane** and in his right mind, in wills, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Test.Epict.” 1.1, “PPetr.” 3p.4 (3rd c.BC)}}, etc.:— in _middle_, φρενὶ θεῖα νοῦνται {{Refs 6th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 129; ὑψηλὰ νενωμένος 6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 10.}}
**4** **consider, deem, presume** to be so and so, ὡς μηκέτ᾽ ὄντα κεῖνον.. νόει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 415}}; τόδε γὰρ νοῶ κράτιστον{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1176; δεῖ ν. συνεχῆ τὰ ἔνοπτρα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 373a19: with}}_infinitive_, δεῖ νοῆσαι τὸ μὲν ὑγρὸν εἶναι{{Refs|[prev. work] 340b24, etc.}}; compare νοητέον.
**II** **think out, devise, conceive** τοῦτό γ᾽ ἐναίσιμον οὐκ ἐνόησε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.122; ἔνθ᾽ αὖτ᾽ ἄλλ᾽ ἐνόησε θεά [prev. work] 382}}; ἄλλα μὲν αὐτὸς ἐνὶ φρεσὶ σῇσι νοήσεις, ἄλλα δὲ καὶ δαίμων ὑποθήσεται{{Refs 8th c.BC+|3.26; οὐ γάρ τις νόον ἄλλος ἀμείνονα τοῦδε νοήσει 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.104}}: frequently with _neuter_ _plural_ _adjective_, πεπνυμένα πάντα νοῆσαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.230}}; ἄνδρων πλεῖστα νοησάμενος **most cunning** of men, of Sisyphus, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 7.6; ὀρθὰ ν. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.3}}:—_passive_, ἐνθύμημα νενοημένον οὐκ ἀτόπως {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Thucydide” 37.}}
**II.2** **purpose, intend,** ἐσθλά τινι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 286; κακόν τινι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.81, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 1.15.}}
**III** with _infinitive_, **to be minded, intend,** οὐδ᾽ ἐνόησε ἐξερύσαι δόρυ **bethought himself,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.665}}; νοέω φρεσὶ τιμήςuσθαι{{Refs|22.235; νοέω δὲ καὶ αὐτὸς Ἕκτορά τοι λῦσαι 24.560}}; ἦ γὰρ νοεῖς θάπτειν σφ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 44, compare 770, “El.” 389, etc.}}:—_middle_, once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, μάστιγα.. νοήσατο χερσἰν ἑλέσθαι}} he **thought with himself** to take the scourge, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.501}}; ἐνέ- νωτο στρατεύειν he **was minded** to march, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.77, compare 7.206, 9.53.}}
**IV** of words, **bear a certain sense, mean,** πυθοίμεθ᾽ ἂν τὸν χρησμὸν ὅ τι νοεῖ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 55, compare “Nu.” 1186, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 407e}}; [εἰ] τοῦτο.. νοεῖ αὐτῷ if this **means** for him that.., {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 335e; also ἐπιδεῖξαι ἐθέλω τὸ νυνί μοι συμβεβηκὸς τί ποτε νοεῖ [prev. author] “Ap.” 40a}}; τὸ νοούμενον the **sense, meaning,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Po.Herc.” 991.4, +others—Not in 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus or Oratt.}}

3540:
** νό-ημα**, ατος, τό,
_Ionic dialect_ **νῶμα** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 110.10}} (but νόημα {{Refs|105.3)}}: (νοέω):—**that which is perceived, perception, thought,** τῶν νέες ὠκεῖαι, ὡς εἰ πτερὸν ἠὲ νόημα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.36 (compare ὀξύτερον νοήματος 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 59.148}}; ἅμα νοήματι 'in the twinkling of an eye', {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.11U.: hence ἡ ἅμα ν. περίοδος}} **lightning** survey, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] p.32 U.,compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Morte - Papiri Ercolanesi” 37, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 35); ν. φρενός 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 704 (Lyric poetry).}}
**2** **thought, purpose, idea, design,** τοιοῦτον ἐνὶ στήθεσσι νόημα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.330; Ζεὺς.. ἐνὶ φρεσὶν ὧδε νόημα ποίησ᾽ 14.273; νοήματα.. ἐκτελέει 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.104, compare 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 77, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 6.29; σοφώτατα νοήματα [prev. author] “O.” 7.72; οὐκ οἶδ᾽ ὄττι θέω· δίχα μοι τὰ ν. 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 36; ἐκτὸς τῶν ἐωθότων ν. στῆσαί τινα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.80; τὸ μὲν ν. τῆς θεοῦ, τὸ δὲ κλέμμ᾽ ἐμόν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1203, compare “Nu.” 743.}}
**3** in Philos., **thought, concept,** opposed to sensation, sense-presentation, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 8.34, etc.; φύσει διῄρηται τά τε ν. καὶ τὰ αἰσθήματα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 87; σύνθεσίς τις νοημάτων [prev. author] “de An.” 430a28}}; **discursive thinking,** as the function of διάνοια, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Hermes Trismegistus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 2.8.31.}}
**4** Rhet., **thought** as expressed in literary form, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Amm.” 2.24, 3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 12.1.}}
**II** **understanding, mind,** παρέπλαγξεν δὲ νόημα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.346, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.218, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 435, 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 110.10; αἷμα γὰρ ἀνθρώποις περικάρδιόν ἐστι ν. [prev. author] 105.3; θεὸς.. οὔ τι δέμας θνητοῖσιν ὁμοίιος οὐδὲ ν. 6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 23.2.}}

3541:
** νόθ-ος**, η, ον,
also ος, ον {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|(3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 279)}}, **bastard, baseborn,** i.e. born of a slave or concubine, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” (never in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”), ν. υἱός 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.727, etc.; παῖδες ν. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.103, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 27d}}; opposed to γνήσιος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.102, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1650}}; ὅδ᾽, εἰ ν. τις, γνησίοις ἴσον σθένει {{NT+8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 87; νόθοι καὶ οὐχ υἱοί ἐστε NT.Heb.12.8: _feminine_, κούρη νόθη 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.173.}}
**2** at Athens, **child of a citizen father and an alien mother,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.213, etc.; νόθος πρὸς μητρός 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Themistocles” 1.}}
**3** _plural_, in Egypt, a class of **temple-attendants,** Wilcken {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Chr.” 66 (3rd c.BC).}}
**4** of animals, **cross-bred,** πρόβατα {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PHib.” 1.32.15 (3rd c.BC).}}
**II** generally, **spurious, counterfeit, supposititious,** of persons and things, λογισμῷ τινι ν. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 52b, compare 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 26; ν. παιδείᾳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 741a; ν. ἡδοναί [prev. author] “R.” 587c}}; ἀοιδαί Call.[same place]; νόθον ἧπαρ ὁ σπλήν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 669b28}}; αἱ ν. πλευραί the **false** ribs, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 1.35.6, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 4.9, 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων ὀξέων παθῶν” 2.6; ν. πυρετός 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 11.30}}; ν. σάλπιγξ, of a serpent's hiss, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 35.214}}; ν. φέγγος, of the moon, opposed to γνήσιον, of the sun, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.628; ν. ἱματισμός}}**meretricious, Peripl.M.** {{Refs|“Rubr.” 39,49}}. _adverb_ **-θως** **insincerely, disingenuously,** {{LXX+5th c.AD+|LXX.3Mac.3.17, compare 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**II.2** of literary works, **spurious,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “Vita Plotini” 16.}}

3542:
** νομ-ή**, ἡ,
(νέμω) **pasturage,** ἀμφίβιον.. ἔδωκε ν. βατράχοισι Κρονίων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Batrachomyomachia 59; νομὰς νέμειν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.110}}; νομὰς νέμεσθαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 78, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 679a, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 575b4, etc.; ποιμνίων νομαί 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 761}}: in concrete sense, νομαὶ βοσκημάτων **herds,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.5.2.}}
**2** **food from pasturing,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 111 C, etc.; αἷμα, ν. σαρκῶν [prev. author] “Ti.” 80e; ἡ προσήκουσα ψυχῆς ν. [prev. author] “Phdr.” 248b; ν. τῶν μελιττῶν τὸ θύμον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 626 b20, compare “PCair.Zen.” 520.10 (3rd c.BC).}}
**3** **feeding, grazing,** of herds, νομὴν ποιεῖσθαι, ={νέμεσθαι}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 596a14.}}
**3.b** _metaphorically_, **spreading,** ν. πυρός {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.48.5, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 35}}; frequently of sores, etc., ν. ποιεῖσθαι **spread,** {{NT+3rd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.81.6; ὡς γάγγραινα, ν. ἕξει NT.2Tim.2.17, compare 3rd c.BC: Asclepiades Epigrammaticus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.995; ἵσταται ἡ ν., of baldness, [prev. author][prev. work]411; νομαί}}**spreading ulcers,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 2.13, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 13.860, +others; ν. σαρκὸς θηριώδεις 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.165e.}}
**II** **division, distribution,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.52 (_plural_), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 321c, +others}}; of an inheritance, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 36.12}}; ἡ πατρῴα (variant{πατρῴων}) ν. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1303b34}}; διεφθαρκὼς νομῇ χρημάτων τὸν δῆμον by **largess** of money, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.76, compare “IG” 5(1).1346 (_Laconian dialect_)}}: in _plural_, = Latin _donativa,_ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 3.8.9, 5.5.8, 6.8.8.}}
**II.2** **paying out** or **distribution** in bandaging, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Heliodorus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 48.25.1, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18 (2).741.}}
**III** in Law, = Latin _possessio,_ Wilcken {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“Chr.” 41 iii 20 (3rd c.AD)}}; μακρᾶς ν. παραγραφή, = **longae posses-** **sionis praescriptio,** Mitteis {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“Chr.” 374 i 3 (3rd c.AD)}}; ν. ἄδικος, = **injusta possessio,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|PTeb. 286.7 (2nd c.AD)}}; ἐπὶ νομῆς πέμπειν, = **in possessionem mittere,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Justinianus Imperator “Novellae” 53.4.1.}}
**IV** ἐν χειρῶν νομαῖς, ={ἐν χειρῶν νόμῳ} {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|compare (νόμος 1.1 e), “SIG” 700.29 (1st c.AD(?): Macedonius Epigrammaticus, 2nd c.BC).}}

3543:
** νομ-ίζω**,
_future_ νομιῶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 571, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.87, etc.}}; _Ionic dialect_ _1st pers._ _plural_ νομιοῦμεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.17; later νομίσω 3rd c.AD(?): Longus Scriptor Eroticus 1.1 codices, 5th-6th c.AD: Procopius Gazaeus Rhetor “Epistulae - Epistolographi” 12:}}_aorist_ ἐνόμισα, _poetry_ νόμισα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 5(4).2}}: _perfect_ νενόμικα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Axionicus Comicus 6.8}}:—_passive_, _future_ νομισθήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 240e, etc.:}}_future_ _middle_ νομιοῦμαι in _passive_ sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἱερῆς νούσου” 1}}: _aorist_ ἐνομίσθην (see. below {{Refs|1.1, 2)}}: _perfect_ νενόμισμαι, _3rd.pers._ _plural_ νενομίδαται {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 51.23}}; _Doric dialect_ _infinitive_ **-ίχθαι**{{Refs 5th c.AD+|Sthenidas Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.7.63}}: _pluperfect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἐνενόμιστο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 962}}: (νόμος):—**use customarily, practise,** ἓν τόδε ἴδιον νενομίκασι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.173}}; ν. γλῶσσαν **to have** a language **in common use,**{{Refs|[prev. work] 142; φωνήν [prev. author] 2.42; οὔτε ἀσπίδα οὔτε δόρυ [prev. author] 5.97}}; πανήγυριν, πληγὴν ἐν τῇ ὁρτῇ, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.63}}; ταῦτα.. Ἕλληνες ἀπ᾽ Αἰγυπτίων νενομίκασι **have adopted** these **customs** from the Egyptians, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 51, compare 4.27; ἱπποτροφίας ἐν Πανελλάνων νόμῳ 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 2.38; ἀργυροστερῆ βίον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 1003(989); ν. θειότατον νόμον 5th c.BC: Gorgias Rhetor et Sophista “fragment” 6D.}}; ν. ἐκκλησίαν **have** a **regular** popular assembly, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1275b7}}; ἀγορᾶς κατασκευὴν ν. (conjecture for ὀνομάζουσιν){{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. work] 1331a32; δραχμὰς ἇν Τροζάνιοι νομίζοντι “IG” 42(1).77.16 (Epid., 2nd c.BC)}}:—frequently in _passive_, **to be customary,** οὔτω τοῦτο νομίσδεται {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 24; ὅπου τὸ χαίρειν μηδαμοῦ νομίζεται 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 423; σωφροσύνη 'νενόμιστο}}**was the fashion,** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 962: _impersonal_, εἰκῇ νομίζεται 6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 2.13}}; ὡς νομίζεται as **is the custom,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 32, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 99 (Lyric poetry), etc.; οἷάπερ ν. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1046; οἷα τοῖς κάτω νομίζεται 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 327, compare 691; ᾗ νομίζεται [prev. author] “OC” 1603:}}_participle_ νομιζόμενος **customary,** γέρα τὰ ν. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.25; εὐχαὶ αἱ ν. [prev. author] 6.32; εἰς τὸν ν. χρόνον “IG” 12.19.15; τὰ ν.}}**customs, usages,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.35, 5.42, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1185; τὰ ν. μυστήρια 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 14; τὰ ἱερὰ τὰ ν. 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.82; συντελέσαι τὰ ν. τοῖς θεοῖς “IG” 12.22.4, compare 54.16; τὰ τοῖς θεοῖς ν. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.14}}; frequently of funeral rites, τὰ ν. ποιεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.13, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 19.33; ἐπειδὴ τὰ ν. αὐτῷ φέροιτο 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.243}}: also _aorist_ _participle_, τό τοι νομισθὲν τῆς ἀληθείας κρατεῖ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 86; τὰ ν. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 71 (Lyric poetry)}}: _perfect_ _participle_, τὰ νενομισμένα τοῖς κατοιχομένοις {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PRyl.” 153.6 (2nd c.AD),compare “SIG” 1109.34 (2nd c.AD).}}
**2** of a legislator, **enact,** ἐνόμισεν ἑνὶ ἱματίῳ δι᾽ ἔτους προσεθίζεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 2.4, compare 12.3, “Cyr.” 8.5.3: with _accusative_, [prev. author] “Lac.” 1.7}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 37.20; τὰ νομισθέντα ὑπὸ Μάρκου [prev. author] 78.22; compare νομιστέος 1.}}
**3** with _dative_, **make common use of, use,** φωνῇ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.117; ὑσί [prev. work] 63}}; νομίζουσι Αἰγύπτιοι οὐδ᾽ ἥρωσι οὐδέν, i.e. practise no such worship, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.50; ἀγῶσι καὶ θυσίαις 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.38; εὐσεβείᾳ [prev. author] 3.82; οὔτε τούτοις χρῆται οὔθ᾽ οἷς ἡ ἄλλη Ἑλλὰς ν. [prev. author] 1.77}}; especially **use as current coin,** ἐν Βυζαντίοις, ὅπου σιδαρέοισι νομίσμασιν νομίζουσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 96 (uncertain reading); ἐν Λακεδαίμονι σιδηρῷ σταθμῷ νομίζουσι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Eryxias” 400b}}:—hence in _passive_, **to be struck,** ἀργύριον νενομισμένον ἐς Τιβέριον, i.e. with the head of Tiberius, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 1.15.}}
**4** with _infinitive_, **to be accustomed** to do, νομίζουσι Διὶ θυσίας ἔρδειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.131, compare 133, 202, 3.15}}, etc.:— _passive_, νενόμισται τὰ σχέτλια ἔργα Λήμνια καλέεσθαι {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.138}}; γυμνοὺς εἰσιέναι νομίζεται **it is customary** for them.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 498, compare 1420, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.15, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.36.}}
**II** **own, acknowledge, consider as,** ὡς δούλους ν. τινάς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.1; τὸν προέχοντα ἔτεσι ν. ὡς πατέρα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 879c: ὡς}} is frequently omitted, ὄμμα γὰρ δόμων νομίζω δεσπότου παρουσίαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 169; τοὺς κακοὺς χρηστοὺς ν. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 610, compare “Ant.” 183, “El.” 1317; τοὺς αὐτοὺς φίλους νομιῶ καὶ ἐχθρούς “IG” 12.71.20; νομίσαι χρὴ ταῦτα μυστήρια 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 143}}; θεὸν ν. τινά **believe in** one as a god, σὺ Ἔρωτα οὐ θεὸν νομίζεις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 202d; θεὰν οὐ τὴν Ἀναίδειαν, ἀλλὰ τὴν Αἰδῶ ν. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 8.35}}; ν. τούτους [θεούς] **believe in** these [as gods], {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.59; οὓς ἡ πόλις ν. θεοὺς οὐ νομίζων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.1.1, “Ap.” 10, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 24b; τοὺς ἀρχαίους οὐ ν. [prev. author] “Euthphr.” 3b}}; but ν. θεοὺς εἶναι **believe** that there are gods, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ap.” 26c, “Lg.” 886a (compare below 4): without εἶναι, δίκην καὶ θεοὺς μόνον ν. [ἄνθρωπος] [prev. author] “Mx.” 237d}}; τὸ παράπαν θεοὺς οὐδαμῶς ν. to be an atheist, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Lg.” 885c, compare 908c, “Ap.” 18c, “Prt.” 322a; θεοὺς ν. οὐδαμοῦ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 497}}:—_passive_, **to be deemed, reputed, considered,** οἷς τὸ πέλειν τε καὶ οὐκ εἶναι ταὐτὸν νενόμισται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 6.8; Ἕλληνες ἤρξαντο νομισθῆναι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.51}}; οἱ νομιζόμενοι μὲν υἱεῖς, μὴ ὄντες δέ.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 40.47}}; ἡ -ομένη (variant{ὀνομαζ-}) πολιτεία {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1293b22.}}
**II.2** **esteem, hold in honour,** χρυσὸν.. περιώσιον ἄλλων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 5(4).2; οὔτε θεοὺς οὔτε ἀνθρώπους ν. 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.9}}:—_passive_, **to be esteemed,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 466b.}}
**II.3** with _accusative_ _of things_, **hold, believe,** ταὐτὰ περί τινος {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phdr.” 258c, etc.; ἐποίει ἄλλα παρ᾽ ἃ ἐνόμισεν [prev. author] “Min.” 320b}}; ἀκοῇ ν., opposed to πείρᾳ αἰσθάνεσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.81.}}
**II.4** with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, **deem, hold, believe that**.., πότερα νομίζεις δυστυχεῖν ἐμ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 800, compare “OT” 549, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.4.11; θεὸν νομίζουσι εἶναι τὸ πῦρ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.16}}: with _future_ _infinitive_, **expect** that.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 551}}: _aorist_ _infinitive_ is sometimes found in codices referring to _future_, ἐνόμισαν ἐπιθέμενοι ῥᾳδίως κρατῆσαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.3 (κρατήσειν in same phrase, 4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 2.3), compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.24, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.6; in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1082 the}}_aorist_ _infinitive_ may be gnomic.
**II.5** with _participle_, νομίσωμεν ἐκγενησόμενον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.68; νόμιζε.. ἄνδρα ἀγαθὸν ἀποκτείνων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.6.24; νόμιζε ταῦτα δεδογμένα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 450a, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 14.9 (assuming variant).}}
**II.6** with ὡς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.88.}}
**II.7** _passive_, with _genitive_ of the person in possession, τοῦ θεῶν νομίζετα; whose sanctuary **is it held to be?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 38}}; οὐ τοῦ κρατοῦντος ἡ πόλις ν.; {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ant.” 738.}}
**II.8** _absolutely_, νομίζοντα λέγειν to speak **with full belief,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 257d (nisi to be read ὀνειδίζοντα).}}
**II.9** **frequent,** μυχὸν ν. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 801 (Lyric poetry, uncertain reading).}}

3544:
** νομ-ικός**, ή, όν,
**relating to laws,** αἴτια, title of work by Democritus; **resting on law,** ἤθη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 625a}}; **conventional,** ν. δίκαιον, opposed to φυσικόν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1134b20; ν. φιλία, opposed to ἠθική, [prev. work] 1162b23.}} _adverb_ -κῶς **after the manner of law,** i.e. **in a broad, general way,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pol.” 1341b31.}}
**2** **forensic,** μάχαι {{NT|NT.Tit.3.9}}; ἀγῶνες, opposed to λογικοί, ἠθικοί, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 1.22.1}}; **relating to points** **of law** (opposed to matters of fact), στάσις, ζήτημα, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ τῶν στάσεων” 2, 3}}; ν. ὀνόματα law-terms, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Meth.” 2; τὰ ν.}}**law matters,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.37 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cicero” 26}}. _adverb_ **-κῶς** **by legal process,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.533b.}}
**II** **learned in the law,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 39, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Minos” 317e (_superlative_)}}; **doctor of the Jewish law,** {{NT|NT.Matt.22.35, +others}}
**II.2** **lawyer, notary,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cicero” 26, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “περὶ τῆς τάξεως τῶν ἰδίων βιβλίων” 5, “BGU” 326ii22 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II.3** **legal adviser, assessor** of a magistrate, Mitteis {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Chr.” 372 iii 18 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}; ν. ἄριστος {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2787 (from Aphrodisias), compare “BGU” 361 iii 2 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}

3545:
Included with: ** νόμ-ιμος**, η, ον,
also ος, ον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.22, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 400b24}}:— **conformable to custom, usage,** or **law,** ν. ὅρκος Legal cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.98; ν. ἔρωτες 5th c.BC: Gorgias Rhetor et Sophista “fragment” 6 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator; ἔργα δίκαια καὶ ν. 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 174}}; **legitimate,** ν. παῖδες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 815 (Lyric poetry)}}: hence, **customary, prescriptive,** φῶς {{Refs|[prev. work] 345 (Lyric poetry)}}, etc.; οἱ ν. θεοί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 954a; ἡ ἐπίδεσις ἡ ν. 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 14; νόμιμόν [ἐστί] τινι ποιεῖν τι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.8.8; ν. τινὰ δεδέσθαι [prev. author] “Mem.” 1.2.49.}}
**2** **observant of law,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Choerilus Epicus 3, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.12, 4th c.BC: Archytas Tarentinus Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.5.61; ν. καὶ κόσμιοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 504d}}; ν. πόλις Isoc. [prev. cited]
**II** νόμιμα, τά, **usages, customs,** ἄλλα ἄλλοισιν νόμιμα, σφετέραν δ᾽ αἰνεῖ δίκαν ἕκαστος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 215, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 334 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.79}}; ν. Δωρικά, Χαλκιδικά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.4, 5; τὰ κοινὰ τῶν Ἑλλήνων ν. [prev. author] 3.59}}; almost, ={νόμοι}, ἄγραπτα ν. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 455; ν. θεῶν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 19; τὰ εἰωθότα ν. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 265a; ἄγραφα ν. [prev. author] “Lg.” 793a, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.70}}; τὰ περὶ τοὺς θεοὺς ν., τὸ πρὸς τοὺς πολεμίους ν., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.6.4, “Cyr.” 1.6.34}}; ν. βαρβαρικά, title of treatise by Aristotle: rare in _singular_, τὸ πάντων ν. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 135.1.}}
**II.2** **legal rights,** ν. καὶ φιλάνθρωπα {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“BGU” 1074.2 (1st c.AD)}}; **process of law,** τοῖς ν. χρῆσθαι Mitteis {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Chr.” 88 i 12 (2nd c.AD)}}; ἄνευ νομίμων ἀπωθεῖσθαι to be **illegally** ejected, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PFay.” 124.18 (2nd c.AD)}}; εἴργεσθαι τῶν ν., i.e. ἱερῶν καὶ ἀγορᾶς, of persons accused of murder, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.34, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 57.2.}}
**II.3** **funeral rites** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare (νομίζω 1.1), 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 2.8; τιμᾶν τινας ἐσθήμασί τε καὶ τοῖς ἄλλοις ν. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.58.}}
**III** _adverb_ **-μως**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.14; κοσμίως καὶ ν. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 182a; ν. ἀποθανεῖν}}**in a natural way,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator “Fragmenta” 53.4}}; οἱ ν. ἀθλοῦντες, πεπαιδευμένοι, **professional** athletes, physicians, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.488, 17(1).26}}, compare 8.171: _comparative_ **-ώτερον**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.5.20}}: _superlative_ **-ώτατα**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 78.13.}}

3546:
** νόμ-ισμα**, ατος, τό,
**anything sanctioned by current** or **established usage, custom,** Ἑλληνικὸν ν. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 269, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1471}}; **institution,** οὐδὲν γὰρ ἀνθρώποισιν οἷον ἄργυρος κακὸν ν. ἔβλαστε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 296; θεοὶ ἡμῖν ν. οὐκ ἔστι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 248, with a play on signification 11}} (do not **pass current** with us).
**II** especially **current coin,** ν. κόψαι or κόψασθαι, coin **money,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.56, 4.166; τἀρχαῖον ν. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 720; ν. σύμβολον τῆς ἀλλαγῆς ἕνεκα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 371b, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1133b11, “Pol.” 1257a11, 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 6.20; τάλαντα νομίσματος 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.8; ν. ἡμεδαποῦ “IG” 12.91.4; τὸ ἐπιχώριον ν. “PCair.Zen.” 21.12 (3rd c.BC): _plural_ νομίσματα}}**pieces of money, coins,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 1.9.7.}}
**III** **full legal measure,** τοῦ χοῶς ἢ τῶν κοτυλῶν τὸ ν. διαλυμαίνεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 348.}}

3548:
** νομο-θεσία**, ἡ,
**legislation,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 427b, “Lg.” 684e: _plural_, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1354b2, Wilcken “Chr.” 6.11 (5th c.AD).}}
**II** **code of laws,** {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 30.35, “Comica Adespota” 110.2, LXX.2Mac.6.23, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.240b.}}
**II.2** _metaphorically_, of the **order** of nature, Vett. Val.344.1, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 22.}}
**II.3** _plural_, **arbitrary principles,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 2p.36U.}}

3549:
** νομοθετ-έω**,
_perfect_ νενομοθέτηκα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 126.13}}:—**frame laws,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 15.9, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 534d, etc.; Λακεδαιμονίοις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Apologia Socratis” 15; ταῖς μοναρχίαις 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.8; περί τινος [prev. author] 11.40; περί τινων 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1294a37; ὑπὲρ ὅλου τοῦ ἐμπορίου 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 56.48}}:—_middle_, **frame laws for oneself,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 398b, “Tht.” 177e, etc.}}:—_passive_, of a state, **to be furnished with laws, have a code of laws,** {{NT|[prev. author] “Lg.” 701d, 962e, cf. NT.Heb.7.11.}}
**II** **ordain by law,** τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 628d, “R.” 417b; ἐναντία τῷ ὅρκῳ τοῦ δήμου ν. 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.3}}: with _infinitive_, **enact,** τῶν ζῴων ἐστὶν ἃ σέβεσθαι ἐνομοθέτησε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 11.26, compare “POxy.” 1119.16 (3rd c.AD)}}: —in _middle_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 736c}}:—_passive_, **to be ordained by law,** {{NT+2nd c.BC+|NT.Heb.8.6; τὰ καλῶς νενομοθετημένα ἡμῖν ὑπὸ τῶν βασιλέων “OGI” 329.13 (Aegina, 2nd c.BC),compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Pr.Im.” 18: _impersonal_, περὶ ταῦτα οὕτω σφι νενομοθέτηται}} **it has been** so **ordained by law,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.41; ν. καλὸν [εἶναι] τὸ χαρίζεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 182b; ἦν νενομοθετημένον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1319a11.}}

3550:
** νομοθέτ-ης**, ου, ὁ,
**lawgiver,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.15, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.97, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 429c, NT.Jam.4.12, etc.}}
**II** in _plural_, at Athens, a committee charged with the revision of the laws, Decrees cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.83, “IG” 22.140.8, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.10, Legal cited in [same work] 24.21, etc.}}

3551:
** νόμος**, ὁ,
(νέμω) **that which is in habitual practice, use** or **possession,** not in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (compare 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “contra Apionem” 2.15), though read by 4th-3rd c.BC: Zenodotus Grammaticus in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.3.}}
**I** **usage, custom,** [Μοῦσαι] μέλπονται πάντων τε νόμους καὶ ἤθεα κεδνά {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 66; ν. ἀρχαῖος ἄριστος [prev. author] “fragment” 221}}; ἔνθα ν. (i.e. ἐστί) with _infinitive_, where it is **the custom**.., {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 25.5}}; ν. πάντων βασιλεύς **custom** is lord of all, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 169.1; ν. δεσπότης 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.104, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 715d; ν. τύραννος τῶν ἀνθρώπων [prev. author] “Prt.” 337d; ἴησις ὀθονίοισι κατὰ τὸν ν. τὸν ἀρθριτικόν 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 18; ὡς νόμος [prev. author] “Mochl.” 37}}: hence, **law, ordinance,** τόνδε.. ν. διέταξε Κρονίων.. θηρσὶ.. ἐσθέμεν ἀλλήλους {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 276; τρέφονται πάντες οἱ ἀνθρώπειοι ν. ὑπὸ τοῦ θείου 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 114; ἄφθογγον εἶναι τὸν παλαμναῖον ν. [ἐστί] 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 448; ν. κάλλιστον ἐξευρόντα, πειθαρχεῖν πατρί 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1177}}; ν. κοινός, ={ὀρθὸς λόγος}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Zeno Citieus Stoicus 1.43: _plural_, ἔργων.. ὧν νόμοι πρόκεινται ὑψίποδες 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 865 (Lyric poetry); νεοχμοῖς ν. Ζεὺς κρατύνει 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 150 (Lyric poetry).}}
**I.b** in **VT,** of the **law** of God, ἐν τῷ νόμῳ Κυρίου τὸ θέλημα αὐτοῦ {{LXX|LXX.Psa.1.2, +others, cf. LXX.Isa.2.3}}; νόμον ὃν ἐνετείλατο ὑμῖν Μωϋσῆς{{LXX|LXX.Deu.33.4}}; so in **NT,** ὁ ν. Μωϋσέως {{NT|NT.Luke.2.22, etc.; but also ὁ ν. τοῦ Χριστοῦ NT.Gal.6.2}}; ὁ ν. τοῦ Πνεύματος τῆς ζωῆς, opposed to ὁ ν. τῆς ἁμαρτίας καὶ τοῦ θανάτου, {{NT|NT.Rom.8.2; ν. τέλειος ὁ τῆς ἐλευθερίας NT.Jam.1.25.}}
**I.c** with Preps., κατὰ νόμον according to **custom** or **law,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 417, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.61, etc.; κὰν νόμον 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.78}}; οἱ κατὰ ν. ὄντες θεοί the **established** deities, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 904a; κατὰ νόμους 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 241}}; παρὰ νόμον contrary to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Eu.” 171 (Lyric poetry); παρὰ τοὺς τῆς φύσεως ν. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 83e; ἐν Πανελλάνων νόμῳ 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 2.38}}; ἐν Ἀδραστείῳ νόμῳ by **the law** of Adrastus, i.e. at the Nemean games, {{Refs|[prev. author] “N.” 10.28: especially in _dative_ νόμῳ}} **by custom, conventionally,** opposed to φύσει, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.39, 5th-6th c.BC: Philolaus Philosophus 9, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1094b16, etc.}}; ν. γλυκύ, ν. πικρόν, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 9; εἰ μή τις λέγοι ν. ὁρᾶν καὶ τὰς λεγομένας ποιότητας μὴ ἐν τοῖς ὑποκειμένοις εἶναι 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 4.4.29}}; ὅσον νόμου χάριν just for **form's** sake, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 43.14, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1076a27.}}
**I.d** **statute, ordinance made by authority,** [Σόλων] νόμους ἔθηκεν ἄλλους, τοῖς δὲ Δράκοντος θεσμοῖς ἐπαύσαντο χρώμενοι πλὴν τῶν φονικῶν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ath.” 7.1 (but τὸν Δράκοντος ν. τὸν περὶ τοῦ φόνου “IG” 12.115.5), etc.}}; νόμον τιθέναι, τίθεσθαι, see at {τίθημ; βασιλικὸς ν}. {{NT+2nd c.AD+|“OGI” 483.1 (Pergam., 2nd c.AD), NT.Jam.2.8}}: frequently of **general laws,** opposed to ψηφίσματα (special decrees), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 173d, etc.; ὅταν τὰ ψηφίσματα κύρια ᾖ ἀλλὰ μὴ ὁ ν. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1292a7}}: generally, **law,** ἄνευ ὀρέξεως νοῦς ὁ ν. ἐστίν {{Refs|[prev. work] 1287a32}}; ἄγραφος ν. Legal cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.85, etc.}}; opposed to γεγραμμένος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1373b6; ν. ἴδιος, opposed to κοινός, [prev. work] 4}}; ὁ ν. frequently as subject, οἱ ν. διδόασι τιμωρίας {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.12; ὧν ὁ ν. ἀγορεύει “Inscription Magn.” 92b16 (2nd c.BC)}}; μὴ ὁ ν. κρίνει τὸν ἄνθρωπον ἐὰν μὴ ἀκούσῃ πρῶτο; {{NT|NT.John.7.51.}}
**I.e** with _genitive_ _of things_, οὗτός τοι πεδίων πέλεται ν. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 388; Ὑλλίδος στάθμας ἐν νόμοις 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.62; τὸν φαρμάκων δίδαξε μαλακόχειρα ν. [prev. author] “N.” 3.55; ν. ἐμβολῆς καὶ διορθώσιος 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Mochl.” 38}}; ὁ ν. τοῦ κριοῦ, τοῦ ἀνδρός, τῶν ἐρανιστῶν, {{LXX+NT+3rd c.BC+|LXX.Lev.6.31 (7.1), NT.Rom.7.2, “SIG” 1198.14 (Arcesine, 3rd c.BC)}}; ἐς χειρῶν νόμον ἀπικέσθαι to come **to blows, into action,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.48}}; ἐν χειρῶν νόμῳ ἀπόλλυσθαι, περιπεσεῖν, die **in action,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. author] 8.89, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.57.8; μεταλλάξαι τὸν βίον ἐν χ. ν. [prev. author] 3.63.5, compare 3.116.9; Ἀσδρούβας.. ἐν χ. ν. κατέστρεψε τὸν βίον [prev. author] 11.2.1; τοὺς μὲν ἐν χ. ν. διέφθειρε [prev. author] 1.82.2}}; τοὺς ἐν χ. ν. τὰς πολιτείας καταλύοντας by 'direct **action',** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.5; but κτεῖναι ἐν ταῖς πολεμικαῖς ἐξόδοις ἐν χειρὸς νόμῳ}}**under martial law,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1285a10; τῷ τοῦ πολέμου νόμῳ κτησάμενος 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.33.}}
**I.2** Νόμος personified, οἱ θεοὶ σθένουσι χὡ κείνων κρατῶν N. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 800, compare 5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Fragmenta” 105, 160.}}
**II** **melody, strain,** οἶδα δ᾽ ὀρνίχων νόμως πάντων {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 67; ν. ἵππιος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.101; Ἀπόλλων ἁγεῖτο παντοίων ν. [prev. author] “N.” 5.25; ν. πολεμικοί 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.69; ἐπηλάλαξαν Ἀραὶ τὸν ὀξὺν ν. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 952 (Lyric poetry); κρεκτοὶ ν. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 463, compare “Anthologia Graeca” 9.584: _metaphorically_, τοὺς Ἅιδου ν. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 861.}}
**II.2** especially a type of early **melody** created by Terpander for the lyre as an accompaniment to Epic texts, ν. ὄρθιος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.24; ν. Βοιώτιος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 966; ν. κιθαρῳδικοί 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1282, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 700d, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1447b26, “Pr.” 918b13, etc.}}; also for the flute, ν. αὐλῳδικός {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1132d}}; without sung text, ν. αὐλητικός {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work]1133d, compare 138b, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 4.79}}; later, **composition including both words and melody,** e.g. {{Refs|Timaeus Grammaticus “Pers.”}}
**III** ={νοῦμμος} (which see), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 136, 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 162, “Inscription Délos” 407.21 (2nd c.BC)}}; ν. σηστέρτιοι, = Latin _nummi sestertii,_ {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|Inscription Prien. 41.13 (2nd c.BC).}}
**IV** Architecture texts, **course** of masonry, {{Refs|“IG” 12(2).11.17 (Mytil.).}}

3552:
** νοσ-έω**
(no _Ionic dialect_ form νουσέω is found), **to be sick, ail,** whether in body or mind, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.105, etc.}}; τῆς πόλεως.. οὔπω νενοσηκυίας not yet **having suffered from the plague,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.31; νενοσηκὸς αἷμα}}**diseased,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 521a18; νόσῳ ν. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 386: with _accusative_ _cognate_, νοῦσον νοσεῖν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.33, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 220; τελευταίαν νόσον ν. 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.30; τι τῶν ἀπορρήτων κακῶν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 293: with _accusative_ partis, νοσεῖν κῶλον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 41; ν. ὀφθαλμούς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 496a; νεφρούς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 671b11, etc.}}; τὸ νοσοῦν, ={νόσος}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 675, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 186b: frequently in}}_aorist_ νοσῆσαι, **fall sick,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.19, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.138, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1392a11, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Macr.” 22, etc.}}; of things, γῆ νοσεῖ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Atheniensium” 2.6}}; of plants, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 5.9.9:}} —_passive_, ἡμέραι αἱ νοσούμεναι days **on which one is ill,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἑπταμήνου” 7.}}
**2** of passion, ν. μάταν **to be mad,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 635 (Lyric poetry); θολερῷ χειμῶνι νοσήσας [prev. work] 207 (_anapaest meter_)}}; ἐξ ἀλαστόρων ν. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tr.” 1235}}; νοσῶν alone, opposed to σώφρων,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 435; ν. τὰς φρένας 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 329}}; ν. περὶ δόξαν **to have a morbid craving** for fame, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.546f; ψυχῆς νοσούσης ἐστὶ φάρμακον λόγος 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 550.}}
**3** generally, **suffer,** νοσεῖ τὰ τῶν θεῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 27; τοῖσιν οἰκείοις κακοῖς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 766; ἀπαιδίᾳ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 620; πονηρίᾳ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.5.18}}; ἐκεῖ νοσοῦμεν ὅτι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 581: with _accusative_ _cognate_, τόδ᾽ ἄλγος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1326}}; especially of states, **suffer** from faction and the like, ἡ Μίλητος νοσήσασα στάσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.28; νοσεῖ πόλις 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1015, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 243e; νοσοῦσι καὶ τεταραγμένοις 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.14; νοσοῦντας ἐν αὑτοῖς [prev. author] 9.50; ἀπόλωλε καὶ νενόσηκεν ἡ Ἑλλάς [prev. work]39; αἱ δὲ πόλεις ἐνόσουν [prev. author] 18.45; τὰ πράγματα νοσοῦντα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 6.4; νοσῶν γάμος 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 8.20.}}

3553:
** νόσ-ημα**,
_Ionic dialect_ **νούσ-**, ατος, τό, (νοσέω) **disease,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ φυσῶν” 1, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 755, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 656, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.49, 53, etc.; τὰ περὶ τὸ σῶμα ν. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.39; νοσήμασι περιπίπτειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.2.27; νοσήματα τῶν σπερμάτων 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 8.10.1; [τῶν φυτῶν] [prev. work] 4.14.1.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, of **passion, vice,** etc., ἔνεστι γάρ πως τοῦτο τῇ τυραννίδι ν. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 227; ν. γὰρ αἴσχιστον εἶναί φημι συνθέτους λόγους [prev. work] 685; νοσοῖμ᾽ ἄν, εἰ ν. τοὺς ἐχθροὺς στυγεῖν [prev. work] 978}}; of love, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 149.1; τὸ ν. τῆς ἀδικίας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 480b, compare 3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 3.103.}}
**2.b** of any **grievous affliction,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1293}}; especially of **disorder** in a state, τυραννίδα.. ἔσχατον πόλεως ν. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 544c, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.259, etc.}}

3554:
** νόσος**,
_Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ (not _Doric dialect_,{{Refs|compare “Berl.Sitzb.” 1927.156 (from Cyrene))}} **νοῦσος**, ἡ, **sickness, disease, plague,** νοῦσον ἀνὰ στρατὸν ὦρσε κακήν (i.e. Apollo), ὀλέκοντο δὲ λαοί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.10; νοῦσόν γ᾽ οὔ πως ἔστι Διὸς μεγάλου ἀλέασθαι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.411; δολιχὴ ν. 11.172; νοῦσοι ἀργαλέαι 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 92:— Phrases: ἐς ν. πεσεῖν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 473; ἐς ν. ἐμπίπτειν 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.20; νόσον ἐμπεπτωκέναι τοῖς κτήνεσιν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.41; μοι ν. ἐπήλυθεν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.200; νόσῳ ληφθέντι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 445}}; κάμνειν νόσον, ὑπὸ νόσου, see at {κάμν; ἀσθενεῖν ταύτην τὴν νόσον} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 19.24; ἐκ τῆς νούσου ἀνέστη 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.22}}; θήλεα ν.[{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 105, compare 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 4.12.2; ἱερὰ νόσος, see at {ἱερός} IV. 8: ν. defined, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 7.43.}}]
**II** generally, **distress, anguish,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 527, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 544 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}
**II.2** **disease of mind,** especially caused by madness, passion, vice, etc., ν. φρενῶν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 750 (troch.)}}; θεία ν., i.e. **madness,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 185 (Lyric poetry); μανιάσιν ν. [prev. work] 59; λυσσώδη ν. [prev. work] 452}}; of love, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Tr.” 491; Ἀφροδίτας ν. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 767 (Lyric poetry); ἀκόλαστον ἔσχε γλῶσσαν, αἰσχίστην ν. [prev. author] “Or.” 10; τῆς μεγίστης ν., ἀνοίας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 691d}}; ν. καὶ στάσιν οὐ ταὐτὸν νενόμικα; {{Refs|[prev. author] “Sph.” 228a.}}
**II.3** **plague, bane, mischief,** e.g. a whirlwind is θεία ν. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 421, compare 1141 (Lyric poetry)}}; of the trident of Poseidon, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 924 (assuming variant)}}; πεντεσύριγγος ν., of the pillory, Polyeuct. cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1411a23; κτείνειν τινὰ ὡς νόσον πόλεως 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 322d.}}

3557:
** νοσφ-ίζω**,
_Attic dialect_ _future_ νοσφῐῶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1427, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 43}}: _aorist_ I ἐνόσφισα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 6.62, etc.}}:—_middle_, _future_ νοσφίσομαι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“IG” 12(7).515.93 (Amorgos, 2nd c.BC)}}; _Epic dialect_ νοσφίσσομαι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.1108:}}_aorist_ ἐνοσφισάμην, _Epic dialect_ νοσφισάμην, νοσφισσάμην (see. below): _perfect_ νενόσφισμαι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 484.4 (3rd c.BC), 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 2.3.4, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lucullus” 37}}:—_passive_, _aorist_ ἐνοσφίσθην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.73, etc.:}}
**I** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} only _middle_ and _passive_, **turn away, shrink back,** νοσφισθείς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. passage], 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 94; νοσφίσατ᾽ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.425: _metaphorically_, ψεῦδός κεν φαῖμεν καὶ νοσφιζοίμεθα μᾶλλον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.81, 24.222.}}
**I.2** with _genitive_, **turn away from,** τίφθ᾽ οὕτω πατρὸς νοσφίζεα; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.98.}}
**I.3** with _accusative_, **forsake, abandon,** παῖδά τ᾽ ἐμὴν νοσφισσαμένην θάλαμόν τε πόσιν τε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4.263; elsewhere in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus of places, Κρήτης ὄρεα νιφόεντα νοσφισάμην [prev. work] 19.339; νοσφισσαμένη τόδε δῶμα [prev. work] 579, 21.77, 104}}; νοσφισθεῖσα θεῶν ἀγορήν {{Refs 7th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Cererem” 92; ὅρκον ἐνοσφίσθης 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 96}}; εἴ σε νοσφίζομαι if I **forsake** thee, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 693 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (ἀπονοσφίσσειεν first in “hymnus ad Cererem” 158)}} in _active_, **set apart, separate, remove,** τινὰ ἐκ δόμων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 641 (Lyric poetry); βρέφος ματέρος ἀποπρό [prev. author] “IA” 1286 (Lyric poetry); τινὰ ἀπό τινος 3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 1331}}; τινα {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.793: _metaphorically_, ν. τινὰ βίου}} **separate** him from life, i. e. **kill** him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1427}}; τῷ νύ μ᾽.. ἐκ βιότοιο νοσφίσατ᾽ (_imperative_) ἐσσυμένως {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 13.282}}; ν. τινά alone, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 438 (Lyric poetry), “Eu.” 211; ν. τινὰ ἐρωμανίης “Anthologia Graeca” 5.292 (Paul. Sil.).}}
**II.2** **deprive, rob,** τινά τι one **of** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 6.62; also ν. τινά τινος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 620 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 43; τοὺς θανόντας νοσφίσας ὧν χρῆν λαχεῖν [prev. author] “Supp.” 539}}; γέροντ᾽ ἄπαιδα νοσφίσας, i. e. ὥστε ἄπαιδα εἶναι, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Andr.” 1207 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II.3** _middle_, **put aside for oneself, appropriate, purloin,** νοσφίσασθαι ὁπόσα ἂν βουλώμεθα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.2.42, compare “SIG” 993.21 (Calauria, 3rd c.BC), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.16.6, Ἑλληνικά 1.18 (Gytheum, 1st c.AD): in}}_perfect_ _passive_, νενοσφισμένος πολλά Str.[same place],compare {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus [prev. cited]: ν. ἀπὸ τῶν ἀμφιτάπων, ἀπὸ τοῦ ἀναθέματος, ἀπὸ τῆς τιμῆς}} **appropriate part of..,** {{NT+3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Zenon Papyri du Caire [same place], LXX.Josh.7.1, NT.Act.5.2; ἐκ τοῦ χρήματος 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 6.234a: _absolutely_, “PPetr.” 3p.162 (3rd c.BC), NT.Tit.2.10.}}
**III** _middle_ in _active_ sense, **deprive, rob,** σφ᾽ ἀδελφὸς χρημάτων νοσφίζεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 153, compare 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.1108.}}
**III.2** in later poets, **remove,** τοὺς.. ἀπὸ Ξάνθοιο.. πνοιαὶ νοσφίσσαντο {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Dionysius Periegeta 684; νοσφίσατ᾽ ἐκ θυμοῖο καὶ ἡδέος ἐκ βιότοιο 4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 10.79.}} —Rare in _Attic dialect_ Prose.

3558:
** νότος**, ὁ,
**south wind** (opposed to Βορέας, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 363b15, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.331), εὖτ᾽ ὄρεος κορυφῇσι Ν. κατέχευεν ὀμίχλην 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.10; ὅ τε ν. καὶ ὁ λίψ, ἀνέμων ὑετιώτατοι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.25 (but ὁ ν. οὐκ ἀρχόμενος ἀλλὰ λήγων ὑέτιος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 942a29); ἐτέγχθη κρᾶτ᾽.. πληγῇσι νότου 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1457 (_anapaest meter_); χειμερίῳ νότῳ [prev. author] “Ant.” 335 (Lyric poetry); ὑγρὸς καὶ βαρύς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 597b11; ὑδατωδέστερος [prev. author] “Pr.” 943a5}}; ὅταν μὲν ἐλάττων ᾖ, αἴθριός ἐστιν, ὅταν δὲ μέγας, νεφώδης{{Refs|[prev. work] 942a34; καυματώδης [prev. author] “Mete.” 364b23: in _plural_, [prev. author] “HA” 612b6.}}
**2** N. personified as god of the South wind, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 380, 870.}}
**II** **south** or **south-west quarter,** πρὸς μεσαμβρίης τε καὶ νότου {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.8; πρὸς νότον κεῖται τῆς Λήμνου [prev. author] 6.139; τῆς δὲ γῆς τὸ πρὸς ν. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 24.6; τὸ πρὸς ν. τῆς πόλεως 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.6; βλέπειν πρὸς ν. “IG” 22.1227.18; ὁ τοῖχος ὁ πρὸς ν. [prev. work]12.372.51; πρὸς νότου ἀνέμου [prev. work]56; βασίλισσα νότου NT.Matt.12.42}}; ἀπὸ νότου with _genitive_, to the **south** of, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 164.17 (2nd c.BC)}}, etc.; later ἐκ νότου with _genitive_, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PStrassb.” 29.8 (3rd c.AD)}}, etc.: _genitive_ νότου **to the south,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|PTeb. 105.13 (2nd c.BC), etc.}}

3560:
** νουθετ-έω**,
(τίθημι) **put in mind**: hence, **admonish, warn, re- buke,** with _accusative_ _person_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.173; παραινεῖν ν. τε τὸν κακῶς πράσσοντα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 266; οὐδὲ νουθετεῖν ἔξεστί σε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 595}}; κᾆτα νουθετεῖς ἐμέ {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” ; Ἔρωτα “Lyrica Alexandrina Adespota” 8}} (**a**): with _accusative_ _of things_, ν. τάδε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1025, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 732 (Lyric poetry)}}; **advise concerning,** μηχανήματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 855 (troch.): with double _accusative_, τοιαῦτ᾽ ἄνολβον ἄνδρ᾽ ἐνουθέτει 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1156; ἅπερ με νουθετεῖς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 337, compare “Or.” 299; ν. τινὰ ὡς.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.2.15}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1193, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 29, etc.; πρὶν ὑπὸ σοῦ ταῦτα νουθετηθῆναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 301c.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **chastise,** ν. τινὰ κονδύλοις, πληγαῖς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 254, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 879d:}} —_passive_, coupled with κολάζεσθαι, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Grg.” 478e.}}

3563:
** νόος**, νόου, ὁ,
_Attic dialect_ _contraction_ **νοῦς**, _genitive_ νοῦ: {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} uses the _contraction_ form once, in _nominative_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.240, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Fragmenta” 205 (5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus never)}}: Trag. use _contraction_ form, except in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 742 (iambic), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1209 (Lyric poetry)}}: _Aeolic dialect_ _genitive_ νῶ {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 9.1; _accusative_ νῶν Sapph.[prev. work]25.2; νόον Ead.70 (assuming variant)}}: irregular forms are found in “NT” and later writers, _genitive_ νοός {{LXX+NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Rom.7.23, LXX.4Mac.1.35; _dative_ νοΐ NT.1Cor.1.10, [2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor] “Or.” 35(9).26; _nominative_ _plural_ νόες 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.86, 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.7.17, 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 96; _accusative_ _plural_ νόας 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Fragmenta - fragment uncertainFragmenta incerta” 7.27, 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de Mysteriis” 1.15, 5th c.AD: Ammonius Philosophus “in Aristotelis de Interpretatione commentarius - pars” 243.3 (variant), 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 103: _Attic dialect_ _plural_ νοῖ, _accusative_ νοῦς, _genitive_ νόων [prev. work] 122, _dative_ νοῖς [prev. passage]}}, is rare in early writers, as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 471, but frequently in later philosophy:}}
**1** **mind,** as employed in perceiving and thinking, **sense, wit,** οὐ λῆθε Διὸς πυκινὸν ν. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.461; ν. πολυκερδέα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.255; ν. ὁρῇ καὶ ν. ἀκούει, τἄλλα κωφὰ καὶ τυφλά 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 249, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 371}}; νόῳ **prudently,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.320}}; παρὲκ νόον **senselessly,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.133}}; σὺν νόῳ **wisely,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.86, 138}}; ξὺν νῷ with play on ξυνῷ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 114 (νόῳ codices 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes); ξὺν νῷ ἑλομένῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 619b; οὐδενὶ ξὺν νῷ [prev. author] “Cri.” 48c; μηδενὶ ξὺν νῷ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 580; τοῦ νοῦ χωρίς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 550; τοῦ ν. κενός [prev. author] “OC” 931}}; νόῳ λαβεῖν τι to apprehend it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.51}}; νόῳ σχεῖν, ἔχειν, **recall, remember,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 5.92.ή, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 490a; κοινὸς ν. 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.37 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 3.6.8}}; ἀγαθὸς ν., σπουδαῖος ν., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.61, 1.252 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}
**2** νοῦν ἔχειν in two senses,
**2.a** to have **sense,** be **sensible,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 553, “El.” 1013, 1465, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 535, etc.; ὁ νοῦς ὅδ᾽ αὐτὸς ν. ἔχων οὐ τυγχάνει 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1139}}; so ν. ὀλίγον κεκτημένος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 747; σμικρὸν νοῦ κεκτῆσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 887e; _impersonal_, τὸ γὰρ περισσὰ πράσσειν οὐκ ἔχει ν. οὐδένα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 68, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 68b}}; compare νουνεχόντως.
**2.b** νοῦν or τὸν ν. ἔχειν to have **one's mind** directed to something, ἄλλοσ᾽ ὄμμα, θητέρᾳ δὲ ν. ἔχειν {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 272, compare 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 25.2; τὸν ν. πρὸς αὑτὸν οὐκ ἔχων, ἐκεῖσε δέ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1418; δεῦρο ν. ἔχε [prev. author] “Or.” 1181; οἴκοι τὸν ν. ἔχειν [prev. author] “Ion” 251}}; ποῦ τὸν ν. ἔχει; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 156}}; τὸν ν. ἔχειν πρός τινα or τι (like προσέχειν τὸν ν.) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.19, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 504d; πρός τινι [prev. author] “Prt.” 324a, etc.; περί τινος [prev. author] “R.” 534b; ἐν πέρδιξιν “Anthologia Graeca” 7.206 (6th c.AD: Damocharis Epigrammaticus): conversely, ἐπὶ νοῦν ἐλθεῖν τινι}} to **occur** to one, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 3.15, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 7.24.3.}}
**3** **mind,** more widely, as employed in feeling, deciding, etc., **heart,** χαῖρε νόῳ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.78; κεῦθε νόῳ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.363; [χόλος] οἰδάνει νόον 9.554; ἐνὶ στήθεσσιν ἀτάρβητος ν. ἐστί 3.63}}; ν. ἔμπεδος, ἀκήλητος, ἀπηνής,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|11.813, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.329, 18.381}}; ν. εὐμενής, ἄγναμπτος, etc., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 8.18, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 164 (Lyric poetry), etc.; πολλῶν ἀνθρώπων νόον ἔγνω 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.3}}; ἐκ παντὸς νόου with all his **heart and soul,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.97}}; τῷ νῷ.. κἀπὸ τῆς γλώσσης in **heart** as well as tongue, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 936: frequently in phrase κατὰ νόον}} according to one's **mind,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.117, 7.104; εἰ τάδ᾽ ἔχει κατὰ νοῦν κείνῳ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1768 (_anapaest meter_); πράξειας κατὰ ν. τὸν ἐμόν [prev. author] “fragment” 469 (_anapaest meter_); κατὰ ν. πράξας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 549; χωρεῖ κατὰ ν. [prev. author] “Pax” 940, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 3e.}}
**4** **mind, resolve, purpose,** ἀγαθῷ νόῳ, i.e. kindly, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.60}}; τί σοι ἐν νόῳ ἐστὶ ποιέει; what do you **intend** to do? {{Refs|[prev. work] 109}}; ἡμῖν ἐν ν. ἐγένετο εἰπεῖν {{Refs|[prev. author] 9.46}}; ἐν ν. ἔχειν with _future_ _infinitive_, **to intend**.., {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.10 (variant)}}: with _present_ _infinitive_,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 27, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 344d}}; ποιέειν τι ἐπὶ νόον τινί to put into his **mind** to do.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.27}}; ἐπὶ νόον τρέπειν τινί.. {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.21; ταύτῃ [ὁ] ν. ἔφερε [prev. author] 9.120.}}
**5** **reason, intellect,** νόου φρενί {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 25, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 16.2, etc.; θεῖος ν. 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 112, compare [prev. author] cited in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 404a28}}; opposed to δόξα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 51d, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 428a5.}}
**5.b** “Mind” as the active principle of the Universe, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Anaxagoras Philosophus 12, etc.; Θαλῆς νοῦν τοῦ κόσμου τὸν θεόν “Placita Philosophorum” 1.7.11; ἡ τοῦ κόσμου γένεσις ἐξ ἀνάγκης καὶ νοῦ συστάσεως 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 48a, compare “Sph.” 249a, “Phlb.” 30c, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1072b20, “de An.” 430a17, 4th-3rd c.BC: Zeno Citieus Stoicus 1.28, 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 5.1.4.}}
**II** **act of mind, thought,** ἡμῖν δ᾽ οὔ τις τοῦδε νόος καὶ μῆτις ἀμείνων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.509; οὐ γάρ τις νόον ἄλλος ἀμείνονα τοῦδε νοήσει 9.104; οὐ γὰρ δὴ τοῦτον μὲν ἐβούλευσας νόον αὐτή 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.23.}}
**II.2** **purpose, design,** νόον τελεῖν τινι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.149; σάφα οἶσθ᾽ οἷος ν. Ἀτρεΐωνος 2.192.}}
**III** **sense, meaning** of a word, etc., οὗτος ὁ νόος τοῦ ῥήματος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.162, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1439, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.83.4, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.106 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, etc.}}; ὁ νόος τῆς θυσίης conjecture for νόμος in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.216}}; **meaning** of a work of art, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 4.28; πολὺς ν. ἐν ὀλίγῃ λέξει συνέσταλται 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.510e}}; πρὸς τὸν αὐτὸν νοῦν to the same **effect,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 15.3.7}}; πρὸς νοῦν οὐδὲν λέγοντες to the **point,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” p.96K.; οὐδὲ νοῦν ἔχον}}**senseless,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Po.” 5.29.}}
**IV** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus name for μονάς, “Theologumena Arithmeticae” 6. (Etym. uncertain; the _proper name_ Πολυνόϝα “IG” 9(1).870 hardly proves νόϝος.)}}

3565:
** νύμφ-η**, ἡ
(_Epic dialect_ _vocative_ νύμφᾰ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.130, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.743}} (_Aeolic dialect_ _accusative_ to {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus “Scholia in Theodosii Canones” 1.304)}}; later also as _nominative_, {{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 14.43}}; but _Doric dialect_ **νύμφᾱ**), **young wife, bride,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.492, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.172; ν. ἄγεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 529; Ἀελίοιο 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.14}}; opposed to νυμφίος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 783e}}; opposed to παρθένος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Praxilla Lyrica 5, “Comica Adespota” 1215; always relatively young, as Iris calls Helen, or as Eurycleia calls Penelope, νύμφα φίλη 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.130, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.743, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 150, “Andr.” 140 (both Lyric poetry).}}
**2** **marriageable maiden,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.560, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 298.}}
**3** **daughter-in-law,** {{LXX+NT|LXX.1Ki.4.19, NT.Matt.10.35.}}
**4** **young girl,** πενταέτης ν. {{Refs|“IG” 14.2040.2.}}
**II** “Nymph” or goddess of lower rank, θεαὶ Νύμφαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.616, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 130, “fragment” 171.5, +others, “IG” 12(8).358 (Thasos, 5th c.BC); N. κοῦραι Διὸς αἰγιόχοιο 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.105; N. ἅλιαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1470}} (_anapaest meter_; compare Ναϊάς, Νηρηΐς); N. Ὀρεστιάδες, Ὀρειάδες, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.420, 2nd c.BC: Bion Bucolicus 1.19, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1098 (Lyric poetry); N. Μελίαι 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 187, compare Ἀδρυάδες, Ἁμαδρυάδες, Δρυάδες}}; N. ὑάδες, ὑδριάδες, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|[prev. author] “fragment” 180, 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Antro Nympharum - Porphyrii Opuscula” 18}}; N. λειμωνιάδες, πετραῖαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1454 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 805.}}
**II.2** especially of **springs,** ὀνομάζεσθαι τὰς πηγὰς N. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 11.465a, compare 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 11.28}}: hence, poetically, **water,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|Anthologia Graeca 9.258 (1st c.AD: Antiphanes Macedo Epigrammaticus), 331 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus), compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.147f.}}
**II.3** in mystical theology, Ζεὺς ἄμβροτος ἔπλετο ν. {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Fragmenta” 21a4.}}
**II.3.b** applied to souls seeking birth, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Antro Nympharum - Porphyrii Opuscula” 18}}; compare νυμφεύω.
**III** **doll, puppet,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|Anthologia Graeca 6.274 (4th c.AD: Perses Epigrammaticus), 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Caesares” 332d.}}
**IV** **young bee** or **wasp,** in the pupa stage, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 551b2, 555a3.}}
**IV.b** **winged male of the ant,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**V** kind of **mollusc,** Speusipp. cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 3.105b.}}
**VI** **point of the ploughshare,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 1.252, Procl.ad 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 425.}}
**VII** **hollow between the under-lip and chin,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus “περὶ ὀνομασίας” 42, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.90, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**VII.b** depression on the shoulder of horses, {{Refs|“Hippiatrica” 26.}}
**VIII** **opening rosebud,** {{Refs 9th c.AD+|9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc.}}
**IX** **clitoris,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus “περὶ ὀνομασίας” 112, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 15.3.}}
**X** **niche,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC(?): Callixinus Historicus 2.}}

3566:
** νυμφ-ίος**, ὁ,
**bridegroom,** παιδὸς ὀδύρεται ὀστέα καίων νυμφίου {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.223; τὸν μὲν ἄκουρον ἐόντα βάλ᾽.. νυμφίον ἐν μεγάρῳ μίαν οἴην παῖδα λιπόντα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.65; ἁρμόζων κόρᾳ ν. ἄνδρα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.118, etc.; ζῆτε νυμφίων βίον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 161}}; opposed to νύμφη, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 783e: in _plural_, τοῖς νεωστὶ νυμφίοις}} to the **bridal** pair, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 366, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 757 (Lyric poetry)}}; νυμφίοισι παρθένοις occurs in Ps.-{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 741.}}
**2** **son-in-law,** {{LXX|LXX.Josh.15.6.}}
**II** as _adjective_ νύμφιος, α, ον, **bridal,** τράπεζα νυμφία {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.16; λέκτρα “epigram Gr.” 373 (from Aezani).}}

3567:
** νυμφ-ών**, ῶνος, ὁ,
**bridechamber,** {{LXX+NT+1st c.AD+|LXX.Tob.6.14, NT.Matt.9.15, 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 7.145, 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 7.8, “PLond.” 3.964.19 (2nd-3rd c.AD).}}
**II** temple of Dionysus, Demeter, and Persephone, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 2.11.3.}}
**III** a kind of νυμφαία, Ps.-{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 3.132.}}

3568:
** νῦν**
(for νυν, νυ, see infr. II), _adverb_ **now,** both of the **present moment,** and of the **present time** generally, οἳ ν. βροτοί εἰσιν mortals **of our day,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.272}}; so in _Ionic dialect_ and _Attic dialect_, οἱ ν. [ἄνθρωποι] men **of the present day,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.68; οἵ γε ν. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.105, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 5.4, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1069a26}}; ὁ ν. τρόπος, τὸ ν. βαρβαρικόν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.6}}; Βοιωτοὶ οἱ ν.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 12; ὁ ν. παρὼν χρόνος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 174, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 141e; ἡμέρα ἡ ν. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 351; νὺξ ἡ ν. [prev. author] “Ant.” 16; ἡ ν. ὁδός [prev. author] “El.” 1295; τὸ ν.}}**the present,** {{LXX+5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 218a6, +others; ἀπὸ τοῦ ν. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 152c, LXX.Gen.46.30, etc.; ἀπὸ ν. “Anthologia Graeca” 5.40 (2nd-3rd c.AD: Rufinus Epigrammaticus); ἕως τοῦ ν. LXX.Gen.46.34}}; μέχρι ν. (variant{μ. τοῦ ν}.) {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.110}}; τὰ ν. simply, ={ν}., {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.104, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 641, etc.; τό περ ν. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.101; τὰ δὲ ν. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 133 (Lyric poetry); τὸ ν. εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 506e, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.3.42, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 31.2; τὸ ν. ἔχον NT.Act.24.25.}}
**2** of the immediate past, **just now, but now,** ν. Μενέλαος ἐνίκησεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.439, compare 13.772, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.43, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 84, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.48; ν. γοῦν ἐπεχείρησας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 341c; ἡλίκα ν. ἐτραγῴδει 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.13.}}
**3** of the future, **presently,** ν. αὖτ᾽ ἐγχείῃ πειρήσομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.279, compare 20.307, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.200; ν. φεύξομαι, τόθ᾽ ἁγνὸς ὤν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 975}}; compare νῦν δή, νυνί.
**4** sometimes opposed to to what might have been under other circumstances, **as it is** (or **was), as the case stands** (or **stood), as a matter offact,** ν. δ᾽ ὁ μὲν ὣς ἀπόλωλε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.166; εἰ μὲν ὑπώπτευον, οὐκ ἂν.. ἐποιούμην· ν. δὲ κτλ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.126, compare 1.122, 3.113, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 384b, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.195, etc.}}; καὶ ν. even **so,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.4.24, 7.7.17.}}
**5** coupled with other Particles, τὰ ν. γε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 245, etc.; ν. γε μάν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.50}}; ν. δή, see entry : with other expressions of Time, ν... σήμερον, ν. ἡμέρη ἥδε, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.29, 13.828}}; ν. ἤδη **henceforth,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 801}} (_anapaest meter_), etc.; ν... ἄρτι **but now,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 396c.}}
**II** _enclitic_ (but see below) νυν, νυ. [νυ only _Epic dialect_, _Boeotian dialect_, and {{Refs 4th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Cypria (also 4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus in ὅνυ, which see)}}; νῠν twice in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.105, 23.485: ῡ?~X in Trag. (ῡ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 242, 246, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 705, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1678, etc.}}; ῠ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 92, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 91, etc.)}}, ῡ in Comedy texts {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1381, “Pl.” 975, +others), except 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 144, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 105 (Lyric poetry, citing 5th c.BC: Agatho Tragicus), and perhaps “Nu.” 141}}; both quantities in τοίνυν, which see]
**II.1** rarely of Time, **now,** perhaps so used in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.105, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 19.1, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 11.44, +others, 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 170.6.}}
**II.2** in _Epic dialect_ mostly as a particle of emphasis, ἧκε δ᾽ ἐπ᾽ Ἀργείοισι κακὸν βέλος· οἱ δέ νυ λαοὶ θνῇσκον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.382, etc.}}: frequently coupled with other Particles or Conjs., ἦ ῥά ν. {{Refs|4.93; καί νύ κεν 3.373; οὔ ν., μή νύ τοι, 10.165, 1.28}}; ἐπεί νύ τοι{{Refs|[prev. work] 416; ὥς νύ περ 2.258.}}
**II.3** in commands or entreaties, μή ν. μοι νεμεσήσετ᾽ {{Refs|15.115: frequently with other _adverbs_, δεῦρό ν.}} come **now !**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|23.485; ἐνταῦθά ν. ὕβριζε 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 82, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 1001, “V.” 149, “Pl.” 724; εἶά ν. [prev. author] “Pax” 467, “V.” 430, “Pl.” 316: frequently with _imperative_, φέρε ν. [prev. work] 789; ἄγε ν. [prev. author] “Pax” 1056, “V.” 381; σπεῦδέ ν. [prev. author] “Pl.” 414; σίγαν. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 87, Cratin. [prev. cited]; περίδου ν. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 644, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.3.21, etc.; ὕφαινέ ν. 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 18.8}}; so in _Boeotian dialect_, ν. ἔνθω {{Refs 5th c.AD+|“IG” 7.3172.88 (Orchom.); also in 5th c.AD: Cypria with _optative_ in commands, δυϝάνοι ν., δώκοι ν., “Inscription Cyprian dialect” 135.6,16 H. (from Idalion).}}
**II.4** in questions, τίς ν.; τί ν.; who, what, why **now?** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.373, 1.414, 4.31}}; ἦ νυ..; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.125. [In signf. I always _perispomenon_ In signf. II _perispomenon_ except when short, Hdn.Gr. 2.39, +others; _enclitic_ when short, sometimes in codices, as 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.485 (Papyrus in “AJP” 21.304, etc.}}; _oxytone_ when ={δή}, Tyrannioap.Hdn.Gr.2.27; καθ᾽ ὁμαλισμόν or κατ᾽ ἔγκλισιν when=δή, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 414, Scholia 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.664)}}. In codices usually _perispomenon_ in both senses, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 82, “Th.” 242, 246, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 705, “El.” 324, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 414, “V.” 758, 922, etc.}}; even νῠν is written νῦν in codices vett. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus}} **passim,** also in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 87, “Tr.” 92, etc.}}; hence νυν may frequently be restored where the sense requires it. The accent of τοίνῡ?~Xν perhaps shows that both νῠν and νῡν could be enclitic.—Position: in signf. I νῦν can occupy any position; in signf. II it prefers (like other enclitics, but also like{ἄν, δέ, γάρ}, etc.) the second place in the sentence, e.g. πρός νύν σε πατρός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 468, compare “OC” 1333; ἀπό νύν με λείπετ᾽ ἤδη [prev. author] “Ph.” 1177 (Lyric poetry); μετά νυν δός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 56 (Lyric poetry)}}; νυ (always enclitic) precedes other enclitics and allows only δέ to precede.] (Cf. Sanskrit _nú, n[umacracute], nūnám,_ O{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **nū** 'now', etc.)

