**2** of the immediate past, **just now, but now,** ν. Μενέλαος ἐνίκησεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.439, compare 13.772, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.43, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 84, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.48; ν. γοῦν ἐπεχείρησας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 341c; ἡλίκα ν. ἐτραγῴδει 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.13.}}
**3** of the future, **presently,** ν. αὖτ᾽ ἐγχείῃ πειρήσομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.279, compare 20.307, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.200; ν. φεύξομαι, τόθ᾽ ἁγνὸς ὤν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 975}}; compare νῦν δή, νυνί.
**4** sometimes opposed to to what might have been under other circumstances, **as it is** (or **was), as the case stands** (or **stood), as a matter offact,** ν. δ᾽ ὁ μὲν ὣς ἀπόλωλε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.166; εἰ μὲν ὑπώπτευον, οὐκ ἂν.. ἐποιούμην· ν. δὲ κτλ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.126, compare 1.122, 3.113, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 384b, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.195, etc.}}; καὶ ν. even **so,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.4.24, 7.7.17.}}
**5** coupled with other Particles, τὰ ν. γε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 245, etc.; ν. γε μάν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.50}}; ν. δή, see entry : with other expressions of Time, ν... σήμερον, ν. ἡμέρη ἥδε, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.29, 13.828}}; ν. ἤδη **henceforth,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 801}} (_anapaest meter_), etc.; ν... ἄρτι **but now,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 396c.}}
**II** _enclitic_ (but see below) νυν, νυ. [νυ only _Epic dialect_, _Boeotian dialect_, and {{Refs 4th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Cypria (also 4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus in ὅνυ, which see)}}; νῠν twice in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.105, 23.485: ῡ?~X in Trag. (ῡ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 242, 246, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 705, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1678, etc.}}; ῠ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 92, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 91, etc.)}}, ῡ in Comedy texts {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1381, “Pl.” 975, +others), except 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 144, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 105 (Lyric poetry, citing 5th c.BC: Agatho Tragicus), and perhaps “Nu.” 141}}; both quantities in τοίνυν, which see]
**II.1** rarely of Time, **now,** perhaps so used in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.105, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 19.1, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 11.44, +others, 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 170.6.}}
**II.2** in _Epic dialect_ mostly as a particle of emphasis, ἧκε δ᾽ ἐπ᾽ Ἀργείοισι κακὸν βέλος· οἱ δέ νυ λαοὶ θνῇσκον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.382, etc.}}: frequently coupled with other Particles or Conjs., ἦ ῥά ν. {{Refs|4.93; καί νύ κεν 3.373; οὔ ν., μή νύ τοι, 10.165, 1.28}}; ἐπεί νύ τοι{{Refs|[prev. work] 416; ὥς νύ περ 2.258.}}
**II.3** in commands or entreaties, μή ν. μοι νεμεσήσετ᾽ {{Refs|15.115: frequently with other _adverbs_, δεῦρό ν.}} come **now !**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|23.485; ἐνταῦθά ν. ὕβριζε 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 82, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 1001, “V.” 149, “Pl.” 724; εἶά ν. [prev. author] “Pax” 467, “V.” 430, “Pl.” 316: frequently with _imperative_, φέρε ν. [prev. work] 789; ἄγε ν. [prev. author] “Pax” 1056, “V.” 381; σπεῦδέ ν. [prev. author] “Pl.” 414; σίγαν. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 87, Cratin. [prev. cited]; περίδου ν. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 644, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.3.21, etc.; ὕφαινέ ν. 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 18.8}}; so in _Boeotian dialect_, ν. ἔνθω {{Refs 5th c.AD+|“IG” 7.3172.88 (Orchom.); also in 5th c.AD: Cypria with _optative_ in commands, δυϝάνοι ν., δώκοι ν., “Inscription Cyprian dialect” 135.6,16 H. (from Idalion).}}
**II.4** in questions, τίς ν.; τί ν.; who, what, why **now?** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.373, 1.414, 4.31}}; ἦ νυ..; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.125. [In signf. I always _perispomenon_ In signf. II _perispomenon_ except when short, Hdn.Gr. 2.39, +others; _enclitic_ when short, sometimes in codices, as 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.485 (Papyrus in “AJP” 21.304, etc.}}; _oxytone_ when ={δή}, Tyrannioap.Hdn.Gr.2.27; καθ᾽ ὁμαλισμόν or κατ᾽ ἔγκλισιν when=δή, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 414, Scholia 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.664)}}. In codices usually _perispomenon_ in both senses, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 82, “Th.” 242, 246, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 705, “El.” 324, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 414, “V.” 758, 922, etc.}}; even νῠν is written νῦν in codices vett. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus}} **passim,** also in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 87, “Tr.” 92, etc.}}; hence νυν may frequently be restored where the sense requires it. The accent of τοίνῡ?~Xν perhaps shows that both νῠν and νῡν could be enclitic.—Position: in signf. I νῦν can occupy any position; in signf. II it prefers (like other enclitics, but also like{ἄν, δέ, γάρ}, etc.) the second place in the sentence, e.g. πρός νύν σε πατρός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 468, compare “OC” 1333; ἀπό νύν με λείπετ᾽ ἤδη [prev. author] “Ph.” 1177 (Lyric poetry); μετά νυν δός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 56 (Lyric poetry)}}; νυ (always enclitic) precedes other enclitics and allows only δέ to precede.] (Cf. Sanskrit _nú, n[umacracute], nūnám,_ O{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **nū** 'now', etc.)

3571:
** νύξ**, νυκτός, ἡ,
**night**: either generally, **mght-season** (opposed to day), or **a night,** ν. ἀμβροσίη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.363; ν. ἄμβροτος 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.330}}; but ν. ὀλοή{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 19, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.567, compare below 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3}}, III; νυκτός **by night,** as _adverb_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.278, etc.; οὔτε ν. οὔτ᾽ ἐξ ἡμέρας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 780}}; νυκτὸς ἔτι **while it was** still **night,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.10; also τῆς νυκτός 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 78.3, 148; ν. τῆσδε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 21}}; ἄκρας ν. at dead of **night,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 285 (but ἄκρῃ νυκτί at}}**night**-fall, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 775, ἀκρόθι νυκτός}} on the verge of dawn, {{Refs|[prev. author] 308)}}; ἀωρὶ νυκτός, τῶν νυκτῶν, see at {ἀωρί}: in _plural_, τῶν νυκτῶν **at nights,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 668: rarely, νυκτί 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.12; ν. τῇδε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 644}}; νύκτα **the night** long, νύκτα φυλάσσειν to watch **the night** through, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.312, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.466: _plural_, νύκτας ἰαύειν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.325, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.154, etc.; δύω νύκτας, τρεῖς ν., [prev. work] 388, 17.515}}: in _Attic dialect_, ὅλην τὴν ν. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 177, 4th c.BC: Amphis Comicus 20.4; τὴν νύχθ᾽ ὅλην 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 3; τὰς νύκτας 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 32.14; ὅλας γε καὶ πάσας τὰς ν. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.54; νύκτας τε καὶ ἦμαρ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.490; νύκτας τε καὶ ἡμέρας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 151a; οὔτε νύκτ᾽ οὔθ᾽ ἡμέραν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 187; τὴν νύχθ᾽ ὅλην τήν θ᾽ ἡμέραν 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 53.1}}; νύκτα ἡμέραν ποιούμενος ἀπόστειλον (κατάπεμψον), i. e. without delay, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 314.7, “PSI” 5.514.3 (both 3rd c.BC)}}; μέσαι νύκτες **midnight,** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 52, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 621b; περὶ μ. νύκτας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.8.12; ἐν μέσῳ νυκτῶν [prev. author] “Cyr.” 5.3.52; πρωΐτερον μέσων νυκτῶν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.101; ἔξω μέσων ν. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 54.26.}}
**2** frequently with Preps., ἀνὰ νύκτα by **night,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.80}}; ἀνὰ πᾶσαν ν. all **night** through, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 1.32.4; διὰ νύκτα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.66, etc.}}; εἰς νύκτα, εἰς τὴν ν., towards **night,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 11.4, “HG” 4.6.7}}; ἐν νυττί (νυκτί), opposed to πεδ᾽ ἀμέραν, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Leg.Gort.” 2.14, “SIG” 527.40 (Dreros, 3rd c.BC)}}; κατὰ νύκτα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 561 (Lyric poetry)}}; ὑπὸ νύκτα to wards **nightfall,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.67, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 2.19}}; μετὰ νύκτας by **night,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 6.6}}; μεθ᾽ ἡμέραν καὶ διὰ νυκτός all through **the night,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 117e}}; ἐκ νυκτός after **nightfall,** {{LXX+6th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.2, LXX.Isa.26.9, etc.; ἐκ πολλῆς ἔτι νυκτός 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.67; ἐκ νυκτῶν 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 460, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 287, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 13, 17 (both _anapaest meter_); ἐκ νυκτὸς εἰς νύκτα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 368b}}; πόρρω τῶν νυκτῶν far into **the night,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Smp.” 217d, “Prt.” 310c}}; ἐπὶ νυκτί by **night,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.529; ἐφ᾽ ἡμέρῃ αἱ δ᾽ ἐπὶ νυκτί 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 102; ἐν νυκτί 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 653, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 1.9, etc.; ὀψίᾳ ἐν ν. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 4(3).36; ἐν ν. τῇ νῦν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 16; νύκτεσσιν ἔν θ᾽ ἁμέραις 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.130.}}
**3** in _plural_, **watches** of the night, {{Refs|[prev. work] 256}}; three such, παροίχωκεν δὲ πλέων νὺξ τῶν δύο μοιράων, τριτάτη δ᾽ ἔτι μοῖρα λέλειπται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.252}}; τρίχα νυκτὸς ἔην, for τρίτον μέρος τῆς νυκτὸς ἦν, it was the third watch, i. e. next before morning, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.312.}}
**II** _metaphorically_ of darkness, νυκτὶ καλύψαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.23, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.351, etc.}}
**II.2** _metaphorically_ of death, ἀμφὶ δὲ ὄσσε κελαινὴ ν. ἐκάλυψε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.310, +others; ν. Ἅιδης τε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 660.}}
**II.3** in Comparisons, of anything dark and direful, νυκτὶ ἐοικώς like **night,** of Apollo in his wrath, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.47, compare 12.463, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.606}}; τάδε νυκτὶ ἐΐσκει what is here he like ns **to night,** {{Refs|20.362}}; ὀλεθρία ν., of a great calamity, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1684 (Lyric poetry).}}
**III** Νύξ as _proper name_, **the goddess of Night,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.259, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 17, “Th.” 123, 211; N. ὀλοή [prev. work] 224.}}
**IV** **the night-** or **evening-quarter of heaven, the West,** πρὸς νυκτός {{Refs|[prev. work] 275. (Cf. Latin}}**nox,** Lithuanian _naktis,_ Gothic _nahts,_ etc.)

3572:
** νύσσω**,
_Attic dialect_ **νύττω**, _passive_, _perfect_ νένυγμαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 10.221}}: _aorist 1_ ἐνύχθην {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 2.109, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 10.390:}}_aorist 2_ ἐνύγην [ῠ], _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _optative_ νυγείη{{Refs|[prev. work]401}}; _participle_ νῠγείς {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.233, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 13.565, “App.Anth.” 3.129.6 (3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius)}}:—**touch with a sharp point, prick, stab, pierce,** ἔγχεϊ νύξε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.579; χείρεσσι.. ἀσπίδα νύσσων 16.704}}; χθόνα.. ἵπποι νύσσοντες χηλῇσι **dinting** the earth with their hoofs, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 62}}; ἀγκῶνι νύξας **having nudged** him with the elbow, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.485, compare 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 21.50, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.79e, etc.}}; γνωμιδίῳ γνώμην ν. **prick** it (and see what is in it), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 321}}; γέονταν. 'beard the lion in his den', {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD: Diogenianus Paroemiographus 1.52.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **sting,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ παρρησίας” p.64O.; νύξας ὁ λόγος 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 71, compare 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 1.49.}}
**II** **impinge upon,** especially of sense-impressions, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 4.5.1, 6.6.12}}:—_passive_, Chrysipp. [same place], {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “de Anima liber - pars” 130.15.}}
**II.2** _passive_, of the νεῦρα, **suffer lesion** (νύγμα {{Refs|1.2), Gal.[same places]}}

3573:
** νυστ-άζω**,
_future_ -άξω {{LXX|LXX.Isa.5.27}}: _aorist_ ἐνύσταξα {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 7.8, LXX.2Ki.4.6, +others; ἐνύστασα 4th c.BC: Dionysius Comicus 2.43, “Anthologia Graeca” 12.135 (3rd c.BC: Asclepiades Epigrammaticus)}}:— mostly _present_, **to be half asleep, doze,** νυστάζοντα οὐδένα ἂν ἴδοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.43; ὥσπερ οἱ νυστάζοντες ἐγειρόμενοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 31a; ὀφθαλμοὶ πλέοντες ὥσπερ τῶν νυσταζόντων 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 7.17}}; οὐχὶ νυστάζειν ἔτι ὥρα ’στίν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 639, compare 4th c.BC: Xenarchus Comicus 2.1, “Comica Adespota” 185; νυστάζοντος δικαστοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 405c: _metaphorically_, ν. τε καὶ ἀπορεῖ [prev. author] “Ion” 533a; τὸν νυστάζοντα καὶ ἀμαθῆ φύσει [prev. author] “Lg.” 747b}}; ἔν τινι **in** a thing, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.675b.}}
**2** **hang the head,** ἐδάκρυσεν καὶ ἐνύστασε {{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” l}}.with (Cf. Lithuanian _snústi_ (stem **snúd**-) 'grow drowsy'.)

3574:
** νυχθ-ήμερος**, α, ον,
**lasting a day and night,** δρόμοι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Periplus Maris Rubri [1st c.AD]” 15; πλοῦς 2nd c.BC: Scymnus Geographus 957.}}
**II** as _substantive_ **νυχθ-ήμερον**, τό, **a night and a day,** the space of {{NT+1st c.BC+|24 hours, NT.2Cor.11.25, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 7.508, 2nd c.AD: Cleomedes Astronomus 1.6, 1st c.BC: Ptolemaeus Epigrammaticus “Alm.” 3.9, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Hermes Trismegistus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 1.21.9, “Geoponica” 5.8. S, etc.}}

3576:
** νωθρ-ός**, ά, όν,
={νωθής}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 10, “Prorrh.” 1.102, 117, etc.; ν. σφυγμοί [prev. author] “Coac.” 136, 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων ὀξέων παθῶν” 2.9}}; καταφορὴ ν. falling into a **heavy** sleep, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 3.6, compare 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 165; ν. κινήσεις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 696b6; ν. σύνεσις 2nd c.BC: Demetrius Lacon Philosophus “Herc.” 1014.58; τῇ κινήσει ν. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 622b32; ν. ὁδίτης 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 275; νωθρότερος τὴν ἀκοήν 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 5.1}}. _adverb_ - θρῶς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Archytas Tarentinus Philosophus}} I; **leisurely, gradually,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 2.7}}: _neuter_ as _adverb_, ὄμμασι νωθρὰ βλέπουσα {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 5.54 (3rd c.BC: Dioscorides Epigrammaticus).}}
**2** of the mind, ν. καὶ λήθης γέμοντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 144b, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Amipsias Comicus 16 (_comparative_)}}; στόματα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Anaxippus Comicus 1.44 (_comparative_)}}; ν. καὶ μωροί {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 954a31; ἡσύχιος καὶ ν. 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 31.23.11; νωθραῖς ἐλπίσιν 2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 16.7}}. _adverb_ ἀγεννῶς καὶ νωθρῶς {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.90.6.}}
**II** _active_, **making sluggish,** νότοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 3.5, compare 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 6.48.}}

3577:
** νῶτον**, τό,
or **νῶτος**, ὁ, _plural_ always νῶτα, τά in early writers (οἱ νῶτοι {{LXX|LXX.3Ki.7.19(33))}}: the gender of thesg. is undetermined in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus and 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus; _neuter_ in 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.28, 4.83, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 237, 643, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 289, “Pax” 747, 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 132.6 (_anapaest meter_)}}, and always in _Attic dialect_ _accusative_ to {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 257, etc.: _accusative_ νῶτον}} is _masculine_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 2.30, 40, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 3.3}} (as cited by Hdn.Gr.{{Refs 4th c.BC+|1.215), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 512b17, 544a6, 4th c.BC: Ephorus Historicus 224J.}}:—**back,** both cf men and animals: _singular_, of a man, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.147, 13.289, etc.}}; of a boar, φρίσσεινῶτον{{Refs|[prev. work] 473; ν... ὄϊος καὶ πίονος αἰγός 9.207}}; of horses, ἐπὶ νῶτον ἐῗσαι {{Refs|2.765}}; of an eagle, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.9}}: _plural_ frequently used in Poets in sense of _singular_, δράκων ἐπὶ νῶτα δαφοινός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.308, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.225, etc.}}: sometimes in _Epic dialect_ of the **chine** of an animal served as food, νῶτα βοὸς.. πίονα{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 4.65; νώτοισιν δ᾽ Ὀδυσῆα διηνεκέεσσι γέραιρεν 14.437, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.321}}; of men in battle, τὰ νῶτα ἐντρέψαι to turn **the back,** i.e. flee, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.211}}; νῶτον ἐπιστρέψαι{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|Oracle texts [prev. work] 141; σν μὴ δῷς ν. μηδενί “PTeb.” 21.8 (2nd c.BC)}}; δοτέον τὰ ν. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.787f; δεῖξαι νῶτα [prev. author] “Marc.” 12}} (this phrase also of the winner in a race, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.557 (Antip. Thess.)); πίπτειν ἐπὶ νώτῳ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 91 (Lyric poetry)}}; κατὰ νώτου in **rear,** κατὰ νώτου γενέσθαι τινός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.9, 10}}; τὸ στρατόπεδον κατὰ ν. λαβεῖν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 75; κατὰ ν. βοηθεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.62, etc.; κατὰ νῶτα 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22.84}}; **back** of the finger, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus Gaz. “Ecphr.” 168.11.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **any wide surface,** especially of the sea, ἐπ᾽ εὐρέα νῶτα θαλάσσης {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.159, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.142, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 762; ἐν νώτοισι ποντίας ἁλός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 129; πόντου 'πὶ νώτοις [prev. work] 774}}; also of the land, σχίζε ν. γᾶς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.228, compare 26; χθονὸς ν. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 46}}; of the sky, ἀστεροειδέα ν. αἰθέρος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “fragment” 114a p. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 1067 (Lyric poetry); ἐπὶ τῷ τοῦ οὐρανοῦ ν. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 247c}}; but ἕσπερα ν. the **evening,** i.e. **western, sky,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 731 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II.2** **ridge** of a hill or rock, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.87, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 128 (Lyric poetry)}}; of a tomb, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Hel.” 842, etc.}}; of a chariot, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tr.” 572 (_anapaest meter_)}}; of a saw, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 6.204 (1st c.AD: Leonidas Alexandrinus Epigrammaticus).}}
**II.3** **nave** of a wheel, {{Refs|LXX [prev. cited]}}
**II.4** **back** of a page, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.624; τὰ κατὰ νώτου “POxy.” 1725.9 (3rd c.AD). (Perh. cf. Latin νᾰτε ¯ ς.)}}

3578:
** ξεν-ία**, ἡ,
_Epic dialect_ **ξενίη** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.286}}; _Ionic dialect_ **ξεινίη** (variant{ξεινηΐη}) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus (see. below)}}: (ξένος):— **hospitality shown to a guest, entertainment,** δώροισιν ἀμειψάμενος.. καὶ ξενίῃ ἀγαθῇ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.286}}; μείξεσθαι ξενίῃ ἠδ᾽ ἀγλαὰ δῶρα διδώσειν{{Refs|[prev. work] 314; κατὰ ξεινίην}}**hospitii causa,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.182}}; ἐπὶ ξενίαν ἐλθεῖν to come **as a guest,** [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.49; but, ἐπὶ ξενίαν καλεῖν, παρακαλεῖν, also ἐπὶ ξενίᾳ καλεῖσθαι, καλεῖν, probably only as _falsa lectio_ for{ἐπὶ ξένια}, see at {ξένιος} I.2}}]: in _plural_, {{NT+5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “O.” 4.17; ξενίαι καὶ φιλότητες 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.145; ἑτοίμαζέ μοι ξ. NT.Philm.22.}}
**2** **friendly relation between** two states, or between a person and a foreign state, ξεινίην τινὶ συντίθεσθαι, Latin _hospitium facere cum aliquo,_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.27, 3.39; ξ. τοῖσι Ἀκανθίοισι προεῖπε [prev. author] 7.116; ἐποιήσαντο ὅρκια ξεινίης πέρι καὶ συμμαχίης [prev. author] 1.69; διαλύσασθαι τὴν ξ. [prev. author] 4.154; τὰς παλαιὰς ξ. ἀνανεώσασθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.43}}; κατὰ τὴν ξ. because of their **friendly relations,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.6; διὰ τὴν ξ. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.816a}}; πρὸς ξ. τᾶς σᾶς by thy **friendship with us,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 515 (Lyric poetry)}}; ξ. τινός **with** him, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.51; φιλίαν καὶ ξ. [prev. work]284.}}
**3** **status of an alien,** opposed to that of a citizen, γραφὴ ξενίας indictment **of an alien for usurping civic rights,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ep.” 3.29}}; ξενίας φεύγειν (i.e. γραφήν) to be so indicted, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 718; ἀγωνίσασθαι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.60; ἁλίσκεσθαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.131; ξενίας γράψασθαί τινα [prev. author] 40.41.}}
**II** **guest-chamber**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PSI” 1.50.16 (4th-5th c.AD)}}; perhaps **lodging,** {{NT|NT.Acts.28.23.}}

3579:
** ξεν-ίζω**,
_Ionic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ **ξεινίζω**, _future_ -ιῶ, later **-ίσω**{{Refs|“Geoponica” 12.13.12: _Epic dialect_}}_aorist_ ἐξείνισσα, ξείνισσα, -ισα (see. below): (ξένος):—**receive** or **entertain as a guest,** ξείνους {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.355; τὸν μὲν ἐγὼ.. ἐῢ ἐξείνισσα 19.194; ἐννῆμαρ ξείνισσε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.174}}; ξείνισ᾽ ἐνὶ μεγάροισιν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 217; ξ. τινὰ ἐν δόμοις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 1013, etc.; ξ. [τινὰ] σίτοισι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 666}}; ξ. τινὰ πολλοῖς ἀγαθοῖς **to present with** hospitable gifts, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.3.2}}; ὑμᾶς ἐν πόλει ξενίσωμεν ὧν.. εἴχομεν **with** or **on** what we had, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1184: _metaphorically_, ὃν.. Ἄρης οὐκ ἐξένισεν}} i.e. who fell not in battle, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 96 (_anapaest meter_)}}: —_passive_, **to be entertained as a guest,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 73; ξενισθεὶς μὴ ἀντιξενίσαι 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ κακιῶν ί” p.30 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus; ὑπό τινος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.30, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.1.24, etc.; παρά τινι 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 14.30, NT.Act.10.6, 21.16; πρός τινα 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 109: _metaphorically_, λαχάνοισιν, ὥσπερ χῆνες, ἐξενισμένοι 5th c.BC: Theopompus Comicus 13.}}
**II** **surprise, astonish by some strange sight,** ξενίζουσαν καὶ καταπληκτικὴν πρόσοψιν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.114.4}}; ξ. τὴν ἀκοήν, of strange words, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 6.14; ξ. καὶ ταράττειν “Geoponica” 2.48.2}}; ξ. [τὴν τῶν πολλῶν συνήθειαν] **do violence to** the ordinary use of language, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Simplicius Philosophus “in Aristotelis de Caelo commentaria - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 679.28}}:—_passive_, **to be astonished,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ichn.” 137}}; τινι {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.23.5, 3.68.9, NT.1Pet.4.12; διὰ τὸ παράδοξον 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.49.7; ἐπί τινι [prev. author] 2.27.4, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 31.2; κατά τι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.33.1; μὴ συντρεχόντων ὑμῶν NT.1Pet.4.4; εἰ.. 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 8.15, compare 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 302.17; πῶς.. “PStrassb.” 35.6 (5th c.AD)}}; **to be puzzled, unable to comprehend,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Tactica - Griechische Kriegsschriftsteller” 1.6}}; of fresh leeches, **to be unaccustomed** to the skin, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Antyllus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 7.21.1.}}
**II.2** **make strange,** of plants and animals, i.e. **stunt their growth and distort** them, {{Refs|“Geoponica” 9.5.3 (_passive_)}}; τῷ πλήθει ξενιζομένη ἡ φύσις being **altered in character,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “Problemata - Physici et Medici Graeci Minores” 1.80, compare “Hippiatrica” 15.}}
**III** _intransitive_, **to be a stranger, speak with a foreign accent,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.18; τὸ ξενίζον τῆς λέξεως 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 12.53, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 45.}}
**III.2** **to be strange** or **unusual,** of diseases, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 17(1).162; ξ. τῷ σχήματι 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Anach.” 16; τῷ τρίβωνι [prev. author] “Merc.Cond.” 24; θάνατος.. τῇ τόλμῃ ξενίζων [prev. author] “Hist. Conscr.” 25.}}

3580:
** ξενο-δοκέω**,
_Ionic dialect_ **ξεινο-**, **entertain guests** or **strangers.** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.127, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 552, “Anthologia Graeca” 10.16 (3rd c.BC: Theaetetus Epigrammaticus)}}, etc.:—later **ξενο-δοχέω**, {{NT+1st c.BC+|NT.1Tim.5.10, 1st c.BC(?): Maximus Astrologus Tyr. 32.9, “Codex Justinianus [4th-6th c.AD]” 1.3.45.1b.}}
**II** **testify,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 311}}:— _middle_, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

3581:
** ξένος**, ὁ,
_Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ **ξεῖνος** (also frequently in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus, “N.” 7.61, +others}}, used by Trag. for the sake of meter even in _trimeter_, mostly in _vocative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 33, +others, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 798 codices, “El.” 247)}}, _Aeolic dialect_ **ξέννος** {{Refs|Hdn.Gr. 2.302}}; scanned ¯ ?~X and written ξεῖνος in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 28.6, 30.17: _Aeolic dialect_ _superlative_ ξεννότατος Scholia 12th c.AD: Tzetzes, Joannes Grammaticus in “An. Ox.” 3.356.18 (sed see near the end)}}.
**I** **guest-friend,** applied to persons and states bound by a treaty or tie of hospitality, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.313, etc.; ξεῖνοι δὲ.. εὐχόμεθ᾽ εἶναι ἐκ πατέρων φιλότητος 15.196; ξ. πατρώϊός ἐσσι παλαιός 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.215; ξ. δ᾽ ἀλλήλων πατρώϊοι εὐχόμεθ᾽ εἶναι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.187; φησὶ δ᾽ Ὀδυσσῆος ξεῖνος πατρώϊος εἶναι 17.522; later frequently coupled with φίλος, Πλούταρχος ὁ τούτου ξένος καὶ φίλος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.110, compare 18.46, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.1.5, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.19; βασιλέως πατρικὸς ξ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 78d.}}
**I.2** of parties giving or receiving hospitality, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.145, etc.}}; mostly of the **guest,** opposed to the host, ξεινοδόκοι καὶ ξεῖνος {{Refs|[prev. work] 543}}, etc.; ἁ ξείνα the **visitor,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.154}}; of **guests** at a club, opposed to σύνδειπνοι, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 118.4 (2nd c.BC)}}: less frequently of the **host,** {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.532, 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.208, NT.Rom.16.23, etc.: with _dative_, ξεῖνός τινι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.20, 22, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.13, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.1.10, etc.; also ξ. τινός [prev. work] 2.4.15.}}
**II** **stranger,** especially **wanderer, refugee** (under the protection of Ζεὺς ξένιος), sometimes coupled with ἱκέτης, Ζεὺς ἐπιτιμήτωρ ἱκετάων τε ξείνων τε ξείνιος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.270, compare 8.546; with πτωχός, πρὸς γὰρ Διός εἰσιν ἅπαντες ξεῖνοί τε πτωχοί τε 6.208.}}
**III** generally, **stranger,** **foreigner,** opposed to ἔνδημος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 225}}; opposed to ἀστός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.90, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 13, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.10, etc.; πολιατᾶν καὶ ξ. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 1.51, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 924 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 473d, etc.}}; opposed to ἐπιχώριος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Men.” 94d: coupled with μέτοικος, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.90, compare “IG” 12.39.53}}; with ἔπηλυς, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 24}}; opposed to a member of the family, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“PMasp.” 169.10 (6th c.AD), etc.}}
**III.b** as a term of address to any **stranger,** ὦ ξένε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 247, 2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus 1.5, etc.; ὦ ξένη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 204c.}}
**III.2** ={βάρβαρος}, at Sparta, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.11, 55.}}
**IV** **hireling,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.102}}; especially **mercenary soldier,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 12.949.89, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.1.10, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.152, etc.; ξ. ναυβάται 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.121}}: rarely simply, **ally,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 12.3.}}
**B** as _adjective_ **ξένος**, η, ον (also ος, ον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 94)}}, _Ionic dialect_ **ξεῖνος**, η, ον, **foreign,** not in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (in the phrases ξεῖνε πάτερ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.28, ἄνθρωποι ξεῖνοι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.202, both words are _substantive_)}}; frequently in later writers, ξείνα γαῖα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.118 codices; ξένης ἐπὶ χθονός 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1256; γᾶς ἐπὶ ξένας [prev. work] 1705 (Lyric poetry, compare ξένη)}}; ἐν ξένῃσι χερσί by **foreign** hands, {{Refs|[prev. author] “El.” 1141}}; ξ. δόμοι, πόλις, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 339 (Lyric poetry), 369, etc.}}; of **alien** property, ξ. ἄρουραι {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“PMasp.” 295.22 (6th c.AD).}}
**B.II** with _genitive_ _of things_, **strange to** a thing, **unacquainted with, ignorant of** it, ξ. τοῦ λόγου {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 219, compare “Anthologia Graeca” 4.3a.37 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus); ξ. τῶν διαθηκῶν τῆς ἐπαγγελίας NT.Eph.2.12, compare “BGU” 405.12 (4th c.AD).}} _adverb_ ξένως, ἔχω τῆς ἐνθάδε λέξεως I am **a stranger to** the mode of speech, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 17d; ἔχειν τῆς διαλέκτου 4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Orationes” 21.253c.}}
**B.III** **strange, unusual,** λόγοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 688 (Lyric poetry); τιμωρίαι 5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 104d; ποιεῖν ξένην τὴν διάλεκτον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1404b11, compare 1415a7; οὐδὲν ξ. ἐν τῷ παντὶ ἀποτελεῖται 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 266; τοῖς νέοις ποιεῖν ξένα τὰ φαῦλα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1336b34; ξένα ταῖς ὄψεσι 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 3.15}}; ὡς ξένου συμβαίνοντος NT.1Pet.4.12 {{NT|διδαχαὶ ποικίλαι καὶ ξ. NT.Heb.13.9; ξ. δαιμόνια NT.Act.17.18}}: _superlative_, πράξεων ὡς -οτάτων {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Herc.” 1251.5; ξ. αὐτῷ δοκεῖ τὸ πρᾶγμα 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Cont.” 13, etc.}} _adverb_ ξένως, λαλεῖν {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus P 0.5.12.}}
**B.III.2** τοῦ πνεύματος.. ῥύσις ὡς -ωτάτη air as **fresh** as possible, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ φύσιος ἀνθρώπου” 9. (From ξένϝος,compare πρόξενϝος “IG” 9(1).867, Ξενϝάρης [prev. work]869, Ξενϝοκλῆς, Ξένϝων, [prev. work]4.315,348: hence it is improbable that the _Aeolic dialect_ form was ξέννος.)}}

3582:
** ξέστ-ης**, ου, ὁ,
formed from Latin _sextarius,_ a Roman measure nearly = 1 **pint,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|Inscriptiones Graecae 7.3498.54 (from Oropus), 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 9.4.4, “Anthologia Graeca” 11.298, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 13.435, 1st c.AD: Damocrates Poeta Medicus cited in [same work] 13.989, “IG” 42(1).93(Epid., 3rd-4th c.AD), 6th c.AD: Philoponus, Joannes Philosophus “in Aristotelis Analytica Priora commentaria - pars” 27.19.}}
**II** **pitcher, cup,** {{NT+3rd c.AD+|NT.Mark.7.4, “POxy.” 921.23 (3rd c.AD)}}, Harp.Astr.in {{Refs|“Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 8(3).139.}}

3583:
** ξηραίνω**,
_future_ **-ᾰνῶ**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 575}}: _aorist_ ἐξήρἀνα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.109, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 2.3.2, but ἐξήρηνα [prev. author] “Hum.” 1, “Mul.” 2.112, 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “χρονίων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 1.3}}:—_passive_, _future_ ξηρανθήσομαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 1.516, etc., but}}_middle_ ξηρᾰνοῦμαι in same sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ παθῶν” 25, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 356b25}}: _aorist_ ἐξηράνθην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.345, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 5.30, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 31e}}: _perfect_ ἐξήρασμαι {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης” 2.66, “Loc.Hom.” 29, 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 217.13; ἐξήραμμαι 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 5.14.6, NT.Mark.3.1, “POxy.” 1188.19 (1st c.AD), Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1082}}; _infinitive_ ἀπ-εξηράνθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.17}}; _participle_ ἐξηρᾱμένος only late, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|Scholia 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 2.6}}: (ξηρός):**—parch, dry up,** ξηρανεῖ σ᾽ ὁ Βάκχιος E. [prev. cited]; of the sun, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.3.8, etc.; τὸ σῶμα πρὸς ἀέρα ξ. 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 6.203b}}; **make costive,** τὴν κοιλίην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 3.17, compare 2.20 (_passive_)}}:—_passive_, **to be** or **become dry, parched,** ἐξηράνθη πεδίον Il.[same place],{{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 88d, etc.}}; **to be withered,** ἐξηράνθη ἡ συκῆ {{NT+2nd c.BC+|NT.Matt.21.19, compare 2nd c.BC: Demetrius Lacon Philosophus “Herc.” 1012.12, “POxy.” [prev. cited]}}
**2** **drain dry,** ξηράνας τὴν διώρυχα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.109.}}
**3** _metaphorically_, κακουχεῖ αὑτὸν καὶ ξ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Teles Philosophus p.34 H.}}
**3.b** _passive_, of a paralytic, {{NT|NT.Mark.9.18.}}

3584:
** ξηρός**, ά, όν,
**dry,** opposed to ὑγρός, of a dried-up river, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.45; χειμάρρους ξηροὺς ὕδατος 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 4.3.2; ἠὴρ ξ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.26; ξ. ἄνεμος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 404; ξηροῖς ἀκλαύτοις ὄμμασιν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 696; ὀμμάτων ξ. κόραι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 389}}; μέτρα ξ. τε καὶ ὑγρά **dry** and liquid measures, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 746d; ὕλη αὔη καὶ ξ. [prev. work] 761d}}; ξ. γάλα, i.e. **ripe** cheese, {{Refs|12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1001.51 (compare περίξηρος)}}; so τυρὸς ξ., opposed to τυρὸς χλωρός, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 133.7, compare 3rd c.BC: Philoxenus Epigrammaticus 3.8}}; ἐν ξηροῖσιν ἐκτρέφειν on **solid** food, i.e. cereals, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 277}}; καρπὸς ξ., i.e. cereal, opposed to κ. ξύλινος, produce of trees, i. e. fruit, wine, or oil, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 115b; ξ. χόρτος}}**hay,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|PPetr. 3p.181 (3rd c.BC)}}; φοῖνιξ ξ. **dried** dates, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PSI” 1.33.14 (2nd c.AD)}}; ξ. καρποί, opposed to οἶνος, ἔλαιον, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.23.5}}; ξ. πυρίαι applications of **dry** heat, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 21, 2nd c.AD: Archigenes Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.621}}; compare ξηροπυρία. _adverb_ ξηρῶς **by the use of dry powder,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 6.3.13 (assuming variant).}}
**2** of bodily condition, **withered, lean,** δέμας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 239; ξηρὸς ὑπαὶ δείους 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 24.61; ξ. κοιλίη}}**costive,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 2.20.}}
**3** of the voice, compare ξηρόφωνος.
**II** **fasting**: hence, generally, **austere,** τρόποι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1452 (Lyric poetry)}}; of persons, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 16}}; **harsh,** opposed to ἡδύς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 784 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II.2** _metaphorically_, of style, πραγματεία ἀτερπὴς καὶ ξ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 505 (p.358 U.); τὸ ξ.}}**aridity,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 238}}; of critics, ξηροὶ Καλλιμάχου πρόκυνες {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 11.322 (1st c.AD: Antiphanes Macedo Epigrammaticus).}}
**III** as _substantive_ ἡ ξηρά (i.e. γῆ), **dry land,** opposed to ὑγρά, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 19.7 (also _comparative_ ξηροτέρα γῆ [prev. work] 6),cf. NT.Matt.23.15, etc.; τὸ ξηρόν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.68}}; ναῦς ἐπὶ τοῦ ξηροῦ ποιεῖν to leave the ships **aground,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.109; ναῦς ἐς τὸ ξ. ἐξωθεῖν [prev. author] 8.105}}; τὸ ξ. τοῦ ποταμοῦ **the part** of its bed **left dry,** [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.18: for 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.51 see at {ἀκράτιστος}.}}]
**III.2** ξηρά, ἡ, in a bath-house, **room for dry heat,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|POxy. 2145.12 (2nd c.AD).}}

3585:
** ξῠλ-ῐνος**, η, ον,
**of wood, wooden,** τεῖχος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.38}}; δόμος, οἰκίαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 3.49, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.108, etc.}}; ὁ ξ. καρπός produce **of trees,** i. e. fruit, wine, or oil, opposed to ξηρός (which see), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 115b, compare “OGI” 55.13 (Telmessus, 3rd c.BC), 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 15.1.20: _plural_, ξ. καρποί, opposed to σιτικοί, [prev. author] 5.4.2, compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 3.63, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 3.78d}}; opposed to ὁ Δημήτριος, {{Refs|“IG” 22.2492.19}}; ξύλιναι ὦναι, opposed to σιτηραί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 2554.17 (5th c.BC: Magnes Comicus Mae., 2nd c.BC)}}; τομὰ ἁ ξυλίνα cutting **of timber,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 42(1).76.9 (Epid., 2nd c.BC).}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **wooden,** νοῦς {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 11.275 (2nd c.AD: Apollonius Biographus Grammars ), compare 255 (6th c.AD: Palladas Epigrammaticus).}}
**3** ξύλινον, τό, **writing-tablet,** ξ. πύξινον {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PGrenf.” 1.14.12 (2nd c.BC).}}
**II** **of cotton,** {{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.Sir.22.16, 1st c.AD: Plinius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “Historia Naturalis” 19.14.}}

3586:
** ξύλον** [ῠ], τό
(_plural_ spelt ξύλεα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Abh. Berl. Akad.” 1928(6).32 (Cos, 5th c.BC))}}, **wood** cut and ready for use, **firewood, timber,** etc., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, mostly in _plural_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.507, 547, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.418}}; ξ. νήϊα ship-**timber,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 808; ξ. ναυπηγήσιμα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.25, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.4.4, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 706b, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 17.28}}; ξ. τετράγωνα **logs** cut square, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.186, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 325d, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1109b7.}}
**2** in _plural_, also, **the wood-market,** ἐπὶ ξύλα ἰέναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 403.}}
**II** in _singular_, **piece of wood, log, beam, post,** once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, ξ. αὖον.. ἢ δρυὸς ἢ πεύκης 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.327}}; ξ. σύκινον **spoon made of** fig **wood,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 291c}}; **peg** or **lever,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Motu Animalium” 701b9}}; **perch,** ἐπὶ ξύλου καθεύδειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1431: by poetry periphrastic, Ἀργοῦς ξύλον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 20}}; ἵπποιο κακὸν ξ., of the Trojan horse, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.152 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus): hence anything made of wood, as}}
**II.2** **cudgel, club,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.63, 4.180, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 357, “PHal.” 1.187 (3rd c.BC)}}; μετὰ ξύλων εἰσπηδῆσαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PTeb.” 304.10 (2nd c.AD)}}; ξύλοις συντρίψειν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Demon.” 50}}; of the **club** of Heracles, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 30.}}
**II.3** **an instrument of punishment,**
**II.3.a** **wooden collar,** put on the neck of the prisoner, ξύλῳ φιμοῦν τὸν αὐχένα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 592; ἐς τετρημένον ξ. ἐγκαθαρμόσαι.. τὸν αὐχένα [prev. author] “Lys.” 680}}; or,
**II.3.b** **stocks,** in which the feet were confined, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.37, 6.75, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 367, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.129; ξ. ἐφέλκειν 5th-6th c.BC: Polyzelus Comicus 3; ἐν τῷ ξ. δεδέσθαι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 10.16 (see. ποδοκάκκη),cf. NT.Act.16.24, “OGI” 483.181 (Pergam., 2nd c.AD)}}: also in _plural_, ἔδησεν ἐν τοῖς ξ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.45.}}
**II.3.c** πεντεσύριγγον ξύλον (see. entry) was a combination of both, with holes for the neck, arms, and legs, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1049.}}
**II.3.d** **gallows,** κρεμάσαι τινὰ ἐπὶ ξύλου {{LXX|LXX.Deu.21.22}}; ξ. δίδυμον{{LXX|LXX.Josh.8.29: proverbial, ἐξ ἀξίου τοῦ ξύλου κἂν ἀπάγξασθαι}} i.e. if one must be hanged, at least let it be on a noble tree, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“App.Prov.” 2.67, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 736}}; in **NT,** of the **cross,** {{NT|NT.Acts.5.30,10.39.}}
**II.3.e** **stake** on which criminals were impaled, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 222.10.}}
**II.4** **bench, table,** especially **money-changer's table,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 45.33.}}
**II.5** πρῶτον ξύλον front **bench** in the Athenian theatre, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 25, “V.” 90, cf. Scholia (at [prev. work]): hence οὑπὶ τῶν ξύλων}} the official who had to take care of the **seats,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hermippus Comicus 9 (according to Meineke).}}
**II.6** the Hippocratic **bench,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 13, “Art.” 72.}}
**III** of live wood, **tree,** [ὄρος] δασὺ πολλοῖς καὶ παντοδαποῖς καὶ μεγάλοις ξύλοις {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.4.5, compare 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Cererem” 41, 2nd c.BC: Agatharchides Geographus 55, LXX.Cant.2.3, +others: opposed to σάρξ, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 1.2.6, +others; τῷ ξ. τοῦ δένδρου ἀνάλογον τὴν λεγομένην εἶναι γῆν 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.7.11; τὸ ξ. τῆς ἀμπέλου 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 572}}; εἴρια ἀπὸ ξύλου, of cotton, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.47; εἵματα ἀπὸ ξύλων πεποιημένα [prev. author] 7.65, compare 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 7.75.}}
**IV** of persons, **blockhead,** {{Refs|Anthologia Graeca Appx. 4.187}}; of a stubborn person, σίδηρός τις ἢ ξ. πρὸς τὰς δεήσεις {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Achilles Tatius Astronomus 5.22.}}
**V** **a measure oflength,** = 3 (also 2 2/3) cubits, the side of the ναύβιον, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus}} “*Geometry texts” 23.4,11, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 669.11, 28 (3rd c.AD), 1053 (6th-7th c.AD).}}

3588:
** ὁ**,
**ἡ**, τό, is, when thus written,
**A** _demonstrative_ Pronoun.
**B ** in _Attic dialect_, definite or prepositive Article.
**C ** in _Epic dialect_, the so-called postpositive Article, = relative Pronoun, ὅς, ἥ, ὅ.—The _nominative_ _masculine_ and _feminine_ _singular_ and _plural_, ὁ, ἡ, οἱ, αἱ, have no accent in codices and most printed books, except when used as the relative; but ὁ, ἡ, οἱ, αἱ differ only in writing from ὃ, ἣ, οἳ, α; the _nominative_ forms of the article are said by Hdn.Gr.1.474 to be oxytone, and by {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 8.7 not to be enclitic. The forms τῶν, τοῖς, ταῖς were barytone (i. e. τὼν, τοὶς, ταὶς)}} in _Aeolic dialect_ _accusative_ to {{Refs 8th c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Aristarchus Grammaticus cited in 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 51.26. For οἱ, αἱ some dialects (not 5th c.AD: Cypria, compare “Inscription Cyprian dialect” 135.30H., nor Cretan dialect, compare “Leg.Gort.” 5.28, nor Lesbian, compare 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 81, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 5.1) and 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus have τοί, ταί (though οἱ, αἱ are also found in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus): other Homeric forms are _genitive_ _sing._ τοῖο}} _genitive_ and _dative_ dual τοῖιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.34, +others: _genitive_ _plural_ _feminine_ τάων [ᾱ], _dative_ τοῖσι, τῇς and τῇσι, never ταῖσι or ταῖς in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}— In _Doric dialect_ and all other dialects except _Attic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ the _feminine_ forms preserve the old ᾱ instead of changing it to η, hence _Doric dialect_ etc. ἁ, τάν, τᾶ; the _genitive_ _plural_ τάων contracts in many dialects to τᾶ; the _genitive_ _singular_ is in many places τῶ, _accusative_ _plural_ τώς, but Cretan dialect, etc., τόνς {{Refs|(“Leg.Gort.” 7.7, +others) or τός ([prev. work]3.50, +others)}}; in Lesbian _Aeolic dialect_ the _accusative_ _plural_ forms are τοὶς, ταὶς, {{Refs|“IG” 12(2).645 A 13, B 62}}; _dative_ _plural_ τοῖς, ταῖς (or τοὶς, ταὶς, see above), {{Refs|[prev. work]645 A 8, [prev. work]1.6}}; ταῖσι as _demonstrative_, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 16. The _Attic dialect_}} Poets also used the _Ionic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ forms τοῖσι, ταῖσ; and in Trag. we find τοὶ μέν.., τοὶ δέ.., for οἱ μέν.., οἱ δέ.., not only in Lyric poetry, as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 584, “Th.” 295, 298; οἱ μέν.. τοὶ δ᾽ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1404 (_anapaest meter_)}}; but even in a trimeter, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 424. In _Attic dialect_ the dual has usually only one gender, τὼ θεώ (for τὰ θεά) 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.113}} {ὅ}; τὼ πόλεε Foed. cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.23; τὼ ἡμέρα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.11; τὼ χεῖρε [prev. author] “Mem.” 2.3.18; τοῖν χεροῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 155e; τοῖν γενεσέοιν [prev. author] “Phd.” 71e; τοῖν πολέοιν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.75 (τά 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 769, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 424, 484, ταῖν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.17, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 5.16, etc. have been corrected)}}; in {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus the form τοῖς}} functions as _genitive_ dual _feminine_, μεσακόθεν τοῖς κράναιυν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Schwyzer” 664.8 (Orchom., 4th c.BC):}} —in Elean and _Boeotian dialect_ ὁ, ἡ (ἁ), τό, with the addition of -ί, ={ὅδε}, ἥδε, τόδε, _nominative_ _plural_ _masculine_ τυΐ **the following** men, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Schwyzer” 485.14 (Thespiae, 3rd c.BC), +others, compare infr. VIII. 5. (With ὁ, ἁ}} cf. Sanskrit _demonstrative_ _pronoun_ **sa, sā,** Gothic _sa, sō,_ ONorse **sá, sú,** Old Latin _accusative_ **sum, sam** (Enn.): —with τό [from *τόδ] cf. Sanskrit _tat (tad),_ Latin _is-tud,_ Gothic _pata_: —with τοί cf. Sanskrit _te,_ Lithuanian _tĩe,_ O{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **pá,** etc.:—with τάων cf. Sanskrit _tāsām,_ Latin _is-tarum_:— the origin of the relative ὅς, ἥ, ὅ (which see) is different.)
**A** ὁ, ἡ, τό, DEMONSTR. PRONOUN, **that,** the oldest and in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} the commonest sense: frequently also in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus (1.86, 5.35, +others)}}, and sometimes in Trag. (mostly in Lyric poetry, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 1047, etc.; in trimeters, [prev. author] “Th.” 197, “Ag.” 7, “Eu.” 174}}; τῶν γάρ.., τῆς γάρ.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Supp.” 358, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1082}}; seldom in _Attic dialect_ Prose, except in special phrases, see infr. VI, VII):
**A.I** joined with a _substantive_, to call attention to it, ὁ Τυδεΐδης **he**—Tydeus' **famous** son, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.660}}; τὸν Χρύσην **that venerable man** Chryses, I.II: and so with _appellative_, Νέστωρ ὁ γέρων N.—**that**aged man, {{Refs|7.324}}; αἰετοῦ.. τοῦ θηρητῆρος the eagle, **that which is called** hunter, {{Refs|21.252, +others}}; also to define and give emphasis, τιμῆς τῆς Πριάμου for honour, **namely that** of Priam, {{Refs|20.181}}; οἴχετ᾽ ἀνὴρ ὤριστος a man is gone, **and he** the best, {{Refs|11.288, compare 13.433, +others: sometimes with words between the _pronoun_ and Noun, αὐτὰρ ὁ αὖτε Πέλοψ 2.105; τὸν Ἕκτορι μῦθον ἐνίσπες 11.186, compare 703, +others}}:—different from this are cases {{Refs 8th c.BC+|like 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.409 αἴ κέν πως ἐθέλῃσιν ἐπὶ Τρώεσσιν ἀρῆξαι, τοὺς δὲ κατὰ πρύμνας τε καὶ ἀμφ᾽ ἅλα ἔλσαι Ἀχαιούς}} if he would help the Trojans, but drive **those** back to the ships—**I mean** the Achaeans, where Ἀχ. is only added to explain τούς, compare {{Refs|1.472, 4.20, 329, +others}}
**A.II** frequently without a _substantive_, **he, she, it,** ὁ γὰρ ἦλθε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.12, +others}}
**A.III** placed after its Noun, before the Relat. Prons., ἐφάμην σὲ περὶ φρένας ἔμμεναι ἄλλων, τῶν ὅσσοι Λυκίην ναιετάουσι far above the rest, **above those to wit** who, etc., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.172}}; οἷ᾽ οὔ πώ τιν᾽ ἀκούομεν οὐδὲ παλαιῶν, τάων αἳ πάρος ἦσαν.. Ἀχαιαί such as we have not heard tell of yet even among the women of old, **those women to wit** who.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.119, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.332; θάλαμον τὸν ἀφίκετο, τόν ποτε τέκτων ξέσσεν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.43, compare 1.116, 10.74:}} —for the _Attic dialect_ usage see below
**A.IV** before a Possessive _pronoun_ its _demonstrative_ force is sometimes very manifest, φθίσει σε τὸ σὸν μένος **that** spirit of thine, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.407, compare 11.608; but in 15.58, 16.40, and elsewhere it is merely the _Article_}}
**A.V** for cases in which the Homeric usage approaches most nearly to the Attic, see below {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus [near the start]}}
**A.VI** ὁ μέν.., ὁ δέ.. without a _substantive_, in all cases, genders, and numbers, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.: sometimes in Opposition, where ὁ μέν}} properly refers to **the former,** ὁ δέ to **the latter**; more rarely ὁ μέν **the latter,** ὁ δέ **the former,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 359e, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.32,34}}: sometimes in Partition, **the one.., the other..,** etc.—The Noun with it is regularly in _genitive_ _plural_, being divided by the ὁ μέν.., ὁ δέ.., into parts, ἠΐθεοι καὶ παρθένοι.., τῶν δ᾽ αἱ μὲν λεπτὰς ὀθόνας ἔχον, οἱ δὲ χιτῶνας εἵατο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.595; τῶν πόλεων αἱ μὲν τυραννοῦνται, αἱ δὲ δημοκρατοῦνται, αἱ δὲ ἀριστοκρατοῦνται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 338d, etc.}}: but frequently the Noun is in the same case, by a kind of apposition, ἴδον υἷε Δάρητος, τὸν μὲν ἀλευάμενον τὸν δὲ κτάμενον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.28, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.73, etc.}}: so in Trag. and _Attic dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 22, etc.; πηγὴ ἡ μὲν εἰς αὐτὸν ἔδυ, ἡ δὲ ἔξω ἀπορρεῖ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 255c}}; if the Noun be collective, it is in the _genitive_ _singular_, ὁ μὲν πεπραμένος ἦν τοῦ σίτου, ὁ δὲ ἔνδον ἀποκείμενος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 42.6: sometimes a Noun is added in apposition with ὁ μέν or ὁ δέ, ὁ μὲν οὔτασ᾽ Ἀτύμνιον ὀξέϊ δουρὶ Ἀντίλοχος.., Μάρις δὲ.. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.317 - 19, compare 116; τοὺς μὲν τὰ δίκαια ποιεῖν ἠνάγκασα, τοὺς πλουσίους, τοὺς δὲ πένητας κτλ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.102, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 501a, etc.}}
**A.VI.2** when a _negative_ accompanies ὁ δέ, it follows δέ, e.g. τὰς γοῦν Ἀθήνας οἶδα τὸν δὲ χῶρον οὔ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 24; τὸν φιλόσοφον σοφίας ἐπιθυμητὴν εἶναι, οὐ τῆς μὲν τῆς δ᾽ οὔ, ἀλλὰ πάσης 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 475b; οὐ πάσας χρὴ τὰς δόξας τιμᾶν, ἀλλὰ τὰς μὲν τὰς δ᾽ οὔ· οὐδὲ πάντων, ἀλλὰ τῶν μὲν τῶν δ᾽ οὔ [prev. author] “Cri.” 47a, etc.}}
**A.VI.3** ὁ μέν τις.., ὁ δέ τις.. is used in Prose, when the Noun to which ὁ refers is left indefinite, ἔλεγον ὁ μέν τις τὴν σοφίαν, ὁ δὲ τὴν καρτερίαν.., ὁ δέ τις καὶ τὸ κάλλος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.41; νόμους.. τοὺς μὲν ὀρθῶς τιθέασιν τοὺς δέ τινας οὐκ ὀρθῶς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 339c, compare “Phlb.” 13c.}}
**A.VI.4** on τὸ μέν.., τὸ δέ.., or τὰ μέν.., τὰ δέ.., {{Refs|see infr. VIII. 4.}}
**A.VI.5** ὁ μέν is frequently used without a corresponding ὁ δέ, οἱ μὲν ἄρ᾽ ἐσκίδναντο.., Μυρμιδόνας δ᾽ οὐκ εἴα ἀποσκίδνασθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.3, compare 24.722, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.12, etc.: also followed by ἀλλά, ἡ μὲν γάρ μ᾽ ἐκέλευε.., ἀλλ᾽ ἐγὼ οὐκ ἔθελον 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.304}}; by ἄλλος δέ, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.147, etc.; τὸν μὲν.., ἕτερον δέ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 843, etc.; ὁ μέν.., ὃς δέ.. 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 205 (variant{οὐδέ}): less frequently ὁ δέ in the latter clause without ὁ μέν preceding, τῇ ῥα παραδραμέτην φεύγων, ὁ δ᾽ ὄπισθε διώκων (for ὁ μὲν φεύγων) 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.157; σφραγῖδε.. χρυσοῦν ἔχουσα τὸν δακτύλιον, ἡ δ᾽ ἑτέρα ἀργυροῦν “IG” 22.1388.45, compare μέν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator III; γεωργὸς μὲν εἷς, ὁ δὲ οἰκοδόμος, ἄλλος δέ τις ὑφαντής 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 369d, compare “Tht.” 181d.}}]
**A.VI.6** ὁ δέ following μέν sometimes refers to the subject of the preceding clause, τοῦ μὲν ἅμαρθ᾽, ὁ δὲ Λεῦκον.. βεβλήκει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.491; τὴν μὲν γενομένην αὐτοῖσι αἰτίην οὐ μάλα ἐξέφαινε, ὁ δὲ ἔλεγέ σφι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.3, compare 1.66, 6.9, 133, 7.6}}: rare in _Attic dialect_ Prose, ἐπεψήφιζεν αὐτὸς ἔφορος ὤν· ὁ δὲ οὐκ ἔφη διαγιγνώσκειν τὴν βοήν [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.87; ἔμενον ὡς κατέχοντες τὸ ἄκρον· οἱ δ᾽ οὐ κατεῖχον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.2.6: this is different from ὁ δέ in apodosi, see below 7; also from passages in which both clauses have a common verb, see at {ὅ γε} 11.}}]
**A.VI.7** ὁ δέ is frequently used simply in continuing a narrative, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.43, etc.}}; also used by [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus in apodosi after a _relative_, see at {ὅδε} III.3.}}]
**A.VI.8** the opposition may be expressed otherwise than by μέν and δέ, οὔθ᾽ ὁ.. οὔθ᾽ ὁ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.417; ἢ τοῖσιν ἢ τοῖς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 439; οὔτε τοῖς οὔτε τοῖς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 701e.}}
**A.VII** the following usages prevailed in _Attic dialect_ Prose,
**A.VII.1** in dialogue, after καί, it was usual to say in _nominative_ _singular_ _masculine_ καὶ ὅ; in the other cases the usual forms of the _Article_ were used (see. ὅς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus}} II.I and cf. Sanskrit _sas,_ alternatative form of **sa**); so, in _accusative_, καὶ τὸν εἰπεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 174a, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.9, etc.; also in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, καὶ τὴν φράσαι 6.61, +others}}
**A.VII.2** ὁ καὶ ὁ **such and such,** τῇ καὶ τῇ ἀτιμίᾳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 721b}}: but mostly in _accusative_, καί μοι κάλει τὸν καὶ τόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.23, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 784d; τὰ καὶ τὰ πεπονθώς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.141, compare 9.68; τὸ καὶ τό [prev. author] 18.243}}; ἀνάγκη ἄρα τὸ καὶ τό it must then be **so and so,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1401a4, compare 1413a22}}; but τὰ καὶ τά **now one thing, now another,** of good and bad, τὸν δ᾽ ἀγαθὸν τολμᾶν χρὴ τά τε καὶ τὰ φέρειν {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 398, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 5.55, 7.20, +others; τῶν τε καὶ τῶν καιρόν [prev. author] “O.” 2.53}}; so πάντα τοῦ μετρίου μεταβαλλόμενα ἐπὶ τὰ καὶ ἐπὶ τά, of excess and defect, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 46; compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus VI.8.}}
**A.VIII** _absolutely_ usages of single cases,
**A.VIII.1** _feminine_ _dative_ τῇ, of Place, **there, on that spot, here, this way, that way,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.752, 858, +others: followed by ᾗ, 13.52}}, etc.: also in Prose, τὸ μὲν τῇ, τὸ δὲ τῇ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Atheniensium” 2.12.}}
**A.VIII.1.b** with a notion of motion towards, **that way, in that direction,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.531, 11.149, 12.124; τῇ ἴμεν ᾗ.. 15.46; δελφῖνες τῇ καὶ τῇ ἐθύνεον ἰχθυάοντες 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 210:}} —only _poetry_
**A.VIII.1.c** of Manner, τῇ περ τελευτήσεσθαι ἔμελλεν **in this way, thus,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.510.}}
**A.VIII.1.d** repeated, τῇ μέν.., τῇ δέ.., **in one way.., in another..,** or **partly.., partly..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 356, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 211a, etc.: without μέν, τῇ μᾶλλον, τῇ δ᾽ ἧσσον 4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 8.48.}}
**A.VIII.1.e** _relative_, **where, by which way,** only _Epic dialect_, as {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.118, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.229.}}
**A.VIII.2** _neuter_ _dative_ τῷ, **therefore, on this account,** frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.418, 2.254, +others (see. below): also in Trag., 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 239, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 510 (Lyric poetry); in Prose, τῷ τοι.. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 179d, “Sph.” 230b.}}
**A.VIII.2.b** **thus, so,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.373, 13.57, etc.: it may also, especially when εἰ}} precedes, be translated, **then, if this be so, on this condition,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.239, 3.224, 258, +others, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 29.11.—In 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus the true form is probably τῶ, as in codex A, or τώ,compare 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Adverbiis;” 199.2.}}
**A.VIII.3** _neuter_ _accusative_ τό, **wherefore,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.176, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.332, +others, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 142 (Lyric poetry)}}; also τὸ δέ _absolutely_, but **the fact is..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 23a, “Men.” 97c, “Phd.” 109d, “Tht.” 157b, “R.” 340d, “Lg.” 967a}}; even when the τό refers to what precedes, the contrast may lie not in the thing referred to, but in another part of the sentence (compare above{{Refs 5th c.BC+|VI. 6), τὸ δ᾽ ἐπὶ κακουργίᾳ.. ἐπετήδευσαν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.37; τὸ δὲ.. ἡμῖν μᾶλλον περιέσται [prev. author] 2.89}}; φασὶ δέ τινες αὐτὸν καὶ τῶν ἑπτὰ σοφῶν γεγονέναι· τὸ δὲ οὐκ ἦν but he was not, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Nicolaus Damascenus Historicus 58J.}}
**A.VIII.4** τὸ μέν.., τὸ δέ.., **partly.., partly..,** or **on the one hand.., on the other..,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.36, etc., compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.46}}; more frequently τὰ μέν.., τὰ δέ.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.173, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 534, etc.; also τὰ μέν τι.., τὰ δέ τι.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.1.14; τὸ μέν τι.., τὸ δέ τι.. 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Macr.” 14; τὰ μέν.., τὸ δὲ πλέον.. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.90: sometimes without τὸ μέν..}} in the first clause, τὸ δέ τι {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.107, 7.48: rarely of Time, τὰ μὲν πολλὰ.., τέλος δέ}} several **times.. ** and finally, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.85.}}
**A.VIII.5** of Time, sometimes **that** time, sometimes **this** (present) time, συνμαχία κ᾽ ἔα ἑκατὸν ϝέτεα, ἄρχοι δέ κα τοΐ (where it is possible, but not necessary, to supply ϝέτος) {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“SIG” 9.3 (Olympia, 6th c.BC): so with Preps., ἐκ τοῦ, _Epic dialect_ τοῖο}} from **that** time, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.493, 15.601.}}
**A.VIII.5.b** πρὸ τοῦ, sometimes written προτοῦ, **before this, aforetime,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.103, 122, 5.55, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1204, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 5, etc.; ἐν τῷ πρὸ τοῦ χρόνῳ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.32, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 462; τὸ πρὸ τοῦ 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 20.59.}}
**A.VIII.5.c** in Thess. Prose, ὑππρὸ τᾶς **yesterday,** τὰ ψαφίσματα τό τε ὑππρὸ τᾶς γενόμενον καὶ τὸ τᾶμον the decree which was passed **yesterday** (literal before **this** [day]), and to-day's, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 9(2).517.43 (Larissa, 3rd c.BC).}}
**A.VIII.6** ἐν τοῖς is frequently used in Prose with Superlatives, ἐν τοῖσι θειότατον a **most** marvellous thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.137}}; ἐν τοῖς πρῶτοι the **very** first, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.6, etc.}}; ἐν τοῖσι πρῶτος (πρώτοις codices) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 145.4}}; [Ζεὺς] Ἔρωτά τε καὶ Ἀνάγκην ἐν τοῖς πρῶτα ἐγέννησεν first **of all,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor “Orationes” 43(1).16, compare 37(2).2: when used with _feminine_ Nouns, ἐν τοῖς remained without change of gender, ἐν τοῖς πλεῖσται δὴ νῆες}} the greatest number of ships, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.17; ἐν τοῖς πρώτη ἐγένετο (i.e. ἡ στάσις) [prev. work] 82}}: also with _adverbs_, ἐν τοῖς μάλιστα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 8.90, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 52a, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.74e, 421d, 723e, “Brut.” 6, 11, +others, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 1.16.3, etc.; ἐν τοῖς χαλεπώτατα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.71; τὴν Αἴγυπτον ἐν τοῖς μάλιστα μελάγγειον οὖσαν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.364c}}: in late Prose, also with Positives, ἐν τοῖς παράδοξον {{Refs 1st c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor “Orationes” 48(24).47 codices; with πάνυ, ἐν τοῖς πάνυ 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.19, compare 66 (ἐν ταῖς πάνυ _falsa lectio_ 4.14, 15).}}
**B** ὁ, ἡ, τό, THE DEFINITE ARTICLE, **the,** to specify individuals: rare in this _signification_ in the earliest Gr., becoming commoner later. In {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} the _demonstrative_ force can generally be traced, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|see above 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus}} I, but the definite _Article_ must be recognized in places {{Refs 8th c.BC+|like 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.167, 7.412, 9.309, 12.289, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.372}}: also when joined to an _adjective_ to make it a _substantive_, αἰὲν ἀποκτείνων τὸν ὀπίστατον **the hindmost man,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.178; τὸν ἄριστον 17.80; τὸν δύστηνον 22.59; τὸν προὔχοντα 23.325; τῷ πρώτῳ.., τῷ δευτέρῳ.., etc., [prev. work] 265s q}}.; also in τῶν ἄλλων {{Refs|2.674, +others: with Advs., τὸ πρίν 24.543, +others; τὸ πάρος περ 17.720; τὸ πρόσθεν 23.583}}; also τὸ τρίτον{{Refs|[prev. work] 733; τὰ πρῶτα 1.6, +others}}; τὸ μὲν ἄλλο for **the** rest,{{Refs|23.454; ἀνδρῶν τῶν τότε 9.559.}}—The true _Article_, however, is first fully established in 5th C _Attic dialect_, whilst the _demonstrative_ usage disappears, except in a few cases, V. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus VI-VIII.}} —Chief usages, especially in _Attic dialect_
**B.I** not only with common Appellats., _adjectives_, and Parts., to specify them as present to sense or mind, but also frequently where we use the Possessive _pronoun_, τὸ κέαρ ηὐφράνθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 5}}; τὴν κεφαλὴν κατεάγην **my** head was broken, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.61, etc.}}; τοὺς φίλους ποιούμεθα we make **our** friends, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 190}}; τὰς πόλεις ἔκτιζον they began founding **their** cities, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.12; οὐχ ὑπὲρ τὴν οὐσίαν ποιούμενοι τοὺς παῖδας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 372b.}}
**B.I.b** omitted with _proper nouns_and frequently with Appellats. which require no specification, as θεός, βασιλεύς, see at {θεός} {{Refs|I.1, βασιλεύς}} III; ἐμ πόλει in **the** Acropolis, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.4.1, +others: but added to proper nouns, when attention is to be called to the previous mention of the person, as 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus (3.70) speaks first of Πειθίας and then refers to him repeatedly as ὁ Π.}}; compare Θράσυλος in {{Refs|[prev. author] 8.104, with ὁ Θ. [prev. work] 105}}; or when the person spoken of is to be specially distinguished, Ζεύς, ὅστις ὁ Ζεύς whoever **this** Zeus is, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 480}}; and therefore properly omitted when a special designation follows, as Σωκράτης ὁ φιλόσοφος: seldom in Trag. with _proper nouns_, save to give peculiar emphasis, like Latin _ille,_ ὁ Λάϊος, ὁ Φοῖβος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 729, “El.” 35, etc.: later, however, the usage became very common (the Homeric usage of ὁ with a _proper name_ is different, see A.I).}}
**B.I.c** Aristotle says Σωκράτης meaning the historical Socrates, as in {{Refs|“SE” 183b7, “PA” 642a28, +others, but ὁ Σωκράτης}} when he means the Platonic Socrates, as {{Refs|“Pol.” 1261a6, +others: so with other proper nouns, “EN” 1145a21, 1146a21, +others}}
**B.I.d** for Σαῦλος ὁ καὶ Παῦλος, etc., see at {καί} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 2.}}
**B.I.2** in a generic sense, where the individual is treated as a type, οἷς ὁ γέρων μετέῃσιν.. λεύσσει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.109; πονηρὸν ὁ συκοφάντης 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.242, etc.}}
**B.I.2.b** frequently with abstract Nouns, ἥ τε ἐλπὶς καὶ ὁ ἔρως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.45, etc.}}
**B.I.3** of outstanding members of a class, ὁ γεωγράφος, ὁ κωμικός, ὁ ποιητής, ὁ τεχνικός, see at {γεωγράφος}, κωμικός, ποιητής, τεχνικός.
**B.I.4** with _infinitives_, which thereby become _Substantives_, τὸ εἴργειν prevention, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 505b}}; τὸ φρονεῖν good sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1348 (_anapaest meter_), etc.: when the subject is expressed it is put between the _Article_ and the}}_infinitive_, τὸ θεοὺς εἶναι **the** existence of gods, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 62b}}; τὸ μηδένα εἶναι ὄλβιον **the fact** or **statement** that no one is happy, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.86.}}
**B.I.5** in _neuter_ before any word or expression which itself is made the object of thought, τὸ ἄνθρωπος **the word** or **notion** man; τὸ λέγω **the word** λέγ; τὸ μηδὲν ἄγαν **the sentiment** 'ne quid nimis', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 265 (Lyric poetry)}}; τὸ τῇ αὐτῇ **the phrase** τῇ αὐτῇ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 72e: and so before whole clauses, ἡ δόξα.. περὶ τοῦ οὕστινας δεῖ ἄρχειν}} the opinion about **the question** 'who ought to rule', {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 431e}}; τὸ ἐὰν μένητε παρ᾽ ἐμοί, ἀποδώσω **the phrase** 'I will give back, if.. ', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.1.21, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 327c, etc.; τοὺς τοῦ τί πρακτέον λογισμούς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.148}}; τὸ ὀλίγοι **the term** few, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1283b11.}}
**B.I.6** before _relative_ clauses, when the _Article_ serves to combine the whole _relative_ clause into one notion, τῇ ᾗ φὴς σὺ σκληρότητι **the** harshness you speak of, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 435a}}; τὸν ἥμερον καρπόν.., καὶ τὸν ὅσος ξύλινος (i.e. καὶ τὸν καρπὸν ὅσος ἂν ᾖ ξύλινος) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Criti.” 115b; τῶν ὅσοι ἂν.. ἀγαθοὶ κριθῶσιν [prev. author] “R.” 469b; ἐκ γῆς καὶ πυρὸς μείξαντες καὶ τῶν ὅσα πυρὶ καὶ γῇ κεράννυται [prev. author] “Prt.” 320d, compare 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Lycophrone” 2; ταύτην τε τὴν αἰτίαν καὶ τὴν ὅθεν ἡ κίνησις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 987a8; τὸν ὃς ἔφη 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 23.8: hence the _relative_, by attraction, frequently follows the case of the _Article_, τοῖς οἵοις ἡμῖν τε καὶ ὑμῖν, i.e. τοῖς οὖσιν οἷοι ἡμεῖς καὶ ὑμεῖς, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.3.25, etc.}}
**B.I.7** before Prons.,
**B.I.7.a** before the _person_ Prons., giving them greater emphasis, but only in _accusative_, τὸν ἐμέ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 166a, “Phlb.” 20b}}; τὸν.. σὲ καὶ ἐμέ[{{Refs|[prev. work] 59b; τὸν αὑτόν [prev. author] “Phdr.” 258a; on ὁ αὐτός, see at {αὐτός} 111.}}]
**B.I.7.b** before the _interrogative_ _pronoun_ (both τίς and ποῖος), referring to something before, which needs to be more distinctly specified, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 251, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 696; also τὰ τ; because οἷα went before, [prev. work] 693. Of τίς only the _neuter_ is thus used (see.above): ποῖος is thus used not only in _neuter_ _plural_, τὰ ποῖ; 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 707; but also in the other genders, ὁ ποῖο; [prev. work] 1704}}; τῆς ποίας μερίδο; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.64}}; τοῖς ποίοις..; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 227b1.}}
**B.I.7.c** with τοιοῦτος, τοιόσδε, τηλικοῦτος, etc., the _Article_ either makes the _pronoun_ into a _substantive_, ὁ τοιοῦτος **that sort of person,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.2.21, etc.}}; or subjoins it to a _substantive_ which already has an _Article_, τὴν ἀπολογίαν τὴν τοιαύτην {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 41.13.}}
**B.I.8** before ἅπας, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.69, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.64, 7.153 (assuming variant), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1224 (Lyric poetry), 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.231, etc.}}; also τὸν ἕνα, τὸν ἕνα τοῦτον, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1287b8, 1288a19: on its usage with ἕκαστος}} see entry; and on οἱ ἄλλοι, οἱ πολλοί, etc., see at {ἄλλος} {{Refs|11.6, πολύς 11.3, etc.}}
**B.I.9** the _Article_ with the _comparative_ is rare, if ἤ follows, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 313, “OC” 796.}}
**B.II** elliptic expressions:
**B.II.1** before the _genitive_ of a _proper name_, to express descent, **son** or **daughter,** Θουκυδίδης ὁ Ὀλόρου (i.e. υἱός) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.104}}; Ἑλένη ἡ τοῦ Διός (i.e. θυγάτηρ) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 470}}: also to denote other relationships, e.g. **brother,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 32.24, 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 2.2.10}}; ἡ Σμικυθίωνος Μελιστίχη M.**the wife** of {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 46}}; Κλέαρχος καὶ οἱ ἐκείνου Cl. and his **men,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.15}}; ὁ τοῦ Ἀντιγένεος **the slave** of {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ χυμῶν” 20.}}
**B.II.2** generally, before a _genitive_ it indicates a wider relation, as τὸ τῶν νεῶν, τὸ τῶν Ἑρμῶν, **the matter of** the ships, **the affair of** the Hermae, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.23, 6.60}}; τὰ τοῦ Ἀρριβαίου πράσσειν to promote **the interests** of Arrhibaeus, {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.83, compare 6.89, etc.}}; τὸ τῆς τύχης,=ἡ τύχη, {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.18}}; τὰ τῆς τύχης **accidents, chance events,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 55}}; τὰ γὰρ φθιτῶν τοῖς ὁρῶσι κόσμος performance of **the rites due to** the dead befits the living, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 78 (Lyric poetry)}}; τὰ τῶν θεῶν **that which is destined** by the gods, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 498 (Lyric poetry): hence with _neuter_ of Possessive _pronoun_, τὸ ἐμόν, τὸ σόν}} **what regards** me or thee, my or thy business or interests, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 124, “El.” 251, etc.}}: and with _genitive_ of {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3 _person_, τὸ τῆσδε 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 48. But τό τινος}} is frequently also, **a man's word** or **saying,** as τὸ τοῦ Σόλωνος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.86}}; τὸ τοῦ Ὁμήρου **as** Homer says, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 183e}}; also τά τινος so-and-so's **house,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1432, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 54.7, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.76, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 5.52, NT.Luke.2.49.}}
**B.II.3** very frequently with cases governed by Preps.. αἱ ἐκ τῆς Ζακύνθου νῆες **the** ships from Zacynthus, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.13; οἱ ἀμφί τινα, οἱ περί τινα, such an one and his followers, see at {ἀμφί} with 1.3, περί with 1.2}}]; also τὰ ἐπὶ Θρᾴκης **the** Thrace-ward **district,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.59, +others}}; τὰ ἀπὸ τοῦ καταστρώματος **matters** on deck, {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.70}}; τὰ ἀπ᾽ Ἀλκιβιάδου **the proposals** of Alcibiades, {{Refs|[prev. author] 8.48}}; τὰ ἀπὸ τῆς τύχης **the incidents** of fortune, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.87, etc.}}
**B.II.4** on μὰ τόν, μὰ τήν, etc., see at {μά} IV.
**B.II.5** in elliptical phrases, ἐπορευόμην τὴν ἔξω τείχους (i.e. ὁδόν) [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 203a; ἡ ἐπὶ θανάτῳ (i.e. στολή, δέσις), see at {θάνατος}; κατὰ τὴν ἐμήν (i.e. γνώμην), see at {ἐμός} II.4}}]; ἡ αὔριον (i.e. ἡμέρα), see at {αὔριον}; ἡ Λυδιστί (i.e. ἁρμονία) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1342b32, etc.: frequently with Advs., which thus take an adjective sense, as ὁ, ἡ, τὸ νῦν}}; ὁ οἴκαδε πλοῦς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.52; οἱ τότε, οἱ ἔπειτα (i.e. ἄνθρωποι), [prev. work] 9, 10}}, etc.; but τό stands _absolutely_ with Advs. of time and place, when one cannot (as in the preceding instances) supply a _substantive_, as κἀκεῖσε καὶ τὸ δεῦρο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 266, compare [315] (Lyric poetry)}}; ὁ μὲν τὸ κεῖθεν, ὁ δὲ τὸ κεῖθεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Or.” 1412 (Lyric poetry): rarely _absolutely_ in _genitive_, ἰέναι τοῦ πρόσω to go forward, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.3.1; τοῦ προσωτάτω δραμεῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 731.}}
**C** as RELATIVE PRONOUN in many dialects; both in _nominative_ _singular_ _masculine_ ὅ, as κλῦθί μοι, ὃ χθιζὸς θεὸς ἤλυθες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.262, compare 1.300, +others; Ἔρως, ὃ κατ᾽ ὀμμάτων στάζεις πόθον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 526 (Lyric poetry); Ἄδωνις, ὃ κἠν Ἀχέροντι φιλεῖται 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.86}}; ὃ ἐξορύξη **he who** banishes him, {{Refs|“Schwyzer” 679.12,25 (from Cyprus)}}; and in the forms beginning with τ, especially in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.160, +others), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.7, +others}}: also in _Ionic dialect_ Poets, ἐν τῷ κάθημαι {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 87.3, compare 7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 7.3, 6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 86 (probably), 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 2.64, +others: frequently in Trag., τῆς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1258, “Tr.” 381, 728, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 883 (_anapaest meter_); τῷ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 14; τήν [prev. author] “OC” 747, “Tr.” 47, “El.” 1144}}; τό {{Refs|[prev. author] “OT” 1427}}; τῶν{{Refs|[prev. work] 1379, “Ant.” 1086.}}—Never in Comedy texts or _Attic dialect_ Prose:—_Epic dialect_ _genitive_ _singular_ τεῦ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.192 (assuming variant).}}
**D** CRASIS OF ARTICLE:
**D.a** _Attic dialect_ ὁ, ἡ, τό, with ᾰ make ᾱ, as ἁνήρ, ἁλήθεια, τἀγαθόν, τᾄτιο; so οἱ, αἱ, τά, as ἅνδρες, τἀγαθ; also τοῦ, τῷ, as τἀγαθοῦ, τἀγαθῷ: ὁ, τό, οἱ, before e gives ου, οὑξ, οὑπί, οὑμός, τοὔργον, οὑπιχώριοι, etc.; also τοῦ, as τοὐμοῦ, τοὐπιόντο; but ἅτερος, θάτερον ([musical notation]), _Ionic dialect_ οὕτερος, τοὔτερον (see. ἕτερος), _Attic dialect_ _feminine_ ἡτέρα, _dative_ θητέρᾳ (see. ἕτερος); τῷ loses the iota, τὠμῷ, τὠπιόντι: ὁ, τό, before ο gives ου, as Οὁδυσσεύς, Οὑλύμπιος, τοὔνομα: ὁ, τό, etc., before αυ gives ᾱυ, αὑτός, ταὐτό, ταὐτῷ (frequently written ἁτός, etc. in Inscrr. and Papyrus); so τὰ αὐτά=ταὐτά, αἱ αὐταί=αὑταί: ἡ before εὐ gives ηὑ, as ηὑλάβεια: τῇ before ἡ gives θη, as θἠμέρᾳ: τὸ before ὑ gives θου, as θοὔδωρ for τὸ ὕδωρ.
**D.b** other dialects: in their treatment of crasis these follow the local laws of contraction, hence, e.g., _Doric dialect_ ὡξ from ὁ ἐξ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.65, ὥλαφος from ὁ ἔλαφος [prev. work] 135}}; _Ionic dialect_ ᾡσυμνήτης from ὁ αἰς-{{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 57.45 (Milet., 5th c.BC)}}; ὡυτή from ἡ αὐτή {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 60, etc.}}

3591:
** ὄγκος**, ὁ,
**B** **bulk, size, mass** of a body, μελέων ἀριδείκετον ὄ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 20.1; ἀέρος ὄ. [prev. author] 100.13; σφαίρης ἐναλίγκιον ὄγκῳ 4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 8.43: frequently in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus, μήτε ὄγκῳ μήτε ἀριθμῷ “Tht.” 155a}}; τὸν.. ὄ. τοῦ ἀριθμοῦ their total number, {{Refs|“Lg.” 737c; τὸν τῶν σαρκῶν ὄ. [prev. work] 959c}}; σμικρᾶς πόλεως ὄ. a city of small **size, Plt.**{{Refs|259b}}; ἔχθρας ὄ. μέγαν {{Refs|“Lg.” 843b}}; θαυμαστὸν ὄ. ἀράμενοι τοῦ μύθου taking on my shoulders a monstrous **great** story, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Plt.” 277b, etc.: frequently also in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus}}, **the space filled by a body,** opposed to τὸ κενόν, {{Refs|“Ph.” 203b28, +others; ἴσος τὸν ὄ.}}**in bulk, GC** {{Refs|326b20; ὄγκῳ μικρόν “EN” 1178a1, etc.}}
**B.b** flatulent **distentions,** {{Refs|Diocl.fragment 43 (_plural_).}}
**B.2** **bulk, mass, body,** ὄ. φρυγάνων **a heap** of faggots, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.62}}; ὄ. μαλθακός **mass** or **roll** of something soft, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 26}}; σμικρὸς ὄ. ἐν σμικρῷ κύτει, of a dead man's ashes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1142}}; γαστρὸς ὄ., of a child in the womb, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 15; ὄ. πλήρης φλεβίων 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 515b1: _plural_, ὄγκοι}}**bodies, material substances,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Metaphorical” 1085a12, 1089b14}}; also ὁ ὄ. τῆς φωνῆς the **volume** of the note, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Aud.” 804a15.}}
**B.3** a bushy **top-knot,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 4.133.}}
**B.4** the human **body,** τῆς χολῆς ἀναχεομένης εἰς τὸν ὄ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus “ἀνατομή” 30, compare 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.26, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.653f, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 1.272.}}
**B.II** _metaphorically_, **bulk, weight, trouble,** βραχεῖ σὺν ὄ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1341.}}
**B.II.2** **weight, dignity, pride,** and in bad sense, **self-importance, pretension,** ὄ. ὀνόματος μητρῷος **pride** in the name of mother, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tr.” 817}}; ὄγκον αἴρειν exalt one's **dignity,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Aj.” 129; βραχὺν.. μῦθον οὐκ ὄγκου πλέων}}**of pretension,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “OC” 1162; μείζον᾽ ὄ. δορὸς ἤ φρενῶν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1158; ἔχει τιν᾽ ὄ. Ἄργος Ἑλλήνων πάρα [prev. author] “Ph.” 717; ἐς ὄ. βλέπειν τύχης [prev. author] “fragment” 81; τοῖς ζῶσι δ᾽ ὄγκος [prev. author] “Rh.” 760; ὁ τῶν ὑπεροπτικῶν ὄ. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.30; τῷ.. γένους ὄγκῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.121b; πραγμάτων ὄ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 548; τῆς ἀρχῆς τὸ μέγεθος καὶ ὁ ὄ. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Fabius Maximus” 4; ὄγκον περιθεῖναί τινι [prev. author] “Per.” 4, etc.}}
**B.II.3** of style, **loftiness, majesty,** ὄ. τῆς λέξεως {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1407b26; ὁ τοῦ ποιήματος ὄ. [prev. author] “Po.” 1459b28, compare 4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 36, +others}}: in bad sense, **bombast,** ὁ Αἰσχύλου ὄ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.79b.}}
**B.III** in Philos., **particle, mass, body,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.16U., “Nat.” 12G., 3rd c.BC: Asclepiades Epigrammaticus Bith. cited in 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 9.363}}; so in the physiology of the Methodics, ὄγκοι καὶ πόροι, = **molecules** and pores, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 1.499.}}

3592:
** ὅδε**, ἥδε, τόδε,
_demonstrative_ _pronoun_, **this,** formed by adding the _enclitic_ -δε to the old _demonstrative_ _pronoun_ ὁ, ἡ, τό, and declined like it through all cases: _Epic dialect_ _dative_ _plural_ τοῖσδεσσι, τοῖσδεσσιν, as well as τοῖσδε, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.462, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.47, +others; and τοῖσδεσι 10.268, 21.93; τοῖσδεσιν 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 175; τοισίδε 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32, +others}}: _Aeolic dialect_ _genitive_ _plural_ τῶνδεων [{{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 126: argument _genitive_ _plural_ τωνδεωνήν (={τῶνδεων ἤν}) “Mnemos.” 57.208(6th c.BC): _nominative_ _plural_ _neuter_ ταδήν [prev. passage], “IG” 4.506.1}}]; ταδή {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 744:}} —ὅδε, like{οὗτος}, is opposed to ἐκεῖνος, to designate what is **nearer** as opposed to to what is **more remote**; but ὅδε refers more distinctly to **what is present,** to **what can be seen** or **pointed out,** though this distinction is sometimes not observed, e.g. ξύμπας Ἀχαιῶν λαός, ἐν δὲ τοῖσδ᾽ ἐγώ [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1243 (variant{τοῖς}),compare “Ant.” 449, and on the other hand, ἦ τόνδε φράζεις}}];— τοῦτον, ὅνπερ εἰσορᾷς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “OT” 1120: the forms ὁδί, ἡδί, etc. [ῑ], are frequently in Comedy texts and Oratt., but are not used in Trag.: the ῑ may be separated from the ὅδε by the adversative δέ, as τὸν μὲν.., τηνδεδί 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 18, compare “Ec.” 989.}}
**I** of Place, to point out **what is present** or **before one,** Ἕκτορος ἥδε γυνή **this is,** or **here is,** the wife of Hector, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.460: very frequently in Trag., ἀκτὴ μὲν ἥδε Λήμνου 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ph.” I, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 4, “Ion” 5, “Hel.” I, “HF” 4, “Ba.” 1}}; in Comedy texts, ἐγὼ σιωπῶ τῷδ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1134, etc.}}; and in Prose, ὧν Θεόδωρος εἷς ὅδε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 164e}}; of what belongs to **this** world, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phdr.” 250a, “Smp.” 211c.}}
**I.2** with Verbs of action, = **here,** ἀνδρί, ὅστις ὅδε κρατέει who holds sway **here,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.175}}; ἔγχος μὲν τόδε κεῖται ἐπὶ χθονός **here** it lies, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|20.345, compare 21.533, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.185, etc.}}; ἥδ᾽ ἡ κορώνη.. λέγει the crow **here..,** variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 23: frequently in Trag., especially to indicate the entrance of a person on the stage, καὶ μὴν Ἐτεοκλῆς.. ὅδε χωρεῖ}} **here** comes.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 443, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 297, 531, 632, “OC” 32, 549}}; _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 80.}}
**I.3** with a _person_ _pronoun_, ὅδ᾽ ἐγὼ.. ἤλυθον **here** am I come, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.205}}; ἡμεῖς οἵδε περιφραζώμεθα let us **here..,** {{Refs|1.76}}; δῶρα δ᾽ ἐγὼν ὅδε.. παρασχέμεν **here** am I [ready] to provide.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.140}}: with a _proper name_, ὅδ᾽ εἰμ᾽ Ὀρέστης {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 380: with αὐτός, ὅδ᾽ αὐτὸς ἐγώ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.207, 24.321.}}
**I.4** also with τίς and other _interrogative_ words, τίς δ᾽ ὅδε Ναυσικάᾳ ἕπετα; who is **this** following her? {{Refs|6.276, compare 1.225}}; τί κακὸν τόδε πάσχετ; what is **this** evil ye are suffering? {{Refs|20.351}}; πρὸς ποῖον ἂν τόνδ᾽.. ἔπλε; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 572, compare 1204.}}
**I.5** in Trag. dialogue, ὅδε and ὅδ᾽ ἀνήρ, ={ἐγώ}, {{Refs|[prev. author] “OT” 534, 815, etc.}}; γυναικὸς τῆσδε, for ἐμοῦ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1438; τῆσδέ γε ζώσης ἔτι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 305}}; so ξὺν τῇδε χερί with **this** hand **of mine,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ant.” 43, compare “OT” 811.}}
**I.6** in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus, τοδί}} designates a **particular thing,** 'such and such', τοδὶ διὰ τοδὶ αἱρεῖται {{Refs|“EN” 1151a35; τόδε μετὰ τόδε “GA” 734a28, compare b9; Καλλίᾳ κάμνοντι τηνδὶ τὴν νόσον τοδὶ συνήνεγκε “Metaphorical” 981a8; τόδε τὸ ἐν τῷ ἡμικυκλίῳ “APo.” 71a20}}; ἥδε ἡ ἰατρική, opposed to αὐτὴ ἡ ἰ., {{Refs|“Metaphorical” 997b30}}; τόδε τι a **this,** i.e. a fully specified particular, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Cat.” 3b10, +others, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.113,171; τόδε τι καὶ οὐσία 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1060b1}}; πορευσόμεθα εἰς τήνδε τὴν πόλιν {{NT|NT.Jam.4.13.}}
**II** of Time, to indicate **the immediate present,** ἥδ᾽ ἡμέρα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 438, etc.: more strongly, κατ᾽ ἦμαρ.. τὸ νῦν τόδε [prev. author] “Aj.” 753; τοῦδ᾽ αὐτοῦ λυκάβαντος 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.161}}; but νυκτὸς τῆσδε in the night **just** past, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 21; νυκτὶ τῇδε [prev. author] “El.” 644}}; so τῆσδε τῆς ὁδοῦ on **this present** journey, {{Refs|[prev. author] “OT” 1478, compare “Ant.” 878 (conjecture)}}; also ἀπόλλυμαι τάλας ἔτος τόδ᾽ ἤδη δέκατον now for **these** ten years, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 312}}; τῶνδε τῶν ἀσκητῶν athletes **of the present day,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 403e.}}
**II.2** ἐς τόδε elliptic with _genitive_, ἐς τόδ᾽ ἡμέρας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 425; ἐς τόδε ἡλικίης 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.38}}; πῶς ἐς τόδ᾽ ἂν τόλμης ἔβ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 125.}}
**III** in sentences beginning **this is..,** the Engl. **this** is frequently represented by _nominative_ _plural_ _neuter_ τάδ; ἐπεὶ οὐκ ἔρανος τάδε γ᾽ ἐστίν **this** is not an ἔρανος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.226}}; ἆρ᾽ οὐχ ὕβρις τάδ; is not **this** insolence? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 883}}; of persons, Ἀπόλλων τάδ᾽ ἦν **this** was {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1329 (Lyric poetry); οὐ γὰρ ἔσθ᾽ Ἕκτωρ τάδε 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 168; οὐκέτι Τροία τάδε [prev. author] “Tr.” 100 (_anapaest meter_); οὐ τάδε Βρόμιος [prev. author] “Cyc.” 63 (Lyric poetry); οὐκ Ἴωνες τάδε εἰσίν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.77}}; τάδ᾽ οὐχὶ Πελοπόννησος, ἀλλ᾽ Ἰωνία Inscription cited in {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 9.1.6.}}
**III.2** to indicate something **immediately to come,** τόδε μοι κρήηνον ἐέλδωρ (which then follows) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.41, 504, compare 455, +others; Ἀθηναίων οἵδε ἀπέθανον “IG” 12.943.2: hence, in historical writers, opposed to what goes before (compare οὗτος with 1.2), ταῦτα μὲν Λακεδαιμόνιοι λέγουσι.., τάδε δὲ ἐγὼ γράφω 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.53; ταῦτα μὲν δὴ σὺ λέγεις· παρ᾽ ἡμῶν δὲ ἀπάγγελλε τάδε 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.1.20, etc.}}; see at {οὗτος} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 1.2}}; opposed to ἐκεῖνος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 784: rarely applied to different persons in the same sentence, νῦν ὅδε}} [La_us] πρὸς τῆς τύχης ὄλωλεν, οὐδὲ τοῦδ᾽ ὕπο [by Oedipus] {{Refs|[prev. author] “OT” 948.}}
**III.3** as 'antecedent' to a defining Relat., ὃν πόλις στήσειε, τοῦδε χρὴ κλύειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ant.” 666, compare “Tr.” 23, “Ph.” 87, etc.: in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, in such cases, the δέ}} is separate, as ὃς δέ κε μηρίνθοιο τύχῃ.., ὁ δ᾽ οἴσεται ἡμιπέλεκκα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.858, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.148, 149, +others (but ὅδε}} sometimes has its deictic force and the _relative_ clause merely explains, as νήσου τῆσδ᾽ ἐφ᾽ ἧς ναίει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 613, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.346, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.3.47, etc.).}}
**IV** Adverbial usage of some cases:
**IV.1** τῇδε,
**IV.1.a** of Place, **here, on the spot,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.345, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.173, etc.; so τῶν τε ὑπὸ γῆς θεῶν καὶ τῶν τ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 958d.}}
**IV.1.b** of Manner, **thus,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 45; ὅρα δὲ καὶ τ., ὅτι.. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 79e, compare “R.” 433e, etc.}}
**IV.2** _accusative_ _neuter_ τόδε with ἱκάνω, etc., **hither, to this spot,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.298, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.409, +others; also δεῦρο τόδε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.309, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.444, 524.}}
**IV.2.b** **therefore, on this account,** τόδε χώεο {{Refs|23.213}}: so also _accusative_ _plural_ _neuter_, τάδε γηθήσειε **on this account,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.77.}}
**IV.3** _dative_ _plural_ _neuter_, τοισίδε **in** or **with these words,** τοισίδε ἀμείβεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.120}}; τοισίδε προέχει **in these respects,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 32.}}

3593:
** ὁδ-εύω**,
**go, travel,** ἐπὶ νῆας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.569; δι᾽ Ἀδραμυττίου 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.8.8; διὰ νυκτός “POxy.” 2153.21 (3rd c.AD)}}; κοινῶς ὁ. τινί {{Refs 6th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 15.2; ἐξ ὑγιείας εἰς νόσον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 41, compare 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ εὐσχημοσύνης” 18: with _accusative_ _cognate_, τὴν ἐπὶ Σμύρνης 6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 15.1; βιότου τρίβον ὁδεύειν “Anacreontea” 38.2.}}
**2** with _accusative_ loci, **travel over,** χθόνα πεζὸς ὁ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.1441; ὁ. τὴν ἔρημον 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Eumenes” 15; μέγαν οὐρανόν “IG” 14.2012 A 36; εἴκοσι.. λυκάβαντας ὁδεύσας “epigram Gr.” 226.3 (from Teos):}} — _passive_, ὁδευομένη (with or without ὁδός) **thoroughfare, highway,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|POxy. 1537.18(3rd c.AD), “Stud.Pal.” 20.117.6 (V 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator).}}
**3** _passive_, of Ravenna, γεφύραις καὶ πορθμείοις ὁδευομένη **provided with thoroughfares** by means of.., {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 5.1.7.}}

3598:
** ὁδός**, ἡ
(οὐδός once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.196)}}:
**I** of Place, **way, road,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.168, 16.374, “IG” 12.878, +others; ἱππηλασίη ὁ. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.340; λαοφόρος 15.682; ὁ. ἁμαξιτός 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 6.54}}; ὁ. ἱερά, to Eleusis, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 1.36.3, compare “IG” 12.881; βασιλικὴ ὁ. “PPetr.” 3p.65 (3rd c.BC), “PSI” 8.917.8 (1st c.AD)}}; ποταμοῦ ὁ. **course, channel** of a river, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.16; ὁ. ἀκοντίου 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.4.5}}: with expression of the direction, ὁδὸς ἐς.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.128; ἡ ὁ. ἡ εἰς ἄστυ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 173b; ἐπί.. [prev. author] “Phdr.” 272c}}; τὴν εὐθὺς Ἄργους.. ὁ. leading straight to Argos, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1197}}; τῆς ἀληθείας ὁ. the way **to truth,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “fragment” 289; compare νόστος 1.1.}}
**I.2** with Preps., πρὸ ὁδοῦ **further on the way, forwards,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.382 (compare φροῦδος)}}; later, ={προὔργου}, **profitable, useful,** πρὸ ὁ. εἶναι πρός τι to be **helpful** towards.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 292b9, compare “Metaphorical” 1044a24; πρὸ ὁδοῦ γέγονεν [prev. author] “Pol.” 1338a35, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Prooemia” 34; κατ᾽ ὁδόν}}**by the way,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.41, 111}}; κατὰ τὴν ὁ. along the **road,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 174d, compare below 111.3}}; ἐκ τῆς ὁ. on his **road,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.157 (but ἄνθρωπος ἐξ ὁ.}} 'the man in the street', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 25 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator)}}; ἐν ὁδῷ on **a road,** [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.114; ἐν τῇ ὁ. μέσῃ [prev. author] 3.76 (but ἐν ὁ. καθελών Legal cited in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.53, explained by ἐν λόχῳ καὶ ἐνέδρᾳ by 1st-2nd c.AD: Harpocratio Grammaticus see at {ὁδός})}}]; ὁδοῦ πάρεργον by **the way,** cursorily, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Atticum” 5.21.13, 7.1.5, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 11.607.}}
**I.3** ὁδός is frequently omitted, ἐπορευόμην τὴν ἔξω τείχους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 203a}}; ἡ ἐπὶ θανάτου, see at {θάνατος}; compare τηνάλλως.
**II** as an Action, **travelling, journeying,** whether by land or water, **journey, voyage,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.285, 8.150, etc.; τρίποδας ὁ. στείχει 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 80 (_anapaest meter_); τὰν νεάταν ὁ. στείχουσαν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 807 (Lyric poetry); ὁ. ἄνω κάτω μία καὶ ὡυτή 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 60}}; also, **expedition, foray,** ὁδὸν ἐλθέμεναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.151, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 714}}; τριήκοντα ἡμερέων.. ὁ. a thirty days' **journey,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.104, compare 206; also ὅσον ἐπὶ τρεῖς ἡμέρας ὁδόν [prev. author] 3.5 (codices, ὁδοῦ editions)}}; ἄστρων ὁδοί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 728 (Lyric poetry): as _accusative_ _cognate_ with Verb of motion, τὴν ὁ. ἣν Ἑλένην περ ἀνήγαγεν}} **by** or **in which..,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.292}}; οὐρανοῦ τέμνων ὁ... Ἥλιε, _metaphorically_ from a ship, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1 (but in Prose ὁ. τέμνειν}} is to make a **road,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.100, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 810e)}}; similarly where ὁ. is **road,** μέσην ἔρχευ τὴν ὁ. {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 220; ὁ. χωρεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.24}}; ἰόντες τὴν ἱρὴν ὁ., from Delphi, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.34.}}
**III** _metaphorically_, **way** or **manner,** πολλαὶ δ᾽ ὁ... εὐπραγίας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.13; γλώσσης ἀγαθῆς ὁδός 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 989 (_anapaest meter_)}}; θεσπεσία ὁ. **the way** or **course** of divination, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ag.” 1154 (Lyric poetry); μαντικῆς ὁ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 311; οἰωνῶν ὁδοῖς [prev. author] “OC” 1314; σῶν ὁ. βουλευμάτων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 744; γνώμης [prev. author] “Hipp.” 290}}; λογίων ὁ. their **way, intent,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1015; εὐτελείας ὁ. 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 6.198d.}}
**III.2** **a way** of doing, speaking, etc., τῆσδ᾽ ἀφ᾽ ὁδοῦ διζήσιος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 1.33, compare 8.18}}; τριφασίας ἄλλας ὁ. λόγων **ways** of telling the story, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.95, compare 2.20, 22}}; but τριφασίας ὁ. τρέπεται turns into three **forms,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 6.119; ἄδικον ὁ. ἰέναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.64}}; ὁ. ἥντιν᾽ ἰών by what **course of action,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 506, compare “Nu.” 75; ἢν ἔχομεν ὁ. λόγων [prev. author] “Pax” 733; μία δὴ λείπεται.. ὁ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 184b.}}
**III.3** **method, system,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Sph.” 218d, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Priora” 53a2, +others; ὁδῷ}}**methodically, systematically,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 533b, “Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 2.39, etc.; so καθ᾽ ὁδόν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 435a; τὴν διὰ τοῦ στοιχείου ὁ. ἔχων ἔγραφεν [prev. author] “Tht.” 208b (compare διέξοδον 208a).}}
**III.4** of **the Christian Faith and its followers,** {{NT|NT.Acts.9.2, 22.4, 24.14}}. (Root **sed-** 'go', in Sanskrit _sad-, ā-sad-_ 'come to', 'reach', OSlavonic _choditi_ 'go'.)

3599:
** ὀδούς**, όντος, ὁ,
_nominative_ ὀδούς {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1161b23, LXX.1Ki.14.4, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Musc.Enc.” 3, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 5.12.2, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 2.13, 3rd c.AD(?): Achilles Tatius Astronomus 7.4}}; _Ionic dialect_ **ὀδών** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.107 (bis), 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 4.19, 52, cf. Hdn.Gr. 2.928}}:—**tooth,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.74, +others}}; ἕρκος ὀδόντων, see at {ἕρκος}; πρίειν ὀδόντας, see at {πρίω}; ὀ. ὀξεῖς **incisors,** opposed to πλατεῖς, **molars,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 661b8, +others}}
**2** _metaphorically_, γλυκὺς ὀ. ὁ τοῦ πόθου {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 3}}; ὁ τῆς λύπης ὀ. the **tooth** of grief, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Achilles Tatius Astronomus [prev. cited]}}
**II** **anything pointed** or **sharp, tooth, prong, spike,** etc., {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 85}}: _plural_, **teeth** of a saw, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 200b6}}; of a comb, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Antyllus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 10.16.2}}; of a **cog**-wheel, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “(Spiritalia) Pneumatica” 2.36, Theo. Sm. p.180}} H.; **ploughshare,** {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.13.21}}; ὀ. πέτρας **peak, pike,**{{LXX|[prev. work] 14.4, LXX.Psa.77.30.}}
**III** **second vertebra** of the neck or its apophysis (the odontoid process), so called from its shape, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 2.2.24, compare 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.131, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 12.7}} (but the **first vertebra** _accusative_ to {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus “περὶ ὀνομασίας” 154)}}. (Old _present_ _participle_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|of 1.- 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **ed-** (alternating with **od-** (cf. Armenian _utem_ 'I eat') and **d-**), the root of ἔδω, ἔδ-μεναι, Latin _edo,_ etc.: cf. Sanskrit _accusative_ **dántam** 'tooth', Latin _dens,_ Gothic _tunpus,_ etc.: _Aeolic dialect_ ἔδοντες {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in Platonis Cratylum commentaria” p.39 P., etc.)}}
